{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014ZXT6c1bzyGnXnjLnyb884","type":"tool_result","content":"470\t\n471\t### 4.1 What a layout is\n472\t\n473\t**DECIDED** (frozen §4.3; UCF §7.4.2). A foreign engine publishes a\n474\tstatic map per segment and engine (`A`, resume pcs, `def`/`use`/`L`\n475\tfrom `analysis/analyze`, `lexically-required`, and a layout from\n476\tphysical location to virtual register) and reconstructs the canonical\n477\tframe from its state at a safepoint; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` on\n478\tadmitted canonical frames. Two kernels are intended to reconstruct\n479\tcanonical frames: the de Bruijn register kernel over `R(A)` and the de\n480\tBruijn stack kernel over `H(A)` (phase 5). The AST walker is not (frozen\n481\t§10 phase 6 names the three kernels; the walker has no pc).\n482\t\n483\t**PROPOSED** namespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.layout`, file\n484\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`, requiring\n485\t`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `.derive` (phase 5),\n486\t`yin.vm.debruijn` (`resolve-name`), `yin.vm.engine` (`bind-params`), and\n487\tthe two kernels' public state. Public: `register-layout`,\n488\t`stack-layout` (static, per segment), `lift-act`, `lift-tail`,\n489\t`lower-act`, `lower-tail` (per kernel, dispatching on the kernel's\n490\t`link-format`).\n491\t\n492\t### 4.2 The register kernel's layout\n493\t\n494\t**DECIDED** by phase-5 §2.4 (R's bodies and pcs are A's) and §2.3\n495\t(`R(v) = locals + slot(v)`); the kernel's payload shape from\n496\t`debruijn/register.cljc:690-704` (call frame: `:site-pc :return-pc\n497\t:frames :regs :live :dest :store-of`) and `write-back` (`:455-485`:\n498\t`:write-result` rebuilds a dense register vector from `regs` and writes\n499\t`dest`; `:return-result` runs the return transition).\n500\t\n501\t**Static half.** For segment `A` with `R = (:image (derive/register-image\n502\tv))`: for every pc `p`, `v[p]` names virtual registers in its `rd`,\n503\t`:reg` and `:regs` slots and `R[p]` names physical registers in the same\n504\tslots. `phys(body, v)` is read off any pc that names `v`; it is total on\n505\tthe virtuals a body names. Its inverse is **restricted**: `virt(p, s)`\n506\tis defined only for `s` in the physical image of\n507\t`live-out*(p) = ⋃ L(q) for q ∈ (analysis/successors v p)` (the actual\n508\tsuccessors, phase-5 §2.5; empty for a tail call) at a **reachable**\n509\tboundary pc `p` (`analysis/reachable`); there it is \"the `v ∈ live-out*(p)`\n510\twith `phys(v) = s`\", single-valued because the allocator's intervals\n511\tcover every live point and distinct live virtuals hold distinct slots\n512\t(a property the harness checks, §5.6 item 3, never assumed). Physical\n513\toperands of **unreachable** layout-syntax instructions (the structural\n514\tslot shared by several definition-less ids, phase-5 §2.2) are not\n515\tinverted; they are validated separately by the kernel's own\n516\t`register-image-defect` and by phase-5's gates, and never reach a\n517\tcanonical frame (no reachable instruction reads them).\n518\t\n519\t**Lift, `act`.** From the kernel's wait entry with `:resume-mode\n520\t:write-result` (payload `:image :site-pc :pc :frames :regs :live\n521\t:continuation :dest :store-of`, v2 amendment §5.3):\n522\t\n523\t1. `A` = the A the image row of the entry's `:image` was derived from\n524\t   (the kernel's code space is keyed by A after phase 5; R is its\n525\t   checksum).\n526\t2. relative `pc` = the entry's `pc` minus the image row's offset\n527\t   (`image-row`/`absolute-pc`, `debruijn/register.cljc:326-337`); R's pcs\n528\t   are A's, so this is the canonical pc.\n529\t3. `rd = (code/rd (nth v (dec pc)))` **from A**, never the physical\n530\t   `:dest` (which is `phys(rd)`).\n531\t4. `W = {virt(pc−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ regs}`; the kernel's `live` is\n532\t   `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(pc−1) rd)))` (the subtraction is on\n533\t   **virtual** ids before mapping: on the worked example `live-out*(3) =\n534\t   L(4) = {1 2}`, `(disj … 2) = {1}`, `phys → [0]`; subtracting the\n535\t   physical `3` from `{1 2}` would wrongly keep `2`), so `(keys W) =\n536\t   saved(pc, rd)`, which rule A5/A6 then checks.\n537\t5. `E` = §4.4 from the entry's `frames` and `store-of`, at `body(pc)`.\n538\t6. `K`: for each continuation frame `f`, **independently**: its\n539\t   `return-pc` is absolute in the kernel's concatenated code space, so\n540\t   first its image row `rowₖ = (image-row images return-pc)` and relative\n541\t   `pcₖ = return-pc − offsetₖ`, then `Aₖ` of that row (frames may cross\n542\t   images: a module closure called from another image returns into its\n543\t   caller's image), `rdₖ = (code/rd (nth vₖ (dec pcₖ)))` from `Aₖ`, never\n544\t   `f.dest`; `Wₖ = {virt_{Aₖ}(pcₖ−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ f.regs}`; `Eₖ` = §4.4\n545\t   from `f.frames` and `f.store-of` at `body(pcₖ)` in `Aₖ`.\n546\t7. pending: the engine fields of the entry, as `lift-pending!` maps them\n547\t   (`handoff.cljc:674-749`).\n548\t\n549\t**Lift, `tail`.** From an entry with `:resume-mode :return-result`\n550\t(`dest nil`, `live []`): `A` and the relative `site = site-pc` as in 1-2\n551\t(the entry's `site-pc` is the tail call; its `pc` is `site+1`); `K` as\n552\tin 6; pending as in 7; **no** `W`, `E` or `pc` are read: the entry's\n553\t`regs` (empty by `live []`) and `frames` are discarded (frozen §2.4: a\n554\ttail state carries no window and no `E`).\n555\t\n556\t**Lower, `act`.** Given a canonical `act` and the receiver's attached\n557\timage for `A` (derived by `derive/register-image` from the carried\n558\tvector, offset `off`): `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + pc − 1`;\n559\t`:pc = off + pc`; `:regs = (sort-by first [[phys(v) V] for [v V] in W])`;\n560\t`:live = (sort (map phys (keys W)))` (the kernel's `live-exact` rule\n561\trecomputes `body-liveness` and must agree); `:dest = phys(rd)`;\n562\t`:resume-mode :write-result`; `:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of`\n563\tfrom `E`; `:continuation` = for each `K[i]`, with its own `Aₖ`'s image\n564\trow `offₖ`: `{:site-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ −1) :return-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ) :regs\n565\t… :live … :dest (phys_{Aₖ} rdₖ) :frames (§4.4 from Eₖ) :store-of …}`;\n566\t`:format reffects/format-tag`, `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's current\n567\taggregate (never carried). Engine fields from the pending\n568\t(`lower-frame!`, `handoff.cljc:3182-3246`), `:deliver` carried.\n569\t\n570\t**Lower, `tail`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + site`;\n571\t`:pc = off + site + 1`; `:regs []`; `:live []`; `:dest nil`;\n572\t`:resume-mode :return-result`; `:frames []` and `:store-of nil`\n573\t(unused: `write-back`'s `:return-result` branch reads `:frames` and\n574\t`:store-of` only to assign them before the return transition replaces\n575\tthem from the popped frame, `register.cljc:461-468`; the harness checks\n576\tthat the values chosen here are never observable, §5.6 item 8);\n577\t`:continuation` as for `act`. The register kernel already supports this\n578\tdelivery (`:return-result`, `register.cljc:458-468`).\n579\t\n580\t### 4.3 The stack kernel's layout\n581\t\n582\t**DECIDED** by phase-5 §3.2 (the emitter's walk) and the kernel's call\n583\tand return arms (`debruijn/stack.cljc:716-776`): a non-tail `:call`\n584\tpops `f` and the `argc` operands, leaving `stack'`, and pushes\n585\t`{:return-pc (inc pc) :frames frames :stack-base (count stack')}`\n586\t(`:728-733`); `:return` truncates to the frame's `stack-base` and pushes\n587\tthe value (`:766-772`); a host-function effect parks with the\n588\tcontinuation after the call site and `stack-restore` resumes by conjing\n589\tthe value at the entry's `pc` (`:748-752`, `:555-568`).\n590\t\n591\t**Static half.**\n592\t\n593\t1. **pc correspondence.** The stack emitter visits parse nodes in\n594\t   `project`'s order and emits one instruction per A instruction plus a\n595\t   `:push` after each child of a call, each `:stream-put` target and\n596\t   each `:ffi-call` operand (phase-5 §3.2). So H **with every `:push`\n597\t   removed** corresponds one-to-one, in order, with A: the k-th non-push\n598\t   H instruction ↔ A pc `k`; `h->a`, `a->h` are the two directions. The\n599\t   harness checks mnemonic families agree at every k (§5.6 item 4).\n600\t2. **operand layout.** For an A pc `p` and the parse node at `p`,\n601\t   `stack-layout(p)` is the vector, bottom to top, of the virtual\n602\t   registers whose values are pushed and not yet consumed while control\n603\t   is inside `p`'s expression: for each ancestor `n` and the child `c` on\n604\t   the path to `p`, the children of `n` before `c` that `n` pushes (a\n605\t   `:call`: the operator and the operands before `c`; a `:stream-put`:\n606\t   the target when `c` is the value; an `:ffi-call`: the operands before\n607\t   `c`; `:if`, `:define`, `:stream-cursor/next/close`, `:resume`: none).\n608\t   **Checked** (§5.6 item 5): for a **non-tail** parking instruction,\n609\t   `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p) (code/rd v[p]))`.\n610\t   For a **tail** `:call` the canonical state has no window and\n611\t   `stack-layout(p)` is not consulted (it may be non-empty for a tail\n612\t   call in operand position, whose pushed siblings are dead layout\n613\t   syntax).\n614\t\n615\t**K slices (corrected).** Let the kernel's continuation be\n616\t`[f₀ … f_{m−1}]` outermost first with bases `b₀ … b_{m−1}` and the\n617\toperand stack `St` of length `n`. Because `bᵢ = (count stack')` is the\n618\tcaller's residual height **after** popping the callee's operands, the\n619\tslice of operands belonging to activation `i` is\n620\t\n621\t```\n622\tslice(f₀)      = St[0, b₀)\n623\tslice(fᵢ)      = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)        for 0 < i < m\n624\tslice(current) = St[b_{m−1}, n)         ; or St[0, n) when K is empty\n625\t```\n626\t\n627\t(the review's correction: `[previous-base, base_i)` with previous-base\n628\t0, and the current activation `[last-base, count)`).\n629\t\n630\t**Lift, `act`.** From a wait entry `{:image :pc :frames :stack\n631\t:continuation :store-of}` (v2 amendment §5.2) whose `pc−1` in H is **not**\n632\ta tail `:call`:\n633\t\n634\t1. `A` from the image row; relative H pc `pcₕ = pc − off`; canonical\n635\t   `pc = h->a(pcₕ − 1) + 1`; `rd = (code/rd v[pc−1])`.\n636\t2. `W = (zipmap (stack-layout (dec pc)) slice(current))`; the two have\n637\t   equal length (checked) and `(keys W) = saved(pc, rd)` (rule A5/A6).\n638\t3. `E` = §4.4 from `frames` and `store-of` at `body(pc)`.\n639\t4. `K`: for `fᵢ` **independently**: its `return-pc` is absolute; row and\n640\t   relative `pcₕᵢ` through the stack kernel's own offset table\n641\t   (`debruijn/stack.cljc:355`, `absolute-pc`'s inverse); `Aᵢ` of that row;\n642\t   `pcᵢ = h->a_{Aᵢ}(pcₕᵢ − 1) + 1`; `rdᵢ = (code/rd vᵢ[pcᵢ−1])`;\n643\t   `Wᵢ = (zipmap (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) slice(fᵢ))`; `Eᵢ` = §4.4\n644\t   from `fᵢ.frames` and `fᵢ.store-of`.\n645\t5. pending from the engine fields.\n646\t\n647\t**Lift, `tail`.** From a wait entry whose `pc−1` in H is a tail `:call`:\n648\t`A` and `site = h->a(pcₕ − 1)`; `K` as in 4; pending; the entry's\n649\t`frames`, `store-of` and `slice(current)` are **discarded** (dead\n650\tlayout syntax; frozen §2.4).\n651\t\n652\t**Lower, `act`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:pc = off + a->h(pc−1) + 1`;\n653\t`:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of` from `E`; `:stack` = the\n654\tconcatenation over `K` outermost first of each `slice(fᵢ)` =\n655\t`(mapv Wᵢ (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)))`, then `slice(current) =\n656\t(mapv W (stack-layout (dec pc)))`; `:continuation` = for each `fᵢ`\n657\t`{:return-pc (+ offᵢ (a->h_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) 1) :frames (§4.4 from Eᵢ)\n658\t:stack-base bᵢ :store-of …}` where **`bᵢ` is the cumulative length of\n659\tthe slices up to and including `slice(fᵢ)`**: `b₀ = |slice(f₀)|`, `bᵢ =\n660\tb_{i−1} + |slice(fᵢ)|`. This is the inverse of the lift's\n661\t`slice(fᵢ) = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)`: the kernel's base is the caller's\n662\tresidual height, which is exactly the end of the caller's own slice\n663\t(`stack.cljc:731-733`). On the multi-frame fixture of §8.3 item 6\n664\t(five return frames, slices `[0,0) [0,0) [0,0) [0,2) [2,4)`) this emits\n665\tbases `0, 0, 0, 2, 4`; the before-slice rule would emit `0, 0, 0, 0, 2`\n666\tand the kernel would truncate to the wrong height on return. `:format\n667\tstack/format-tag`; `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's. `stack-restore`\n668\tthen conjes the delivered value onto `:stack` at `:pc`, the `:push`-free\n669\tposition after the parking instruction, exactly where H expects the\n670\tresult.\n671\t\n672\t**Lower, `tail`, and the tail-completion seam.** Today the stack kernel\n673\tresumes a parked host effect at the entry's `pc` by pushing the value\n674\t(`stack-restore`, `:566`); for a **tail** call that resumes the body at\n675\t`a->h(site)+1`, which then runs the layout syntax after the tail call\n676\t(for a tail call in operand position: the remaining pushes and the\n677\tenclosing call) **before** reaching `:return`. That is not the canonical\n678\tthrough-return delivery (frozen §2.4, §4.1 \"effectful call, tail\":\n679\tdelivery **through return** against `K`). The layout therefore cannot\n680\tlower a canonical `tail` into today's stack kernel. **PROPOSED, gated\n681\t(§8.3 slice 6c-pre):** the stack kernel gains a tail-completion seam:\n682\tits park-entry builders mark an entry whose parking instruction is a\n683\ttail `:call` with `:resume-mode :return-result` (the register kernel's\n684\texisting vocabulary, `register.cljc:458-468`), and `stack-restore`\n685\tdelivers such an entry by the **return transition** (pop the top frame,\n686\ttruncate `:stack` to its base, push the value, restore its `frames`,\n687\t`store-of` and `return-pc`; halt with the value when `K` is empty) instead\n688\tof resuming at `pc`; the retained-FFI re-park and the response decoding\n689\t(`stack-restore`'s `:request-sent` and `:call-id` steps, `:548-557`) keep\n690\ttheir order before the final delivery, as in the register kernel.\n691\tLowering a canonical `tail` then produces\n692\t`{:image row-of-A :pc (+ off (a->h site) 1) :resume-mode :return-result\n693\t:stack (concat of the K slices, in order) :frames [] :store-of nil\n694\t:continuation …}` with each continuation frame's `:stack-base bᵢ` the\n695\t**same cumulative-including rule as the `act` lowering** (the last\n696\tframe's base equals the stack's length, since the current activation\n697\tcontributes no slice); the `:frames`/`:store-of` values are unobservable\n698\t(the return transition replaces them) and the harness checks that (§5.6\n699\titem 8). File:\n700\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc` (not a 4b or phase-5 file); the\n701\tregister kernel needs no change.\n702\t\n703\t### 4.4 Positional lexical frames, the free environment and `E`\n704\t\n705\t**DECIDED** by frozen §2.1 and §2.4 (`E_c[ps ↦ a]` by `bind-params`),\n706\tphase-5 §1.2 (the owner chain), and the kernels' resolution of free\n707\tnames through the task-wide `free-env` (`stack.cljc:683`:\n708\t`(engine/resolve-var free-env …)`). Both de Bruijn kernels hold the\n709\tlexical environment as positional frames `F = [[V …] …]` outermost first,\n710\tone per enclosing closure activation, plus the task-wide `free-env`.\n711\t\n712\t**Forward (F → E), lossless.** At a pc of body `b` with\n713\t`chain = [c₀ … c_{j}]` outermost first (phase-5's `chain` reversed):\n714\t\n715\t```\n716\tE = (merge free-env\n717\t           (bind-params params(c₀) F[0])\n718\t           …\n719\t           (bind-params params(c_j) F[j]))\n720\t    ∪ {engine/store-of-key M}  when the activation's closure carries store-of M\n721\t```\n722\t\n723\tThis reproduces the register evaluator's `E` on closure entry\n724\t(`semantic_register.cljc:141-143`: `(merge (:env c) (bind-params\n725\t(:params c) args))`, `(:env c)` being the creating activation's `E`,\n726\titself `free-env` merged with the outer chain), including rightmost-wins\n727\tduplicates, nil-fill and extra-argument dropping, which are\n728\t`engine/bind-params`'s.\n729\t\n730\t**Inverse (E → F), and why the free environment travels.** The reviewer's\n731\tcounterexample, with initial free `x = 99`:\n732\t\n733\t```clojure\n734\t(let [f (fn [] x)]\n735\t  ((fn [x] (f (stream-next c))) 1))\n736\t```\n737\t\n738\tAt the blocked read the current `E` binds `x` to 1 (the parameter). The\n739\tearlier revision recovered the receiver's free environment as \"`E` minus\n740\tthe chain's names\", which drops `x` entirely, and the lowered de Bruijn\n741\tclosure `f`, whose body is `[:load-free x]`, resolves `x` through the\n742\ttask-wide `free-env` and loses 99. **So the task's free environment is\n743\tcarried explicitly** (root slot `free-env`, §2.6; §6.3), every `E` is\n744\tchecked against it off the lexical names (rule E2), and the inverse is:\n745\t\n746\t```\n747\tfree-env  := the root's free-env row (never derived from any E)\n748\tF[i]      := (mapv #(get E %) params(cᵢ))      for i = 0 … j\n749\t```\n750\t\n751\t`F[i]` for an **outer** frame whose parameter is shadowed by an inner\n752\tframe receives the inner value, not the outer one; that value is\n753\tunobservable from `body(pc)` (resolution picks the innermost binder,\n754\t`resolve-name`, `debruijn.cljc:617-637`), and the canonical frame does\n755\tnot carry it (frozen §2.1: parameters live in `E`, and `E` holds one\n756\tvalue per name). The round-trip law is therefore stated on **canonical\n757\tframes** (`lift(lower(frame)) = frame`), not on kernel state, and the\n758\tharness's `E` check (§5.6 item 6) compares the layout's `E` with the\n759\tevaluator's; shadowed outer positional values are not a claim of this\n760\tdesign. The free bindings that *are* observable, by any closure's\n761\t`:load-free`, are preserved exactly by the root slot.\n762\t\n763\t**Closure values.** A de Bruijn closure (positional marker: segment,\n764\tentry, arity, frames `F`, store-of) reconstructs to the canonical\n765\tclosure (`{:type :closure :params :entry :segment :env}`, evaluator\n766\t§1.8): `params` from A's `:closure` tuple at the owner pc of the entry\n767\tbody, `env` by the forward rule over the closure's captured `F` and the\n768\ttask's `free-env`, `entry` the body pc. The inverse reads `F` back from\n769\t`env` by the chain. Checked both ways (§5.6 item 7).\n770\t\n771\t### 4.5 What a layout never does\n772\t\n773\t**DECIDED.** A layout reads a kernel's state, A and the carried task\n774\tcontext; it never runs code, never fills a binding from the receiver's\n775\tstore or free environment (the free environment is the **carried** one,\n776\t§6.3), never reads a native image's hash as an identity, and never\n777\tinvents a safepoint: a kernel pc whose predecessor is not a parking\n778\tinstruction is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (UCF §7.4.2).\n779\t\n780\t---\n781\t\n782\t## 5. The safepoint harness\n783\t\n784\t### 5.1 What it proves\n785\t\n786\t**DECIDED** (frozen §10 phase 6 gate; UCF §7.11). For every row of the\n787\texpanded matrix, both delivery modes, nested and reified captures,\n788\tmodule contexts and every refusal: lift at the safepoint, lower into a\n789\tfresh machine, continue, and result and effect trace equal the\n790\tuninterrupted reference run; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame`; the frame's\n791\treason is the observed wait's; on the three lanes: `:semantic-register`,\n792\t`:stack-through-layout`, `:register-through-layout`.\n793\t\n794\t### 5.2 The matrix\n795\t\n796\t**Expected results are stated for the bare expression.** Each row runs\n797\tunder the four segment wrappers of the old harness\n798\t(`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:92-106`), whose expected results follow\n799\tmechanically from the bare result `r`: `:bare` → `r`; `:operand` →\n800\t`[:operand r]` (`(cat [:operand] r)`); `:depth-3` → `[3 [2 [1 r]]]`\n801\t(`nest` wraps `(cat [d] ((fn [x] …) d))` three times, as the old test's\n802\tpinned `[2 [1 [:two 2 \"C\"]]]` shows for depth 2, `:574`); `:captured` →\n803\t`[:captured :arg r]` (`(cat (cat (cat [] k) y) r)` with `k = :captured`,\n804\t`y = :arg`). **Frames the wrappers add.** The harness marks `:tail?\n805\ttrue` only where a row says so (`:228`, `:256`; `ast->semantic-bytecode`\n806\tdefaults it to false, `vm.cljc:1089`; `mark-tail` is the expander's and\n807\tis not run here), so every `let1` and every `nest` application is a\n808\tnon-tail call that pushes a return frame. `with-cat` (`:69-76`,\n809\toutermost in every segment, `:96`) adds **one** frame below everything\n810\tthe row builds, with `stack-base 0` and an empty slice on the stack lane\n811\t(nothing is pushed before its application). On top of it `:bare` and\n812\t`:operand` add none of their own (the row is the operand), `:depth-3`\n813\tadds three (`nest`'s applications, each with two values, `cat` and\n814\t`[d]`, pushed under it), and `:captured` adds three (`let1 k`, `let1 f`,\n815\t`(f :arg)`). A row's own `let1`s add theirs. Row 20 alone runs outside\n816\tthe wrappers.\n817\t\n818\tPrograms use `v2_support`'s AST helpers (`v2_support.cljc:56-104`:\n819\t`lit v lam app def! then cursor-of next-of let1`) and the four segments\n820\tof the old harness (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:92-106`: `:bare`,\n821\t`:operand`, `:depth-3`, `:captured`); `cat` is the closure `(fn [p q]\n822\t(conj p q))` bound around every body (`:69-81`). \"Expected\" is the\n823\treference run's normalised result; where a row says \"executable\" it runs\n824\ton all three lanes; \"synthetic\" means the frame is built by hand and\n825\tjudged by `frame/defect` only, because no program reaches it on the\n826\tlanded or stage-2 evaluator.\n827\t\n828\t| # | Row | Program (inside each segment) | How parked / satisfied | State, pending | Expected after satisfy | Executable? |\n829\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n830\t| 1 | blocked read | `(let1 s (stream/make 4) (let1 c (cursor-of s) (let1 a (next-of c) (cat (cat [] a) (next-of c)))))`, `\"A\"` appended before the run | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next` | `[\"A\" \"B\"]` | yes |\n831\t| 2 | blocked write | `(stream-put w \"v\")` on a one-slot stream warmed with `:warmed` | take one | act, `:put` with retained `\"v\"` | `\"v\"` landed | yes |\n832\t| 3 | FFI sent | `(dao.stream.apply/call :op/echo \"hello\")` | append the success response | act, `:ffi` | `\"echo!\"` | 4b stage 2 |\n833\t| 4 | FFI retained | as 3 with a one-slot, warmed call-in | free the slot, then answer | act, `:ffi-request` | `\"echo!\"` | 4b stage 2 |\n834\t| 5 | effectful call, non-tail, stream | `(cat [:ordinary] (stream/next! c))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next` | `[:ordinary \"B\"]` | yes (stage 1 has streams; `stream/next!` is the stream module's) |\n835\t| 6 | **effectful call, tail, stream** | `((fn [u] (stream/next! c)) 0)` with the inner call `:tail? true` | append `\"B\"` | **tail**, `:next` | `\"B\"` delivered through `K` | yes |\n836\t| 7 | link request phase | `(require 'host.mod)` parked **before** the request append succeeds (one-slot, warmed request stream) | free the slot, then answer `:ok` | act, `:link-request` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n837\t| 8 | link response phase | `(require 'host.mod)` polling (request appended) | append the `:ok` response | act, `:link-response` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n838\t| 9 | **link, tail** | `((fn [u] (require 'host.mod)) 0)` tail | as 8 | **tail**, `:link-response` | `'host.mod` through `K` | 4b stage 2 |\n839\t| 10 | install, child running | `(require 'host.mod)` where `host.mod`'s body reads a stream the test has not fed, so the child is `:blocked` and the parent's wait is `:install` | feed the child's stream | act, `:install` + install entry, child kind `:blocked`, phase `:running` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n840\t| 11 | install, child parked | as 10 with the child body executing `:park` | resume the child by id | act, `:install`, child kind `:parked`, phase `:parked` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n841\t| 12 | install, tail | `((fn [u] (require 'host.mod)) 0)` tail with 10's child | as 10 | **tail**, `:install` | `'host.mod` through `K` | 4b stage 2 |\n842\t| 13 | explicit park | `(vm/park)` | `(vm/resume pid \"back\")` beside | parked record, root `:parked` | `\"back\"` | yes |\n843\t| 14 | reified capture live in a window | `(let1 k (vm/current-continuation) (cat (cat [] k) (next-of c)))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `k` is a `:reified` value in `W` | `[:continuation \"B\"]` normalised | yes |\n844\t| 15 | capture invoked after lower | `(let1 k (vm/current-continuation) (if (= k :second) :done (let1 r (next-of c) (k :second))))`: on the first pass `k` is a continuation, so the test fails and the read runs and parks; after satisfaction `(k :second)` delivers `:second` into the capture's `rd` (the `let1` operand) and re-enters at the capture's successor pc, so the body is entered again with `k = :second` and the test succeeds **without repeating the read** (the invocation is abortive on every lane: `:continuation` operator kind, `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:212-217` stage 2, `stack.cljc:760-763`). `if` is `{:type :if :test :consequent :alternate}` (`vm.cljc:1069`), a helper the new harness file adds; `=` is the primitive (`vm.cljc:387`), consumed before the read | append `\"x\"` after lower | act, `:next`; `k` is a `:reified` value in `E` (the closure's) | `:done` | yes |\n845\t| 16 | nested capture | `(let1 k1 (vm/current-continuation) (let1 k2 (vm/current-continuation) (cat (cat (cat [] k1) k2) (next-of c))))` | append `\"B\"` | act; `k2`'s act window holds `k1` (a `:reified` inside a `:reified`'s window) | `[:continuation :continuation \"B\"]` | yes |\n846\t| 17 | module context, blocked inside a module closure | `host.mod` exports `f = (fn [c] (stream/next! c))`; root: `(f c)` after `(require 'host.mod)` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`, `E` carries `store-of host.mod` | `\"B\"` | 4b stage 2 (link) |\n847\t| 18 | module-store snapshot | `parked-module-store-cursors` (`lift_support.cljc:310-333`): an explicit park whose module store holds two cursors named by a module closure | resume | parked record; `:module-store` row | the two cursor cells | yes |\n848\t| 19 | halt | `(lit 42)` | — | result | `42` | yes |\n849\t| 20 | **tail-effect halt** | run **outside the wrappers**, in two batches on one machine: a setup batch `(then (def! 's (stream/make 4)) (def! 'c (cursor-of s)))` run to halt (store writes persist across batches, as `lift_support.cljc:320-322` relies on), then the batch `(stream/next! c)` **marked `:tail? true` at the root**, `c` resolving through the store; no application precedes the call, so `K` is empty (any `let1` or wrapper would push a frame, §5.2 above) | append `\"B\"` | tail, `:next` | halts with `\"B\"` (4b: a tail effect with no frame to pop halts, `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:205-211` stage 2; stack: the seam's empty-`K` case, §4.3), env free of `store-of` | yes |\n850\t| 21 | **free-env shadowing** (the review's row) | initial free env `{x 99}`; `(let1 f (fn [] x) ((fn [x] (f (next-of c))) 1))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `E` at the read binds `x` to 1; root `free-env` carries `x 99` | the lowered `f` returns `99` (not nil, not 1) on every lane; **the one row the earlier design would have failed** | yes |\n851\t| 22 | nil-valued live register | `(let1 pair (fn [p q] (cat (cat [] p) q)) (pair nil (next-of c)))`: the `nil` operand is evaluated **before** the read and is live across it (`(cat nil (next-of c))` would give `(conj nil \"B\")`, the list `(\"B\")`, so a vector-building `pair` is used; there is no `vector` primitive, `vm.cljc:380-410`, and `pair` builds through the segment's `cat`) | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `W` has a present key with `[:lit nil]` | `[nil \"B\"]` | yes |\n852\t| 23 | tail FFI bookkeeping | a `tail` parked record under a call id `P ∈ :ffi-call-ids` | — | parked, context `:parked`, bookkeeping clause of P4 | validator accepts; the **same valid tail under a key not in `:ffi-call-ids`** is `:parked-site` (P4); moving `site` to a non-tail call is `:site` (T1, which runs first) | **synthetic**: no primitive returns an FFI effect through a tail `:call` today (evaluator §1.6) |\n853\t| 24 | `act` FFI bookkeeping | 3's parked bookkeeping record under its call id | — | parked, P4 bookkeeping | validator accepts | 4b stage 2 |\n854\t\n855\tRefusal rows: `:yin.k/not-quiescent` (`a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-not-quiescent`,\n856\t`:591-603`); `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (a register kernel stepped one\n857\tinstruction past a `:const`, asked to lift); `:yin.k/non-portable\n858\t:reason-mismatch` (the `:observe` hold, `:675-692`); and the validator's\n859\t**membership tampers**, each a row-set edit of a valid body before\n860\tlower: a window with an extra live key → `:window-extra`; a live key\n861\tremoved → `:window-missing`; `rd` added → `:window-self`; `rd` replaced\n862\tby another register → `:destination`; a return frame's `pc` moved after\n863\ta tail call → `:return-site`; a reason the site does not admit →\n864\t`:reason-mismatch`; a `:tail` row given a fourth slot → `:frame-shape`;\n865\tthe `[:lit nil]` of row 22 dropped → `:window-missing`; a `:reified`\n866\tact whose `pc−1` is not a capture → `:captured-site`; a parked act whose\n867\t`pc−1` is not `:park` → `:parked-site`; for E2, with row 21's initial\n868\tfree environment extended to `{x 99 y 7}`, the **unshadowed** free\n869\tbinding `y` in the act's `E` changed to `8` → `:free-env-mismatch`\n870\t(changing the lexical `x`, which is `1` at the read, trips nothing: E2\n871\tcompares off the lexical names), and the lexical `x` **removed** from\n872\t`E` → `:free-env-mismatch` (presence clause (a)); the root `free-env`\n873\trow given a reserved key → `:reserved-name` (X1). **A changed live value**\n874\t(e.g. `\"A\"` → `\"Z\"` in row 1's window) is not a membership defect and is\n875\tnot expected to be refused: it is tested by **execution parity** (the\n876\tlowered run's result differs from the reference), as the review ruled.\n877\t\n878\t### 5.3 Lanes\n879\t\n880\tThe `:semantic-register` lane's wait entries are canonical frames plus\n881\tengine fields. The de Bruijn lanes load `(:image (derive/stack-image v))`\n882\tand `(:image (derive/register-image v))` of `v = (:vector (project P))`\n883\tthrough `dvm/create-vm`/`rvm/create-vm`, run to the safepoint and lift\n884\tthrough `layout/lift-act`/`lift-tail`; the stack lane's tail rows (6,\n885\t9, 12, 20) require the seam of §4.3 and are gated on 6c-pre.\n886\t\n887\t### 5.4 Laws\n888\t\n889\t1. **Reference parity**: result and effect trace of the lowered,\n890\t   satisfied machine equal the reference run (`reference`/`handed-off`,\n891\t   `:368-401`).\n892\t2. **Round trip**: `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the\n893\t   fresh-receiver context of the draft's §9, after the re-emission of\n894\t   §5.5.\n895\t3. **Observed reason**: the pending's reason is the wait's, never the\n896\t   static kind (ucf-revisions §8).\n897\t4. **Cross-engine canonical equality**: one program at one safepoint\n898\t   lifts to row sets whose root ids are **equal after the re-emission of\n899\t   §5.5** on all three lanes.\n900\t5. **Cross-engine lowering**: a row set lifted on one lane lowers on\n901\t   each of the other two (six ordered pairs) and continues to the\n902\t   reference result.\n903\t6. **Validator agreement**: every lifted frame passes `frame/defect` in\n904\t   its context; every tamper of §5.2 is refused with its kind; the\n905\t   verdict is lane-independent.\n906\t\n907\t### 5.5 Re-emission for equality (replacing \"stream-blinding\")\n908\t\n909\t**PROPOSED** (reviewer: `stream-blind` rewrites decoded maps and does\n910\tnot recompute Merkle ids, so it cannot establish root-id equality). The\n911\tharness first **fixes identities where it can**: all three lanes serve\n912\ttheir streams through one toy channel (`v2_support/toy`,\n913\t`served-peer`), so stream identities and descriptors are equal across\n914\tlanes by construction where the same logical stream is lifted. Where a\n915\tlane mints its own (fresh cursor cells on relift, the receiver's own call\n916\tpair), the comparison uses `rows/canonical-under`, a **test-only**\n917\ttransformation whose placeholder assignment is **never derived from\n918\t*host* traversal order** (the canonical walk of draft §6 is a function\n919\tof the state and is what the procedure reads; the review's objection: map and set traversal, and\n920\t\"cursor cells by body\", can depend on the very identities being erased). It\n921\ttakes an **explicit logical-stream correspondence** from the fixture:\n922\tthe harness created every stream the row reaches, so it supplies\n923\t`{lane-identity → logical-name}` (e.g. `{id-of-s :s, id-of-call-out\n924\t:call-out}`) for each lane; `canonical-under` decodes the row set to the\n925\tabstract task (draft §6's input), replaces each stream marker's identity\n926\tand descriptor by the logical name the map gives it (a marker the map\n927\tdoes not cover is a test failure, never a guess), runs the canonical\n928\tcursor-cell numbering (draft §6), re-emits every row and recomputes every id,\n929\tand compares root ids. Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the\n930\t**cursor cells** (its labels never encode an incoming cursor-cell id), and the only\n931\tother identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's body\n932\tlabel (`[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6 as\n933\tamended, spelled by rank inside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies; after the substitution those are the fixture's\n934\tlogical names, so the procedure's inputs no longer depend on any lane's\n935\tidentities. Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise order of\n936\ttheir members' keys, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the parked table in\n937\tthe byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 W1), never in host\n938\tenumeration order, so\n939\ta permutation of the erased identities cannot change the result once\n940\tthe markers are substituted. It is never used outside the harness.\n941\t\n942\t### 5.6 Static checks the harness runs once per corpus segment\n943\t\n944\t1. `frame/defect` on every frame the reference lane mints: nil.\n945\t2. For every **reachable** parking pc `p` of `v`: `(analysis/saved a\n946\t   (inc p) rd)` has no member `≥ k(body(p))` and does not contain `rd`.\n947\t3. Register layout: at every reachable boundary pc `p`, `virt(p, s)` is\n948\t   single-valued on the physical image of `live-out*(p)`; the `live`\n949\t   operand of `R[p]` equals `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(p) rd)))`,\n950\t   the destination removed as a **virtual** id before mapping, which is\n951\t   `[]` for a tail call (phase-5 §2.5). Worked example, inner call at\n952\t   `p = 3`: `L(4) = {1 2}`, minus `rd 2` → `{1}` → `[0]`, the pinned\n953\t   `live` of phase-5 §6.3 row 2.\n954\t4. Stack layout: the k-th non-push instruction of `H` has the mnemonic\n955\t   family of `v[k]` for every `k`; counts agree.\n956\t5. Stack layout: `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p)\n957\t   rd)` for every reachable **non-tail** parking pc `p`.\n958\t6. `E` reconstruction: at every safepoint reached, the layout's `E`\n959\t   equals the reference lane's `E` (values normalised), including rows\n960\t   21 and 22 and the `:duplicate-param`, `:nested-closure`,\n961\t   `:nested-lambdas` programs.\n962\t7. Closure reconstruction: canonical ↔ positional closures round-trip on\n963\t   the `\"closure value\"` and `\"higher order\"` B0 rows and\n964\t   `:duplicate-param`.\n965\t8. Tail lowering: the `:frames`/`:store-of` chosen for a lowered `tail`\n966\t   entry are unobservable: lowering with `[]`/`nil` and with arbitrary\n967\t   values gives equal observables on rows 6, 9, 12, 20.\n968\t9. Slices: for every non-tail safepoint with `m` return frames, the\n969\t   `m+1` slices of §4.3 partition the stack kernel's operand stack\n970\t   exactly, and each has the length of its `stack-layout`.\n971\t\n972\t---\n973\t\n974\t## 6. Code and task context in the carrier\n975\t\n976\t### 6.1 Code\n977\t\n978\t**DECIDED** (rulings 1, 3, 4; phase-5 §7.6, §9.2; v2 amendment §4's\n979\t2026-10-10 note). The root's `code` slot is plain `{A canonical-vector}`"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b921a1a6-65e4-4bc4-853c-fc87c8b912fe","uuid":"193733e5-5e05-44ae-9260-e0bb2ab4ffd2","timestamp":"2026-10-12T17:41:52.046Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6c/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"\n### 4.1 What a layout is\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §4.3; UCF §7.4.2). A foreign engine publishes a\nstatic map per segment and engine (`A`, resume pcs, `def`/`use`/`L`\nfrom `analysis/analyze`, `lexically-required`, and a layout from\nphysical location to virtual register) and reconstructs the canonical\nframe from its state at a safepoint; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame` on\nadmitted canonical frames. Two kernels are intended to reconstruct\ncanonical frames: the de Bruijn register kernel over `R(A)` and the de\nBruijn stack kernel over `H(A)` (phase 5). The AST walker is not (frozen\n§10 phase 6 names the three kernels; the walker has no pc).\n\n**PROPOSED** namespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.layout`, file\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`, requiring\n`yin.vm.semantic-register.code`, `.analysis`, `.derive` (phase 5),\n`yin.vm.debruijn` (`resolve-name`), `yin.vm.engine` (`bind-params`), and\nthe two kernels' public state. Public: `register-layout`,\n`stack-layout` (static, per segment), `lift-act`, `lift-tail`,\n`lower-act`, `lower-tail` (per kernel, dispatching on the kernel's\n`link-format`).\n\n### 4.2 The register kernel's layout\n\n**DECIDED** by phase-5 §2.4 (R's bodies and pcs are A's) and §2.3\n(`R(v) = locals + slot(v)`); the kernel's payload shape from\n`debruijn/register.cljc:690-704` (call frame: `:site-pc :return-pc\n:frames :regs :live :dest :store-of`) and `write-back` (`:455-485`:\n`:write-result` rebuilds a dense register vector from `regs` and writes\n`dest`; `:return-result` runs the return transition).\n\n**Static half.** For segment `A` with `R = (:image (derive/register-image\nv))`: for every pc `p`, `v[p]` names virtual registers in its `rd`,\n`:reg` and `:regs` slots and `R[p]` names physical registers in the same\nslots. `phys(body, v)` is read off any pc that names `v`; it is total on\nthe virtuals a body names. Its inverse is **restricted**: `virt(p, s)`\nis defined only for `s` in the physical image of\n`live-out*(p) = ⋃ L(q) for q ∈ (analysis/successors v p)` (the actual\nsuccessors, phase-5 §2.5; empty for a tail call) at a **reachable**\nboundary pc `p` (`analysis/reachable`); there it is \"the `v ∈ live-out*(p)`\nwith `phys(v) = s`\", single-valued because the allocator's intervals\ncover every live point and distinct live virtuals hold distinct slots\n(a property the harness checks, §5.6 item 3, never assumed). Physical\noperands of **unreachable** layout-syntax instructions (the structural\nslot shared by several definition-less ids, phase-5 §2.2) are not\ninverted; they are validated separately by the kernel's own\n`register-image-defect` and by phase-5's gates, and never reach a\ncanonical frame (no reachable instruction reads them).\n\n**Lift, `act`.** From the kernel's wait entry with `:resume-mode\n:write-result` (payload `:image :site-pc :pc :frames :regs :live\n:continuation :dest :store-of`, v2 amendment §5.3):\n\n1. `A` = the A the image row of the entry's `:image` was derived from\n   (the kernel's code space is keyed by A after phase 5; R is its\n   checksum).\n2. relative `pc` = the entry's `pc` minus the image row's offset\n   (`image-row`/`absolute-pc`, `debruijn/register.cljc:326-337`); R's pcs\n   are A's, so this is the canonical pc.\n3. `rd = (code/rd (nth v (dec pc)))` **from A**, never the physical\n   `:dest` (which is `phys(rd)`).\n4. `W = {virt(pc−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ regs}`; the kernel's `live` is\n   `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(pc−1) rd)))` (the subtraction is on\n   **virtual** ids before mapping: on the worked example `live-out*(3) =\n   L(4) = {1 2}`, `(disj … 2) = {1}`, `phys → [0]`; subtracting the\n   physical `3` from `{1 2}` would wrongly keep `2`), so `(keys W) =\n   saved(pc, rd)`, which rule A5/A6 then checks.\n5. `E` = §4.4 from the entry's `frames` and `store-of`, at `body(pc)`.\n6. `K`: for each continuation frame `f`, **independently**: its\n   `return-pc` is absolute in the kernel's concatenated code space, so\n   first its image row `rowₖ = (image-row images return-pc)` and relative\n   `pcₖ = return-pc − offsetₖ`, then `Aₖ` of that row (frames may cross\n   images: a module closure called from another image returns into its\n   caller's image), `rdₖ = (code/rd (nth vₖ (dec pcₖ)))` from `Aₖ`, never\n   `f.dest`; `Wₖ = {virt_{Aₖ}(pcₖ−1, s) ↦ V : [s V] ∈ f.regs}`; `Eₖ` = §4.4\n   from `f.frames` and `f.store-of` at `body(pcₖ)` in `Aₖ`.\n7. pending: the engine fields of the entry, as `lift-pending!` maps them\n   (`handoff.cljc:674-749`).\n\n**Lift, `tail`.** From an entry with `:resume-mode :return-result`\n(`dest nil`, `live []`): `A` and the relative `site = site-pc` as in 1-2\n(the entry's `site-pc` is the tail call; its `pc` is `site+1`); `K` as\nin 6; pending as in 7; **no** `W`, `E` or `pc` are read: the entry's\n`regs` (empty by `live []`) and `frames` are discarded (frozen §2.4: a\ntail state carries no window and no `E`).\n\n**Lower, `act`.** Given a canonical `act` and the receiver's attached\nimage for `A` (derived by `derive/register-image` from the carried\nvector, offset `off`): `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + pc − 1`;\n`:pc = off + pc`; `:regs = (sort-by first [[phys(v) V] for [v V] in W])`;\n`:live = (sort (map phys (keys W)))` (the kernel's `live-exact` rule\nrecomputes `body-liveness` and must agree); `:dest = phys(rd)`;\n`:resume-mode :write-result`; `:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of`\nfrom `E`; `:continuation` = for each `K[i]`, with its own `Aₖ`'s image\nrow `offₖ`: `{:site-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ −1) :return-pc (+ offₖ pcₖ) :regs\n… :live … :dest (phys_{Aₖ} rdₖ) :frames (§4.4 from Eₖ) :store-of …}`;\n`:format reffects/format-tag`, `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's current\naggregate (never carried). Engine fields from the pending\n(`lower-frame!`, `handoff.cljc:3182-3246`), `:deliver` carried.\n\n**Lower, `tail`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:site-pc = off + site`;\n`:pc = off + site + 1`; `:regs []`; `:live []`; `:dest nil`;\n`:resume-mode :return-result`; `:frames []` and `:store-of nil`\n(unused: `write-back`'s `:return-result` branch reads `:frames` and\n`:store-of` only to assign them before the return transition replaces\nthem from the popped frame, `register.cljc:461-468`; the harness checks\nthat the values chosen here are never observable, §5.6 item 8);\n`:continuation` as for `act`. The register kernel already supports this\ndelivery (`:return-result`, `register.cljc:458-468`).\n\n### 4.3 The stack kernel's layout\n\n**DECIDED** by phase-5 §3.2 (the emitter's walk) and the kernel's call\nand return arms (`debruijn/stack.cljc:716-776`): a non-tail `:call`\npops `f` and the `argc` operands, leaving `stack'`, and pushes\n`{:return-pc (inc pc) :frames frames :stack-base (count stack')}`\n(`:728-733`); `:return` truncates to the frame's `stack-base` and pushes\nthe value (`:766-772`); a host-function effect parks with the\ncontinuation after the call site and `stack-restore` resumes by conjing\nthe value at the entry's `pc` (`:748-752`, `:555-568`).\n\n**Static half.**\n\n1. **pc correspondence.** The stack emitter visits parse nodes in\n   `project`'s order and emits one instruction per A instruction plus a\n   `:push` after each child of a call, each `:stream-put` target and\n   each `:ffi-call` operand (phase-5 §3.2). So H **with every `:push`\n   removed** corresponds one-to-one, in order, with A: the k-th non-push\n   H instruction ↔ A pc `k`; `h->a`, `a->h` are the two directions. The\n   harness checks mnemonic families agree at every k (§5.6 item 4).\n2. **operand layout.** For an A pc `p` and the parse node at `p`,\n   `stack-layout(p)` is the vector, bottom to top, of the virtual\n   registers whose values are pushed and not yet consumed while control\n   is inside `p`'s expression: for each ancestor `n` and the child `c` on\n   the path to `p`, the children of `n` before `c` that `n` pushes (a\n   `:call`: the operator and the operands before `c`; a `:stream-put`:\n   the target when `c` is the value; an `:ffi-call`: the operands before\n   `c`; `:if`, `:define`, `:stream-cursor/next/close`, `:resume`: none).\n   **Checked** (§5.6 item 5): for a **non-tail** parking instruction,\n   `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p) (code/rd v[p]))`.\n   For a **tail** `:call` the canonical state has no window and\n   `stack-layout(p)` is not consulted (it may be non-empty for a tail\n   call in operand position, whose pushed siblings are dead layout\n   syntax).\n\n**K slices (corrected).** Let the kernel's continuation be\n`[f₀ … f_{m−1}]` outermost first with bases `b₀ … b_{m−1}` and the\noperand stack `St` of length `n`. Because `bᵢ = (count stack')` is the\ncaller's residual height **after** popping the callee's operands, the\nslice of operands belonging to activation `i` is\n\n```\nslice(f₀)      = St[0, b₀)\nslice(fᵢ)      = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)        for 0 < i < m\nslice(current) = St[b_{m−1}, n)         ; or St[0, n) when K is empty\n```\n\n(the review's correction: `[previous-base, base_i)` with previous-base\n0, and the current activation `[last-base, count)`).\n\n**Lift, `act`.** From a wait entry `{:image :pc :frames :stack\n:continuation :store-of}` (v2 amendment §5.2) whose `pc−1` in H is **not**\na tail `:call`:\n\n1. `A` from the image row; relative H pc `pcₕ = pc − off`; canonical\n   `pc = h->a(pcₕ − 1) + 1`; `rd = (code/rd v[pc−1])`.\n2. `W = (zipmap (stack-layout (dec pc)) slice(current))`; the two have\n   equal length (checked) and `(keys W) = saved(pc, rd)` (rule A5/A6).\n3. `E` = §4.4 from `frames` and `store-of` at `body(pc)`.\n4. `K`: for `fᵢ` **independently**: its `return-pc` is absolute; row and\n   relative `pcₕᵢ` through the stack kernel's own offset table\n   (`debruijn/stack.cljc:355`, `absolute-pc`'s inverse); `Aᵢ` of that row;\n   `pcᵢ = h->a_{Aᵢ}(pcₕᵢ − 1) + 1`; `rdᵢ = (code/rd vᵢ[pcᵢ−1])`;\n   `Wᵢ = (zipmap (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) slice(fᵢ))`; `Eᵢ` = §4.4\n   from `fᵢ.frames` and `fᵢ.store-of`.\n5. pending from the engine fields.\n\n**Lift, `tail`.** From a wait entry whose `pc−1` in H is a tail `:call`:\n`A` and `site = h->a(pcₕ − 1)`; `K` as in 4; pending; the entry's\n`frames`, `store-of` and `slice(current)` are **discarded** (dead\nlayout syntax; frozen §2.4).\n\n**Lower, `act`.** `:image` the row of `A`; `:pc = off + a->h(pc−1) + 1`;\n`:frames` by §4.4 from `E`; `:store-of` from `E`; `:stack` = the\nconcatenation over `K` outermost first of each `slice(fᵢ)` =\n`(mapv Wᵢ (stack-layout_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)))`, then `slice(current) =\n(mapv W (stack-layout (dec pc)))`; `:continuation` = for each `fᵢ`\n`{:return-pc (+ offᵢ (a->h_{Aᵢ} (dec pcᵢ)) 1) :frames (§4.4 from Eᵢ)\n:stack-base bᵢ :store-of …}` where **`bᵢ` is the cumulative length of\nthe slices up to and including `slice(fᵢ)`**: `b₀ = |slice(f₀)|`, `bᵢ =\nb_{i−1} + |slice(fᵢ)|`. This is the inverse of the lift's\n`slice(fᵢ) = St[b_{i−1}, bᵢ)`: the kernel's base is the caller's\nresidual height, which is exactly the end of the caller's own slice\n(`stack.cljc:731-733`). On the multi-frame fixture of §8.3 item 6\n(five return frames, slices `[0,0) [0,0) [0,0) [0,2) [2,4)`) this emits\nbases `0, 0, 0, 2, 4`; the before-slice rule would emit `0, 0, 0, 0, 2`\nand the kernel would truncate to the wrong height on return. `:format\nstack/format-tag`; `:segment`/`:hash` the receiver's. `stack-restore`\nthen conjes the delivered value onto `:stack` at `:pc`, the `:push`-free\nposition after the parking instruction, exactly where H expects the\nresult.\n\n**Lower, `tail`, and the tail-completion seam.** Today the stack kernel\nresumes a parked host effect at the entry's `pc` by pushing the value\n(`stack-restore`, `:566`); for a **tail** call that resumes the body at\n`a->h(site)+1`, which then runs the layout syntax after the tail call\n(for a tail call in operand position: the remaining pushes and the\nenclosing call) **before** reaching `:return`. That is not the canonical\nthrough-return delivery (frozen §2.4, §4.1 \"effectful call, tail\":\ndelivery **through return** against `K`). The layout therefore cannot\nlower a canonical `tail` into today's stack kernel. **PROPOSED, gated\n(§8.3 slice 6c-pre):** the stack kernel gains a tail-completion seam:\nits park-entry builders mark an entry whose parking instruction is a\ntail `:call` with `:resume-mode :return-result` (the register kernel's\nexisting vocabulary, `register.cljc:458-468`), and `stack-restore`\ndelivers such an entry by the **return transition** (pop the top frame,\ntruncate `:stack` to its base, push the value, restore its `frames`,\n`store-of` and `return-pc`; halt with the value when `K` is empty) instead\nof resuming at `pc`; the retained-FFI re-park and the response decoding\n(`stack-restore`'s `:request-sent` and `:call-id` steps, `:548-557`) keep\ntheir order before the final delivery, as in the register kernel.\nLowering a canonical `tail` then produces\n`{:image row-of-A :pc (+ off (a->h site) 1) :resume-mode :return-result\n:stack (concat of the K slices, in order) :frames [] :store-of nil\n:continuation …}` with each continuation frame's `:stack-base bᵢ` the\n**same cumulative-including rule as the `act` lowering** (the last\nframe's base equals the stack's length, since the current activation\ncontributes no slice); the `:frames`/`:store-of` values are unobservable\n(the return transition replaces them) and the harness checks that (§5.6\nitem 8). File:\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc` (not a 4b or phase-5 file); the\nregister kernel needs no change.\n\n### 4.4 Positional lexical frames, the free environment and `E`\n\n**DECIDED** by frozen §2.1 and §2.4 (`E_c[ps ↦ a]` by `bind-params`),\nphase-5 §1.2 (the owner chain), and the kernels' resolution of free\nnames through the task-wide `free-env` (`stack.cljc:683`:\n`(engine/resolve-var free-env …)`). Both de Bruijn kernels hold the\nlexical environment as positional frames `F = [[V …] …]` outermost first,\none per enclosing closure activation, plus the task-wide `free-env`.\n\n**Forward (F → E), lossless.** At a pc of body `b` with\n`chain = [c₀ … c_{j}]` outermost first (phase-5's `chain` reversed):\n\n```\nE = (merge free-env\n           (bind-params params(c₀) F[0])\n           …\n           (bind-params params(c_j) F[j]))\n    ∪ {engine/store-of-key M}  when the activation's closure carries store-of M\n```\n\nThis reproduces the register evaluator's `E` on closure entry\n(`semantic_register.cljc:141-143`: `(merge (:env c) (bind-params\n(:params c) args))`, `(:env c)` being the creating activation's `E`,\nitself `free-env` merged with the outer chain), including rightmost-wins\nduplicates, nil-fill and extra-argument dropping, which are\n`engine/bind-params`'s.\n\n**Inverse (E → F), and why the free environment travels.** The reviewer's\ncounterexample, with initial free `x = 99`:\n\n```clojure\n(let [f (fn [] x)]\n  ((fn [x] (f (stream-next c))) 1))\n```\n\nAt the blocked read the current `E` binds `x` to 1 (the parameter). The\nearlier revision recovered the receiver's free environment as \"`E` minus\nthe chain's names\", which drops `x` entirely, and the lowered de Bruijn\nclosure `f`, whose body is `[:load-free x]`, resolves `x` through the\ntask-wide `free-env` and loses 99. **So the task's free environment is\ncarried explicitly** (root slot `free-env`, §2.6; §6.3), every `E` is\nchecked against it off the lexical names (rule E2), and the inverse is:\n\n```\nfree-env  := the root's free-env row (never derived from any E)\nF[i]      := (mapv #(get E %) params(cᵢ))      for i = 0 … j\n```\n\n`F[i]` for an **outer** frame whose parameter is shadowed by an inner\nframe receives the inner value, not the outer one; that value is\nunobservable from `body(pc)` (resolution picks the innermost binder,\n`resolve-name`, `debruijn.cljc:617-637`), and the canonical frame does\nnot carry it (frozen §2.1: parameters live in `E`, and `E` holds one\nvalue per name). The round-trip law is therefore stated on **canonical\nframes** (`lift(lower(frame)) = frame`), not on kernel state, and the\nharness's `E` check (§5.6 item 6) compares the layout's `E` with the\nevaluator's; shadowed outer positional values are not a claim of this\ndesign. The free bindings that *are* observable, by any closure's\n`:load-free`, are preserved exactly by the root slot.\n\n**Closure values.** A de Bruijn closure (positional marker: segment,\nentry, arity, frames `F`, store-of) reconstructs to the canonical\nclosure (`{:type :closure :params :entry :segment :env}`, evaluator\n§1.8): `params` from A's `:closure` tuple at the owner pc of the entry\nbody, `env` by the forward rule over the closure's captured `F` and the\ntask's `free-env`, `entry` the body pc. The inverse reads `F` back from\n`env` by the chain. Checked both ways (§5.6 item 7).\n\n### 4.5 What a layout never does\n\n**DECIDED.** A layout reads a kernel's state, A and the carried task\ncontext; it never runs code, never fills a binding from the receiver's\nstore or free environment (the free environment is the **carried** one,\n§6.3), never reads a native image's hash as an identity, and never\ninvents a safepoint: a kernel pc whose predecessor is not a parking\ninstruction is `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (UCF §7.4.2).\n\n---\n\n## 5. The safepoint harness\n\n### 5.1 What it proves\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §10 phase 6 gate; UCF §7.11). For every row of the\nexpanded matrix, both delivery modes, nested and reified captures,\nmodule contexts and every refusal: lift at the safepoint, lower into a\nfresh machine, continue, and result and effect trace equal the\nuninterrupted reference run; `lift(lower(frame)) = frame`; the frame's\nreason is the observed wait's; on the three lanes: `:semantic-register`,\n`:stack-through-layout`, `:register-through-layout`.\n\n### 5.2 The matrix\n\n**Expected results are stated for the bare expression.** Each row runs\nunder the four segment wrappers of the old harness\n(`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:92-106`), whose expected results follow\nmechanically from the bare result `r`: `:bare` → `r`; `:operand` →\n`[:operand r]` (`(cat [:operand] r)`); `:depth-3` → `[3 [2 [1 r]]]`\n(`nest` wraps `(cat [d] ((fn [x] …) d))` three times, as the old test's\npinned `[2 [1 [:two 2 \"C\"]]]` shows for depth 2, `:574`); `:captured` →\n`[:captured :arg r]` (`(cat (cat (cat [] k) y) r)` with `k = :captured`,\n`y = :arg`). **Frames the wrappers add.** The harness marks `:tail?\ntrue` only where a row says so (`:228`, `:256`; `ast->semantic-bytecode`\ndefaults it to false, `vm.cljc:1089`; `mark-tail` is the expander's and\nis not run here), so every `let1` and every `nest` application is a\nnon-tail call that pushes a return frame. `with-cat` (`:69-76`,\noutermost in every segment, `:96`) adds **one** frame below everything\nthe row builds, with `stack-base 0` and an empty slice on the stack lane\n(nothing is pushed before its application). On top of it `:bare` and\n`:operand` add none of their own (the row is the operand), `:depth-3`\nadds three (`nest`'s applications, each with two values, `cat` and\n`[d]`, pushed under it), and `:captured` adds three (`let1 k`, `let1 f`,\n`(f :arg)`). A row's own `let1`s add theirs. Row 20 alone runs outside\nthe wrappers.\n\nPrograms use `v2_support`'s AST helpers (`v2_support.cljc:56-104`:\n`lit v lam app def! then cursor-of next-of let1`) and the four segments\nof the old harness (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:92-106`: `:bare`,\n`:operand`, `:depth-3`, `:captured`); `cat` is the closure `(fn [p q]\n(conj p q))` bound around every body (`:69-81`). \"Expected\" is the\nreference run's normalised result; where a row says \"executable\" it runs\non all three lanes; \"synthetic\" means the frame is built by hand and\njudged by `frame/defect` only, because no program reaches it on the\nlanded or stage-2 evaluator.\n\n| # | Row | Program (inside each segment) | How parked / satisfied | State, pending | Expected after satisfy | Executable? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | blocked read | `(let1 s (stream/make 4) (let1 c (cursor-of s) (let1 a (next-of c) (cat (cat [] a) (next-of c)))))`, `\"A\"` appended before the run | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next` | `[\"A\" \"B\"]` | yes |\n| 2 | blocked write | `(stream-put w \"v\")` on a one-slot stream warmed with `:warmed` | take one | act, `:put` with retained `\"v\"` | `\"v\"` landed | yes |\n| 3 | FFI sent | `(dao.stream.apply/call :op/echo \"hello\")` | append the success response | act, `:ffi` | `\"echo!\"` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 4 | FFI retained | as 3 with a one-slot, warmed call-in | free the slot, then answer | act, `:ffi-request` | `\"echo!\"` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 5 | effectful call, non-tail, stream | `(cat [:ordinary] (stream/next! c))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next` | `[:ordinary \"B\"]` | yes (stage 1 has streams; `stream/next!` is the stream module's) |\n| 6 | **effectful call, tail, stream** | `((fn [u] (stream/next! c)) 0)` with the inner call `:tail? true` | append `\"B\"` | **tail**, `:next` | `\"B\"` delivered through `K` | yes |\n| 7 | link request phase | `(require 'host.mod)` parked **before** the request append succeeds (one-slot, warmed request stream) | free the slot, then answer `:ok` | act, `:link-request` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 8 | link response phase | `(require 'host.mod)` polling (request appended) | append the `:ok` response | act, `:link-response` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 9 | **link, tail** | `((fn [u] (require 'host.mod)) 0)` tail | as 8 | **tail**, `:link-response` | `'host.mod` through `K` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 10 | install, child running | `(require 'host.mod)` where `host.mod`'s body reads a stream the test has not fed, so the child is `:blocked` and the parent's wait is `:install` | feed the child's stream | act, `:install` + install entry, child kind `:blocked`, phase `:running` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 11 | install, child parked | as 10 with the child body executing `:park` | resume the child by id | act, `:install`, child kind `:parked`, phase `:parked` | `'host.mod` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 12 | install, tail | `((fn [u] (require 'host.mod)) 0)` tail with 10's child | as 10 | **tail**, `:install` | `'host.mod` through `K` | 4b stage 2 |\n| 13 | explicit park | `(vm/park)` | `(vm/resume pid \"back\")` beside | parked record, root `:parked` | `\"back\"` | yes |\n| 14 | reified capture live in a window | `(let1 k (vm/current-continuation) (cat (cat [] k) (next-of c)))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `k` is a `:reified` value in `W` | `[:continuation \"B\"]` normalised | yes |\n| 15 | capture invoked after lower | `(let1 k (vm/current-continuation) (if (= k :second) :done (let1 r (next-of c) (k :second))))`: on the first pass `k` is a continuation, so the test fails and the read runs and parks; after satisfaction `(k :second)` delivers `:second` into the capture's `rd` (the `let1` operand) and re-enters at the capture's successor pc, so the body is entered again with `k = :second` and the test succeeds **without repeating the read** (the invocation is abortive on every lane: `:continuation` operator kind, `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:212-217` stage 2, `stack.cljc:760-763`). `if` is `{:type :if :test :consequent :alternate}` (`vm.cljc:1069`), a helper the new harness file adds; `=` is the primitive (`vm.cljc:387`), consumed before the read | append `\"x\"` after lower | act, `:next`; `k` is a `:reified` value in `E` (the closure's) | `:done` | yes |\n| 16 | nested capture | `(let1 k1 (vm/current-continuation) (let1 k2 (vm/current-continuation) (cat (cat (cat [] k1) k2) (next-of c))))` | append `\"B\"` | act; `k2`'s act window holds `k1` (a `:reified` inside a `:reified`'s window) | `[:continuation :continuation \"B\"]` | yes |\n| 17 | module context, blocked inside a module closure | `host.mod` exports `f = (fn [c] (stream/next! c))`; root: `(f c)` after `(require 'host.mod)` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`, `E` carries `store-of host.mod` | `\"B\"` | 4b stage 2 (link) |\n| 18 | module-store snapshot | `parked-module-store-cursors` (`lift_support.cljc:310-333`): an explicit park whose module store holds two cursors named by a module closure | resume | parked record; `:module-store` row | the two cursor cells | yes |\n| 19 | halt | `(lit 42)` | — | result | `42` | yes |\n| 20 | **tail-effect halt** | run **outside the wrappers**, in two batches on one machine: a setup batch `(then (def! 's (stream/make 4)) (def! 'c (cursor-of s)))` run to halt (store writes persist across batches, as `lift_support.cljc:320-322` relies on), then the batch `(stream/next! c)` **marked `:tail? true` at the root**, `c` resolving through the store; no application precedes the call, so `K` is empty (any `let1` or wrapper would push a frame, §5.2 above) | append `\"B\"` | tail, `:next` | halts with `\"B\"` (4b: a tail effect with no frame to pop halts, `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc:205-211` stage 2; stack: the seam's empty-`K` case, §4.3), env free of `store-of` | yes |\n| 21 | **free-env shadowing** (the review's row) | initial free env `{x 99}`; `(let1 f (fn [] x) ((fn [x] (f (next-of c))) 1))` | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `E` at the read binds `x` to 1; root `free-env` carries `x 99` | the lowered `f` returns `99` (not nil, not 1) on every lane; **the one row the earlier design would have failed** | yes |\n| 22 | nil-valued live register | `(let1 pair (fn [p q] (cat (cat [] p) q)) (pair nil (next-of c)))`: the `nil` operand is evaluated **before** the read and is live across it (`(cat nil (next-of c))` would give `(conj nil \"B\")`, the list `(\"B\")`, so a vector-building `pair` is used; there is no `vector` primitive, `vm.cljc:380-410`, and `pair` builds through the segment's `cat`) | append `\"B\"` | act, `:next`; `W` has a present key with `[:lit nil]` | `[nil \"B\"]` | yes |\n| 23 | tail FFI bookkeeping | a `tail` parked record under a call id `P ∈ :ffi-call-ids` | — | parked, context `:parked`, bookkeeping clause of P4 | validator accepts; the **same valid tail under a key not in `:ffi-call-ids`** is `:parked-site` (P4); moving `site` to a non-tail call is `:site` (T1, which runs first) | **synthetic**: no primitive returns an FFI effect through a tail `:call` today (evaluator §1.6) |\n| 24 | `act` FFI bookkeeping | 3's parked bookkeeping record under its call id | — | parked, P4 bookkeeping | validator accepts | 4b stage 2 |\n\nRefusal rows: `:yin.k/not-quiescent` (`a-nonempty-ready-queue-is-not-quiescent`,\n`:591-603`); `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` (a register kernel stepped one\ninstruction past a `:const`, asked to lift); `:yin.k/non-portable\n:reason-mismatch` (the `:observe` hold, `:675-692`); and the validator's\n**membership tampers**, each a row-set edit of a valid body before\nlower: a window with an extra live key → `:window-extra`; a live key\nremoved → `:window-missing`; `rd` added → `:window-self`; `rd` replaced\nby another register → `:destination`; a return frame's `pc` moved after\na tail call → `:return-site`; a reason the site does not admit →\n`:reason-mismatch`; a `:tail` row given a fourth slot → `:frame-shape`;\nthe `[:lit nil]` of row 22 dropped → `:window-missing`; a `:reified`\nact whose `pc−1` is not a capture → `:captured-site`; a parked act whose\n`pc−1` is not `:park` → `:parked-site`; for E2, with row 21's initial\nfree environment extended to `{x 99 y 7}`, the **unshadowed** free\nbinding `y` in the act's `E` changed to `8` → `:free-env-mismatch`\n(changing the lexical `x`, which is `1` at the read, trips nothing: E2\ncompares off the lexical names), and the lexical `x` **removed** from\n`E` → `:free-env-mismatch` (presence clause (a)); the root `free-env`\nrow given a reserved key → `:reserved-name` (X1). **A changed live value**\n(e.g. `\"A\"` → `\"Z\"` in row 1's window) is not a membership defect and is\nnot expected to be refused: it is tested by **execution parity** (the\nlowered run's result differs from the reference), as the review ruled.\n\n### 5.3 Lanes\n\nThe `:semantic-register` lane's wait entries are canonical frames plus\nengine fields. The de Bruijn lanes load `(:image (derive/stack-image v))`\nand `(:image (derive/register-image v))` of `v = (:vector (project P))`\nthrough `dvm/create-vm`/`rvm/create-vm`, run to the safepoint and lift\nthrough `layout/lift-act`/`lift-tail`; the stack lane's tail rows (6,\n9, 12, 20) require the seam of §4.3 and are gated on 6c-pre.\n\n### 5.4 Laws\n\n1. **Reference parity**: result and effect trace of the lowered,\n   satisfied machine equal the reference run (`reference`/`handed-off`,\n   `:368-401`).\n2. **Round trip**: `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the\n   fresh-receiver context of the draft's §9, after the re-emission of\n   §5.5.\n3. **Observed reason**: the pending's reason is the wait's, never the\n   static kind (ucf-revisions §8).\n4. **Cross-engine canonical equality**: one program at one safepoint\n   lifts to row sets whose root ids are **equal after the re-emission of\n   §5.5** on all three lanes.\n5. **Cross-engine lowering**: a row set lifted on one lane lowers on\n   each of the other two (six ordered pairs) and continues to the\n   reference result.\n6. **Validator agreement**: every lifted frame passes `frame/defect` in\n   its context; every tamper of §5.2 is refused with its kind; the\n   verdict is lane-independent.\n\n### 5.5 Re-emission for equality (replacing \"stream-blinding\")\n\n**PROPOSED** (reviewer: `stream-blind` rewrites decoded maps and does\nnot recompute Merkle ids, so it cannot establish root-id equality). The\nharness first **fixes identities where it can**: all three lanes serve\ntheir streams through one toy channel (`v2_support/toy`,\n`served-peer`), so stream identities and descriptors are equal across\nlanes by construction where the same logical stream is lifted. Where a\nlane mints its own (fresh cursor cells on relift, the receiver's own call\npair), the comparison uses `rows/canonical-under`, a **test-only**\ntransformation whose placeholder assignment is **never derived from\n*host* traversal order** (the canonical walk of draft §6 is a function\nof the state and is what the procedure reads; the review's objection: map and set traversal, and\n\"cursor cells by body\", can depend on the very identities being erased). It\ntakes an **explicit logical-stream correspondence** from the fixture:\nthe harness created every stream the row reaches, so it supplies\n`{lane-identity → logical-name}` (e.g. `{id-of-s :s, id-of-call-out\n:call-out}`) for each lane; `canonical-under` decodes the row set to the\nabstract task (draft §6's input), replaces each stream marker's identity\nand descriptor by the logical name the map gives it (a marker the map\ndoes not cover is a test failure, never a guess), runs the canonical\ncursor-cell numbering (draft §6), re-emits every row and recomputes every id,\nand compares root ids. Draft §6 is invariant under renaming of the\n**cursor cells** (its labels never encode an incoming cursor-cell id), and the only\nother identity it consumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's body\nlabel (`[:body [:stream identity descriptor] position]`, draft §6 as\namended, spelled by rank inside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies; after the substitution those are the fixture's\nlogical names, so the procedure's inputs no longer depend on any lane's\nidentities. Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise order of\ntheir members' keys, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the parked table in\nthe byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 W1), never in host\nenumeration order, so\na permutation of the erased identities cannot change the result once\nthe markers are substituted. It is never used outside the harness.\n\n### 5.6 Static checks the harness runs once per corpus segment\n\n1. `frame/defect` on every frame the reference lane mints: nil.\n2. For every **reachable** parking pc `p` of `v`: `(analysis/saved a\n   (inc p) rd)` has no member `≥ k(body(p))` and does not contain `rd`.\n3. Register layout: at every reachable boundary pc `p`, `virt(p, s)` is\n   single-valued on the physical image of `live-out*(p)`; the `live`\n   operand of `R[p]` equals `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(p) rd)))`,\n   the destination removed as a **virtual** id before mapping, which is\n   `[]` for a tail call (phase-5 §2.5). Worked example, inner call at\n   `p = 3`: `L(4) = {1 2}`, minus `rd 2` → `{1}` → `[0]`, the pinned\n   `live` of phase-5 §6.3 row 2.\n4. Stack layout: the k-th non-push instruction of `H` has the mnemonic\n   family of `v[k]` for every `k`; counts agree.\n5. Stack layout: `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p)\n   rd)` for every reachable **non-tail** parking pc `p`.\n6. `E` reconstruction: at every safepoint reached, the layout's `E`\n   equals the reference lane's `E` (values normalised), including rows\n   21 and 22 and the `:duplicate-param`, `:nested-closure`,\n   `:nested-lambdas` programs.\n7. Closure reconstruction: canonical ↔ positional closures round-trip on\n   the `\"closure value\"` and `\"higher order\"` B0 rows and\n   `:duplicate-param`.\n8. Tail lowering: the `:frames`/`:store-of` chosen for a lowered `tail`\n   entry are unobservable: lowering with `[]`/`nil` and with arbitrary\n   values gives equal observables on rows 6, 9, 12, 20.\n9. Slices: for every non-tail safepoint with `m` return frames, the\n   `m+1` slices of §4.3 partition the stack kernel's operand stack\n   exactly, and each has the length of its `stack-layout`.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Code and task context in the carrier\n\n### 6.1 Code\n\n**DECIDED** (rulings 1, 3, 4; phase-5 §7.6, §9.2; v2 amendment §4's\n2026-10-10 note). The root's `code` slot is plain `{A canonical-vector}`","numLines":510,"startLine":470,"totalLines":1762}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014ZXT6c1bzyGnXnjLnyb884","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
