{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfxko9rfzawZzLH8croea","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015jWXGfp1D9xGeZZzFaWe4r","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6c/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6470,"cache_read_input_tokens":134715,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6470},"output_tokens":53,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b921a1a6-65e4-4bc4-853c-fc87c8b912fe","uuid":"34d04811-87fd-4ebd-9bd9-8281537d3f5b","timestamp":"2026-10-12T17:42:27.424Z","request_id":"req_011Cfxko9ZKT6j3kxMyWT86o","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_015jWXGfp1D9xGeZZzFaWe4r":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6c/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015jWXGfp1D9xGeZZzFaWe4r","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t(ns yin.vm.debruijn.stack\n2\t  \"B3 + B4 (docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md, 'B3: de Bruijn VM kernel'\n3\t   and 'B4: effects and continuations'): the sibling stack VM that\n4\t   interprets RAW positional instruction vectors --\n5\t   `[:closure arity body-pc]`, `[:load-bound depth position]`,\n6\t   `[:load-free name]`, `[:const value]`, `[:call argc tail?]`, `[:return]`,\n7\t   `[:jump target]`, `[:branch-false target]`, `[:halt]`, `[:store-get key]`,\n8\t   `[:store-put key value]`, `[:gensym prefix]`, `[:stream-make buffer]`,\n9\t   `[:stream-put]`, `[:stream-cursor]`, `[:stream-next]`, `[:stream-close]`,\n10\t   `[:current-continuation]`, `[:park]`, `[:resume parked-id]`,\n11\t   `[:ffi-call op argc]`, `[:define name]` (Rule R's definition\n12\t   transition) -- whose shapes come from\n13\t   `yin.vm.code/vector-operand-table` and section 2 of the design doc.\n14\t\n15\t   B3 implemented the pure kernel: frames, closures, loads, calls, returns,\n16\t   branches, `:const`, and the store operations. B4 puts this machine on\n17\t   the shared scheduler seam `yin.vm.engine.md` states and section 4.1 of\n18\t   the design decides: the engine's bookkeeping keys replace `:status`;\n19\t   `run` is `engine/run-loop`; every blocking instruction parks a pure-data\n20\t   entry carrying this machine's register payload; one restore function,\n21\t   `stack-restore`, puts an entry back into the registers; and the FFI\n22\t   two-step lives in that restore, keyed on the entry keys `:request-sent`\n23\t   and `:call-id`, as the semantic VM places it.\n24\t\n25\t   The register payload of every parked record, wait entry, and reified\n26\t   continuation is\n27\t\n28\t       {:segment :pc :frames :stack :continuation :format :hash :image}\n29\t\n30\t   plus `:store-of` while a linked module's closure runs (yin.vm.linker.md\n31\t   section 7.3): its module store is the active store, a return frame\n32\t   saves the caller's, and a halt clears it. `:format` is\n33\t   `:yin.debruijn.code`, `:hash` is the held code space's H\n34\t   (`yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-hash`), and `:image` is the identity of\n35\t   the offset-table row the payload's pc falls in. `attach-image` extends\n36\t   the code space and the table (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3, r6), so a\n37\t   continuation is never interpreted by a VM or against an image other\n38\t   than one it holds: `stack-restore` refuses any entry whose model\n39\t   differs, or whose `:image` is no row of the table, with a qualified\n40\t   `:continuation-format` outcome. Cross-model transport is not a lift\n41\t   here and is deliberately unsupported (design section 4.1, item 4).\n42\t\n43\t   Every program used in this namespace's own tests is a hand-built\n44\t   instruction vector, the same technique B1's standalone validator tests\n45\t   use; B2 (`yin.vm.debruijn-linearize`) produces real images against this\n46\t   kernel from its own test namespace. This namespace requires\n47\t   `yin.vm.debruijn-code` for one thing only: computing H at load time.\n48\t\n49\t   B3's one accumulator-free obligation carries into every B4 opcode: there\n50\t   is no `val` register, so every value-producing instruction conjes its\n51\t   result straight onto `:stack`, and a `:push` is a pc advance.\"\n52\t  (:refer-clojure :exclude [eval])\n53\t  (:require [dao.stream.apply :as apply2]\n54\t            [yin.vm :as vm]\n55\t            [yin.vm.debruijn-code :as dcode]\n56\t            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]\n57\t            [yin.vm.ffi :as ffi]\n58\t            [yin.vm.module :as module]\n59\t            [yin.vm.values :as values]))\n60\t\n61\t\n62\t;; =============================================================================\n63\t;; State\n64\t;; =============================================================================\n65\t;; The record carries the design's explicit VM state (:segment :pc :frames\n66\t;; :free-env :stack :continuation :store), the engine's bookkeeping keys\n67\t;; (:blocked? :halted? :wait-set :ready-queue :parked :id-counter :value\n68\t;; :make-stream -- `yin.vm.engine.md` section 1), and the fixed composition\n69\t;; values :primitives and :modules that `:load-free` resolves through in\n70\t;; the same `env -> store -> primitives -> module registry` order\n71\t;; `yin.vm.engine/resolve-var` implements for the named VM. :hash is the\n72\t;; held code space's H and :images its offset table, which only the\n73\t;; loaders and `attach-image` write; :bridge is the explicit host-side FFI\n74\t;; bridge state `yin.vm.ffi` attaches.\n75\t\n76\t(defrecord DebruijnVM\n77\t  [segment      ; vector of instructions, pc-indexed; the whole program\n78\t   hash         ; H of `segment` (yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-hash)\n79\t   images       ; offset table: [[identity offset length] ...], base first\n80\t   pc           ; program counter into segment\n81\t   frames       ; positional frame stack, outermost first, innermost last\n82\t   free-env     ; initial free-name environment map, fixed for this instance\n83\t   stack        ; operand stack, a vector\n84\t   continuation ; vector of return frames (innermost last); see step-call\n85\t   store        ; heap map, keyed by whatever :store-get/:store-put use\n86\t   blocked?     ; engine: true while parked in the wait set\n87\t   halted?      ; engine: true when the active continuation has completed\n88\t   wait-set     ; engine: vector of pure-data entries waiting on a transport\n89\t   ready-queue  ; engine: vector of woken entries\n90\t   parked       ; engine: parked continuations map\n91\t   id-counter   ; engine: counter for gensym, stream, cursor and park ids\n92\t   value        ; engine: last computed value\n93\t   make-stream  ; host-supplied stream constructor, or nil\n94\t   bridge       ; explicit host-side FFI bridge state, or nil\n95\t   primitives   ; primitive registry, for :load-free\n96\t   modules      ; module registry, for :load-free and effect dispatch\n97\t   store-of     ; the running body's module store address, or nil\n98\t   resources    ; private engine resources: the link pair\n99\t   origin       ; this task's origin tag for link ids\n100\t   ancestry     ; the modules installing on this task's install chain\n101\t   gc])         ; {:since :base :threshold :pinned}: heap reclamation\n102\t\n103\t\n104\t(def format-tag\n105\t  \"The model tag every register payload carries as `:format`.\"\n106\t  :yin.debruijn.code)\n107\t\n108\t\n109\t(defn- install-image\n110\t  \"Install an admitted (or empty) `segment` as the one image, unchecked.\n111\t   The offset table restarts at the base row `[H 0 n]`.\"\n112\t  [vm segment]\n113\t  (let [h (dcode/image-hash segment)]\n114\t    (assoc vm\n115\t           :segment segment\n116\t           :hash h\n117\t           :images [[h 0 (count segment)]]\n118\t           :pc 0\n119\t           :frames []\n120\t           :stack []\n121\t           :continuation []\n122\t           :halted? (empty? segment)\n123\t           :blocked? false\n124\t           :value nil)))\n125\t\n126\t\n127\t(defn- admit!\n128\t  \"Admit a non-empty `segment` under `contract`: the stamp, then\n129\t   `yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-defect`, Rule R included.\"\n130\t  [segment contract]\n131\t  (when (seq segment)\n132\t    (vm/check-contract! vm/stack-contract contract)\n133\t    (when-let [defect (dcode/image-defect segment)]\n134\t      (throw (ex-info (str \"Invalid stack image: \" (:rule defect))\n135\t                      defect)))))\n136\t\n137\t\n138\t(defn load-image\n139\t  \"Load `segment` (a vector of instructions) into `vm` as its one image:\n140\t   H is recomputed, the registers are reset to pc 0 with empty frames,\n141\t   stack, and continuation, and the machine is running unless the segment\n142\t   is empty. The store, the parked map, the wait set, the ready queue,\n143\t   the id counter, and the composition values survive, exactly as they\n144\t   survive a `yin.vm.semantic/load-vector`. The offset table restarts at\n145\t   `[H 0 n]`, so a continuation parked under an earlier image names no row\n146\t   of it and `stack-restore` refuses it.\n147\t\n148\t   `contract` is required and compared with `vm/stack-contract` first\n149\t   (`:contract-missing`, `:contract-mismatch`); the image must then pass\n150\t   `yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-defect`, Rule R included. An empty segment\n151\t   admits no code and needs neither.\"\n152\t  [vm segment contract]\n153\t  (admit! segment contract)\n154\t  (install-image vm segment))\n155\t\n156\t\n157\t(defn relocate\n158\t  \"Shift every `:pc`-kind operand of `inst` by `offset`.\"\n159\t  [offset inst]\n160\t  (reduce (fn [inst [i [_ kind]]]\n161\t            (if (= :pc kind) (update inst (inc i) + offset) inst))\n162\t          inst\n163\t          (map-indexed vector (get dcode/opcode-table (nth inst 0)))))\n164\t\n165\t\n166\t(defn attach-image\n167\t  \"Extend the code space of `vm` with `image`, non-destructively\n168\t   (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3, act 3): `image` is admitted alone as\n169\t   `load-image` admits it, relocated by the held length, and appended to\n170\t   `:segment`; one `[identity offset length]` row, identity H of `image`,\n171\t   is appended to the offset table; `:hash` becomes H of the\n172\t   concatenation. `:pc`, `:stack`, `:frames`, `:continuation`, and every\n173\t   other register are unchanged: appending moves no instruction already\n174\t   held. An image whose identity is already a row is not attached twice,\n175\t   and an empty image, which adds no code, is not attached at all.\"\n176\t  [vm image contract]\n177\t  (admit! image contract)\n178\t  (let [ident (dcode/image-hash image)]\n179\t    (if (or (empty? image) (some #(= ident (nth % 0)) (:images vm)))\n180\t      vm\n181\t      (let [held (:segment vm)\n182\t            offset (count held)\n183\t            combined (into held (map #(relocate offset %)) image)]\n184\t        (assoc vm\n185\t               :segment combined\n186\t               :hash (dcode/image-hash combined)\n187\t               :images (conj (:images vm)\n188\t                             [ident offset (count image)]))))))\n189\t\n190\t\n191\t(defn row-at\n192\t  \"The offset-table row whose range holds `pc`; a pc one past the end of\n193\t   the last row (the pc after a final instruction) falls in that row.\"\n194\t  [images pc]\n195\t  (or (some (fn [[_ off len :as row]]\n196\t              (when (and (<= off pc) (< pc (+ off len))) row))\n197\t            images)\n198\t      (let [[_ off len :as row] (peek images)]\n199\t        (when (and row (= pc (+ off len))) row))))\n200\t\n201\t\n202\t;; =============================================================================\n203\t;; Layouts: the ordered image list a code space is built from (UCF v2, 4.1)\n204\t;; =============================================================================\n205\t\n206\t(def max-layout-length\n207\t  \"2^52-1: the largest length sum a layout may name.\"\n208\t  4503599627370495)\n209\t\n210\t\n211\t(defn unrelocate\n212\t  \"The inverse of `relocate`: shift every `:pc`-kind operand of `inst`\n213\t   down by `offset`.\"\n214\t  [offset inst]\n215\t  (relocate (- offset) inst))\n216\t\n217\t\n218\t(defn layout\n219\t  \"The ordered identities of `vm`'s offset table: its current layout.\"\n220\t  [vm]\n221\t  (mapv #(nth % 0) (:images vm)))\n222\t\n223\t\n224\t(defn compose\n225\t  \"The code space `images`, the component images in layout order,\n226\t   build: `{:segment :hash :images}` where `:segment` is the relocated\n227\t   concatenation, `:images` the offset table derived by prefix sums, and\n228\t   `:hash` the concatenation's H.  A length sum beyond 2^52-1 refuses\n229\t   with `:layout-overflow`; nothing here validates an image.\"\n230\t  [images]\n231\t  (let [{:keys [segment rows]}\n232\t        (reduce (fn [{:keys [segment rows]} image]\n233\t                  (let [offset (count segment)\n234\t                        total (+ offset (count image))]\n235\t                    (when (> total max-layout-length)\n236\t                      (throw (ex-info \"Layout length overflow\"\n237\t                                      {:rule :layout-overflow})))\n238\t                    {:segment (into segment (map #(relocate offset %)) image)\n239\t                     :rows (conj rows [(dcode/image-hash image) offset\n240\t                                       (count image)])}))\n241\t                {:segment [] :rows []}\n242\t                images)]\n243\t    {:segment segment :hash (dcode/image-hash segment) :images rows}))\n244\t\n245\t\n246\t(defn layout-images\n247\t  \"The component image of each row of the offset table `images` over\n248\t   `segment`, unrelocated, in table order; nil unless the table is\n249\t   contiguous from zero, covers the whole segment, and every slice\n250\t   rehashes to its row's identity.\"\n251\t  [segment images]\n252\t  (loop [rows (seq images), expected 0, out []]\n253\t    (if-let [[ident off len] (first rows)]\n254\t      (let [slice (when (and (= off expected) (<= (+ off len) (count segment)))\n255\t                    (mapv #(unrelocate off %) (subvec segment off (+ off len))))]\n256\t        (when (and slice (= ident (dcode/image-hash slice)))\n257\t          (recur (next rows) (+ off len) (conj out slice))))\n258\t      (when (= expected (count segment)) out))))\n259\t\n260\t\n261\t(defn rebuild\n262\t  \"`vm` over the code space `images` (the component images in layout\n263\t   order) build, as `load-image` would admit them: each non-empty image\n264\t   is admitted alone under the stack contract; the registers are reset\n265\t   and the machine stays whatever the caller makes it.  The store, the\n266\t   parked map, the queues and the composition values survive.\"\n267\t  [vm images]\n268\t  (doseq [image images]\n269\t    (admit! image vm/stack-contract))\n270\t  (let [{:keys [segment hash images]} (compose images)]\n271\t    (assoc vm\n272\t           :segment segment\n273\t           :hash hash\n274\t           :images images\n275\t           :pc 0\n276\t           :frames []\n277\t           :stack []\n278\t           :continuation []\n279\t           :store-of nil)))\n280\t\n281\t\n282\t(def boundary-ops\n283\t  \"The mnemonics whose successor may be a resume pc: `:call`,\n284\t   `:stream-put`, `:stream-next`, `:ffi-call`, `:current-continuation`\n285\t   and `:park`.\"\n286\t  #{:call :stream-put :stream-next :ffi-call :current-continuation :park})\n287\t\n288\t\n289\t(defn continuation-defect\n290\t  \"The first defect of a native stack payload, or nil: the format, the\n291\t   code space (an unrelocated slice per row, each rehashing to its\n292\t   identity and image-valid, the concatenation rehashing to `:hash`),\n293\t   `:image` equal to the row of the resume pc in its own table, a resume\n294\t   pc strictly inside the captured code space whose site (the pc before)\n295\t   is one of `sites`, and every return frame resuming strictly inside\n296\t   after a non-tail `:call`, with stack bases nondecreasing and no\n297\t   deeper than the saved operand stack.  Pure data: nothing runs.\"\n298\t  [payload sites]\n299\t  (let [{:keys [segment images pc stack frames continuation]} payload\n300\t        n (count segment)\n301\t        defect (fn [rule & kvs] (apply hash-map :rule rule kvs))]\n302\t    (cond\n303\t      (not= format-tag (:format payload)) (defect :continuation-format)\n304\t      (not (and (vector? segment) (vector? images)))\n305\t      (defect :continuation-segment)\n306\t\n307\t      :else\n308\t      (let [slices (layout-images segment images)]\n309\t        (cond\n310\t          (nil? slices) (defect :continuation-segment)\n311\t          (some (fn [slice]\n312\t                  (and (seq slice) (dcode/image-defect slice)))\n313\t                slices)\n314\t          (defect :continuation-segment)\n315\t          (not= (dcode/image-hash segment) (:hash payload))\n316\t          (defect :continuation-hash)\n317\t          (not (and (nat-int? pc) (< 0 pc n)))\n318\t          (defect :continuation-pc :pc pc)\n319\t          (not (contains? sites (nth (nth segment (dec pc)) 0)))\n320\t          (defect :continuation-site :pc pc)\n321\t          (not= (:image payload) (nth (row-at images pc) 0 nil))\n322\t          (defect :continuation-image)\n323\t          (not (and (vector? stack) (vector? frames)\n324\t                    (every? vector? frames) (vector? continuation)))\n325\t          (defect :continuation-shape)\n326\t          :else\n327\t          (let [r (reduce\n328\t                    (fn [base frame]\n329\t                      (let [rp (:return-pc frame)\n330\t                            b (:stack-base frame)\n331\t                            site (when (and (nat-int? rp) (< 0 rp n))\n332\t                                   (nth segment (dec rp)))]\n333\t                        (if (and (map? frame)\n334\t                                 (= :call (first site))\n335\t                                 (false? (nth site 2))\n336\t                                 (vector? (:frames frame))\n337\t                                 (every? vector? (:frames frame))\n338\t                                 (nat-int? b)\n339\t                                 (<= base b (count stack)))\n340\t                          b\n341\t                          (reduced (defect :return-frame :return-pc rp)))))\n342\t                    0\n343\t                    continuation)]\n344\t            (when (map? r) r)))))))\n345\t\n346\t\n347\t(defn image-pc\n348\t  \"Lift an absolute `pc` of `vm` to `[identity rel-pc]` by its offset\n349\t   table, or nil when no row holds it.\"\n350\t  [vm pc]\n351\t  (when-let [[ident off] (row-at (:images vm) pc)]\n352\t    [ident (- pc off)]))\n353\t\n354\t\n355\t(defn absolute-pc\n356\t  \"Lower `[identity rel-pc]` to an absolute pc of `vm` by its offset\n357\t   table, or nil when the identity is no row of it or `rel-pc` is no pc\n358\t   `image-pc` lifts into that row.\"\n359\t  [vm [ident rel-pc]]\n360\t  (let [images (:images vm)]\n361\t    (some (fn [[id off :as row]]\n362\t            (when (and (= id ident)\n363\t                       (nat-int? rel-pc)\n364\t                       (= row (row-at images (+ off rel-pc))))\n365\t              (+ off rel-pc)))\n366\t          images)))\n367\t\n368\t\n369\t(defn create-vm\n370\t  \"Build a fresh `DebruijnVM` over `segment` (a vector of instructions).\n371\t\n372\t   Options: `:free-env`, `:store`, `:primitives`, `:modules` (all default to\n373\t   `{}`, matching the named VM's empty defaults except that `:primitives`\n374\t   defaults to `{}` here whereas `yin.vm/empty-state` populates the full\n375\t   primitive table; callers pass `yin.vm/primitives` when they want the\n376\t   standard registry), `:make-stream` (the host's stream constructor; no\n377\t   for every v2 VM), `:call-in`/`:call-out`/`:call-out-cursor`/\n378\t   `:ffi-caller-id`/`:call-capacity` (the FFI pair,\n379\t   built by `yin.vm/empty-state` exactly as the semantic VM's is), and\n380\t   `:bridge` (host FFI handlers, attached by `yin.vm.ffi/attach`), and\n381\t   `:contract`, the segment's stamp, required when `segment` is non-empty\n382\t   (`load-image`). A `:free-env`, `:store`, or `:primitives` binding a\n383\t   reserved name is refused (Rule R). `:link-request`/`:link-response`\n384\t   (the link pair `require` lowers to), `:origin`, and `:ancestry` are\n385\t   `yin.vm/empty-state`'s.\n386\t\n387\t   An empty segment starts halted with an empty program, as the semantic\n388\t   VM's `create-vm` does; `load-image` loads work into it.\n389\t\n390\t   Always fresh: never resume or mutate a VM that has already run, per the\n391\t   design's D4 fixture restriction (the named VM's environment-leak defect\n392\t   is out of scope here and must not be reproduced by accident).\"\n393\t  ([segment] (create-vm segment {}))\n394\t  ([segment opts]\n395\t   (let [base (vm/empty-state\n396\t                (assoc (select-keys opts [:modules :make-stream :call-in\n397\t                                          :call-out :call-out-cursor\n398\t                                          :ffi-caller-id :call-capacity\n399\t                                          :link-request :link-response\n400\t                                          :origin :ancestry\n401\t                                          :capability-secret :secret-source\n402\t                                          :attach-stream :gc-threshold])\n403\t                       :primitives (or (:primitives opts) {})))]\n404\t     (-> (map->DebruijnVM\n405\t           {:segment [],\n406\t            :store-of nil,\n407\t            :resources (:resources base),\n408\t            :origin (:origin base),\n409\t            :ancestry (:ancestry base),\n410\t            :capability-secret (:capability-secret base),\n411\t            :owner (:owner base),\n412\t            :secret-source (:secret-source base),\n413\t            :attach-stream (:attach-stream base),\n414\t            :hash nil,\n415\t            :images [],\n416\t            :pc 0,\n417\t            :frames [],\n418\t            :free-env (vm/check-bindings! :env (or (:free-env opts) {})),\n419\t            :stack [],\n420\t            :continuation [],\n421\t            :store (merge (:store base)\n422\t                          (vm/check-bindings! :store (:store opts))),\n423\t            :blocked? false,\n424\t            :halted? true,\n425\t            :wait-set [],\n426\t            :ready-queue [],\n427\t            :parked {},\n428\t            :heap (:heap base),\n429\t            :gc (:gc base),\n430\t            :id-counter 0,\n431\t            :ffi-caller-id (:ffi-caller-id base),\n432\t            :value nil,\n433\t            :make-stream (:make-stream base),\n434\t            :bridge nil,\n435\t            :primitives (:primitives base),\n436\t            :callable-effects (:callable-effects base),\n437\t            :modules (or (:modules opts) {})})\n438\t         (load-image segment (:contract opts))\n439\t         (ffi/attach (:bridge opts))))))\n440\t\n441\t\n442\t;; =============================================================================\n443\t;; Frame addressing\n444\t;; =============================================================================\n445\t\n446\t(defn- frame-value\n447\t  \"Section 4: frame 0 for `:load-bound` is the INNERMOST frame, read from\n448\t   the END of `:frames` -- the opposite of a naive front-of-vector reading,\n449\t   and deliberate per the design. `frames` is outermost-first, so depth `d`\n450\t   is `(count frames) - 1 - d`.\"\n451\t  [frames depth position]\n452\t  (let [n (count frames)\n453\t        idx (- n 1 depth)]\n454\t    (when (or (neg? idx) (>= idx n))\n455\t      (throw (ex-info \"load-bound: depth out of range\"\n456\t                      {:rule :load-bound-depth, :depth depth,\n457\t                       :frame-count n})))\n458\t    (let [frame (nth frames idx)]\n459\t      (when (or (neg? position) (>= position (count frame)))\n460\t        (throw (ex-info \"load-bound: position out of range\"\n461\t                        {:rule :load-bound-position, :depth depth,\n462\t                         :position position, :frame-arity (count frame)})))\n463\t      (nth frame position))))\n464\t\n465\t\n466\t(defn- bind-positional\n467\t  \"The positional equivalent of the named `bind-params`\n468\t   (`yin.vm.engine/bind-params`, which zips params with args and nil-fills):\n469\t   `(vec (take arity (concat args (repeat nil))))`. Missing arguments are\n470\t   nil-filled; extra arguments are dropped. Not `bind-params` itself, which\n471\t   is name-keyed -- this is positional.\"\n472\t  [arity args]\n473\t  (vec (take arity (concat args (repeat nil)))))\n474\t\n475\t\n476\t;; =============================================================================\n477\t;; The engine seam: register payload, restore, builders\n478\t;; =============================================================================\n479\t\n480\t(defn- registers\n481\t  \"The register payload of the continuation after the current instruction\n482\t   (design section 4.1, item 3): `pc'` is the advanced pc and `stack'` the\n483\t   operand stack with the instruction's operands popped. Frames and the\n484\t   return-frame continuation are the running ones. `:format` stamps the\n485\t   model and `:image` the offset-table row `pc'` falls in, the identity its\n486\t   positions are relative to and the one `stack-restore` checks; `:hash`\n487\t   is the concatenation's H at the time, informative only. Free\n488\t   environment, store, primitives, and modules are not registers and stay\n489\t   on the state.\"\n490\t  [vm pc' stack']\n491\t  (cond-> {:segment (:segment vm),\n492\t           :pc pc',\n493\t           :frames (:frames vm),\n494\t           :stack stack',\n495\t           :continuation (:continuation vm),\n496\t           :format format-tag,\n497\t           :hash (:hash vm),\n498\t           :image (nth (row-at (:images vm) pc') 0 nil)}\n499\t    (:store-of vm) (assoc :store-of (:store-of vm))))\n500\t\n501\t\n502\t(defn- refuse-continuation!\n503\t  [base entry]\n504\t  (throw (ex-info \"Cannot restore a continuation of another model or image\"\n505\t                  {:reason :foreign-format,\n506\t                   :rule :continuation-format,\n507\t                   :format (:format entry),\n508\t                   :hash (:hash entry),\n509\t                   :image (:image entry),\n510\t                   :expected-format format-tag,\n511\t                   :expected-hash (:hash base),\n512\t                   :images (:images base)})))\n513\t\n514\t\n515\t(defn- return-transition\n516\t  \"Deliver a result through the caller's frame, or halt when K is empty.\"\n517\t  [vm val]\n518\t  (if-let [frame (peek (:continuation vm))]\n519\t    (assoc vm\n520\t           :pc (:return-pc frame)\n521\t           :frames (:frames frame)\n522\t           :stack (conj (subvec (:stack vm) 0 (:stack-base frame)) val)\n523\t           :continuation (pop (:continuation vm))\n524\t           :store-of (:store-of frame))\n525\t    (assoc vm :halted? true :stack [val] :value val :store-of nil)))\n526\t\n527\t\n528\t(defn stack-restore\n529\t  \"This machine's one restore function, `base entry val -> state`\n530\t   (`yin.vm.engine.md` section 2.1; design section 4.1, item 4). `base` is\n531\t   the state after the engine has done its part; `entry` is the wait, ready,\n532\t   or parked record; `val` is the value the parked instruction receives.\n533\t\n534\t   It first refuses with a qualified `:continuation-format` outcome unless\n535\t   the entry's `:format` is `:yin.debruijn.code` and its `:image` is a row\n536\t   of the offset table (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3, r6): a continuation\n537\t   from the named VM, from the register kernel, or from an image this\n538\t   kernel does not hold is never interpreted here. `:hash` is never a\n539\t   restore key: it changes at every `attach-image`, while the table only\n540\t   grows, so an entry parked before an attach still names its row.\n541\t\n542\t   Then the FFI two-step, on the entry keys the `yin.vm.ffi` convention\n543\t   owns, in the semantic VM's placement:\n544\t\n545\t   - A `:request-sent` entry is a writer whose retained request has now been\n546\t     appended. It must not resume: its registers are re-parked as the\n547\t     call-out reader (`ffi/response-wait-entry`) and the machine stays\n548\t     blocked.\n549\t   - A `:call-id` entry is a response reader. The woken value is a response\n550\t     envelope, so `ffi/call-result` unwraps it and checks correlation, and\n551\t     the parked call leaves `:parked` rather than accumulating.\n552\t\n553\t   A `:return-result` entry delivers through the return transition.\n554\t   Otherwise the registers are written from the entry and `val` is conjed\n555\t   onto the restored `:stack` -- the B3 obligation that every\n556\t   value-producing opcode lands on the stack, since there is no\n557\t   accumulator. `:segment` is never written from the entry: the code space\n558\t   is kernel state, and since appending moves nothing, the entry's\n559\t   absolute pcs and stack bases still name what they named.\"\n560\t  [base entry val]\n561\t  (when-not (and (= format-tag (:format entry))\n562\t                 (some #(= (:image entry) (nth % 0)) (:images base)))\n563\t    (refuse-continuation! base entry))\n564\t  (if (:request-sent entry)\n565\t    (-> base\n566\t        (update :wait-set (fnil conj [])\n567\t                (ffi/response-wait-entry entry (:call-id entry)))\n568\t        (assoc :value :yin/blocked\n569\t               :blocked? true\n570\t               :halted? false))\n571\t    (let [call-id (:call-id entry)\n572\t          base (if call-id (update base :parked dissoc call-id) base)\n573\t          val (if call-id (ffi/call-result val call-id) val)\n574\t          restored (assoc base\n575\t                          :pc (:pc entry)\n576\t                          :frames (:frames entry)\n577\t                          :continuation (:continuation entry)\n578\t                          :stack (vec (:stack entry))\n579\t                          :store-of (:store-of entry)\n580\t                          :value val\n581\t                          :halted? false)]\n582\t      (if (= :return-result (:resume-mode entry))\n583\t        (return-transition restored val)\n584\t        (assoc restored :stack (conj (:stack restored) val))))))\n585\t\n586\t\n587\t(defn- park-entry-fns\n588\t  \"Wait-entry builders for an instruction whose effect may block (design\n589\t   section 4.1, item 5): each closes over the post-instruction registers\n590\t   and returns them merged with `:reason` and the resource ids from the\n591\t   handler's result. Nothing else is attached: no handle, no closure, no\n592\t   restore function.\"\n593\t  [vm pc' stack']\n594\t  (let [site (nth (:segment vm) (:pc vm))\n595\t        regs (cond-> (registers vm pc' stack')\n596\t               (and (= :call (first site)) (true? (nth site 2)))\n597\t               (assoc :resume-mode :return-result))]\n598\t    {:stream/put (fn [_state _effect result]\n599\t                   (assoc regs\n600\t                          :reason :put\n601\t                          :stream-id (:stream-id result))),\n602\t     :stream/next (fn [_state _effect result]\n603\t                    (assoc regs\n604\t                           :reason :next\n605\t                           :cursor-ref (:cursor-ref result)\n606\t                           :stream-id (:stream-id result))),\n607\t     :module/require (fn [_state _effect _result] regs)}))\n608\t\n609\t\n610\t(defn- run-effect\n611\t  \"Dispatch one effect through the engine from the instruction at `(:pc vm)`\n612\t   with `stack'` its operand stack after popping the instruction's operands.\n613\t   On a value the machine continues at pc+1 with the value on the stack; on\n614\t   a park the engine has already placed the pure-data entry in the wait set\n615\t   and marked the machine blocked, and the registers mirror that entry.\"\n616\t  [vm effect stack']\n617\t  (let [pc' (inc (:pc vm))\n618\t        {:keys [state value blocked?]}\n619\t        (engine/handle-effect vm\n620\t                              effect\n621\t                              {:park-entry-fns (park-entry-fns vm pc' stack')})]\n622\t    (if blocked?\n623\t      (assoc state :pc pc' :stack stack')\n624\t      (assoc state :pc pc' :stack (conj stack' value)))))\n625\t\n626\t\n627\t(defn- ffi-call\n628\t  \"`[:ffi-call op argc]`: park-and-call over the FFI pair (the two-step of\n629\t   `yin.vm.engine.md` section 4). The continuation after the call is parked,\n630\t   its id is the call's correlation id, and the request is appended to the\n631\t   call-in stream. An `ok` append waits on the call-out cursor for the\n632\t   correlated response; a `full` append waits as a writer retrying the\n633\t   identical request, and `stack-restore` turns that writer into the\n634\t   response reader when it wakes.\"\n635\t  [vm op argc]\n636\t  (let [{:keys [pc stack resources]} vm\n637\t        total (count stack)\n638\t        args (subvec stack (- total argc))\n639\t        stack' (subvec stack 0 (- total argc))\n640\t        ;; The pair is checked before parking: an error raised after\n641\t        ;; park-continuation would strand a continuation in :parked and\n642\t        ;; consume an id counter.\n643\t        {:keys [call-in]} (ffi/require-call-pair! resources op)\n644\t        regs (registers vm (inc pc) stack')\n645\t        call-id (ffi/call-id vm (engine/park-id vm))\n646\t        ;; the request carries `args` out of the VM's view\n647\t        parked (engine/park-continuation (assoc (engine/pin-refs vm args)\n648\t                                                :pc (inc pc)\n649\t                                                :stack stack')\n650\t                                         regs\n651\t                                         call-id)\n652\t        request (apply2/request call-id op args)\n653\t        result (ffi/put-request vm call-in request)\n654\t        blocked (fn [entry]\n655\t                  (-> parked\n656\t                      (update :wait-set (fnil conj []) entry)\n657\t                      (assoc :value :yin/blocked\n658\t                             :blocked? true\n659\t                             :halted? false)))]\n660\t    (case (:dao.stream/outcome result)\n661\t      :dao.stream/ok (blocked (ffi/response-wait-entry regs call-id))\n662\t      :dao.stream/full (blocked (assoc regs\n663\t                                       :request-sent true\n664\t                                       :call-id call-id\n665\t                                       :op op\n666\t                                       :reason :put\n667\t                                       :stream-id vm/call-in-stream-key\n668\t                                       :datom request))\n669\t      (throw (ex-info \"FFI request could not be appended\"\n670\t                      {:op op,\n671\t                       :outcome (or (:dao.stream/outcome result)\n672\t                                    (:dao.stream.apply/outcome result))})))))\n673\t\n674\t\n675\t;; =============================================================================\n676\t;; The step function\n677\t;; =============================================================================\n678\t\n679\t(defn- step1\n680\t  \"Execute exactly one instruction and return the resulting VM. Assumes the\n681\t   VM's continuation is active; callers (`step`, `run`) check that first.\"\n682\t  [vm]\n683\t  (let [{:keys [segment pc frames free-env stack continuation\n684\t                primitives modules store-of]}\n685\t        vm\n686\t        inst (nth segment pc)\n687\t        op (nth inst 0)]\n688\t    (case op\n689\t      :const\n690\t      (assoc vm :pc (inc pc) :stack (conj stack (nth inst 1)))\n691\t\n692\t      :load-bound\n693\t      (let [depth (nth inst 1), position (nth inst 2)]\n694\t        (assoc vm\n695\t               :pc (inc pc)\n696\t               :stack (conj stack (frame-value frames depth position))))\n697\t\n698\t      :load-free\n699\t      (let [name (nth inst 1)\n700\t            value (engine/resolve-var free-env\n701\t                                      (engine/active-store vm store-of)\n702\t                                      primitives modules name)]\n703\t        (assoc vm :pc (inc pc) :stack (conj stack value)))\n704\t\n705\t      ;; This positional dimension has no `:macro?` flag on `:closure` (it\n706\t      ;; is named-datom-only, section 4): the instruction carries only\n707\t      ;; arity and a body pc, so there is nothing here to ignore.\n708\t      :closure\n709\t      (let [arity (nth inst 1), body-pc (nth inst 2)]\n710\t        (assoc vm\n711\t               :pc (inc pc)\n712\t               :stack (conj stack\n713\t                            (values/closure\n714\t                              (:owner vm)\n715\t                              (cond-> {:type :closure, :arity arity,\n716\t                                       :body-pc body-pc, :frames frames}\n717\t                                store-of (assoc :store-of store-of))))))\n718\t\n719\t      ;; The named VM (semantic.cljc) keeps a separate `val` accumulator\n720\t      ;; distinct from its operand stack `St`, so `:push` there commits\n721\t      ;; `val` onto `St` (St <- St ++ [val]). This machine has no such\n722\t      ;; split: every value-producing case already conjes its result\n723\t      ;; straight onto `:stack`. By the time control reaches a `:push`,\n724\t      ;; the value it would push is already there -- so here it is a no-op\n725\t      ;; that only advances `pc`. Every value-producing opcode below\n726\t      ;; (:stream-*, :gensym, :current-continuation, :resume, and the\n727\t      ;; restore after :ffi-call) honours this, or a `:push` immediately\n728\t      ;; after it would silently drop the value -- there is no `val`\n729\t      ;; register to recover it from.\n730\t      :push\n731\t      (assoc vm :pc (inc pc))\n732\t\n733\t      :call\n734\t      (let [argc (nth inst 1), tail? (nth inst 2)\n735\t            total (count stack)\n736\t            f-pos (- total argc 1)\n737\t            f (nth stack f-pos)\n738\t            args (subvec stack (inc f-pos) total)\n739\t            stack' (subvec stack 0 f-pos)]\n740\t        (case (engine/operator-kind vm f)\n741\t          :closure\n742\t          (let [c (values/payload f)\n743\t                locals (bind-positional (:arity c) args)\n744\t                body-frames (conj (:frames c) locals)\n745\t                continuation' (if tail?\n746\t                                continuation\n747\t                                (conj continuation\n748\t                                      (cond-> {:return-pc (inc pc),\n749\t                                               :frames frames,\n750\t                                               :stack-base (count stack')}\n751\t                                        store-of (assoc :store-of store-of))))]\n752\t            (assoc vm\n753\t                   :pc (:body-pc c)\n754\t                   :frames body-frames\n755\t                   :stack stack'\n756\t                   :continuation continuation'\n757\t                   :store-of (:store-of c)))\n758\t\n759\t          ;; A primitive host function, resolved by :load-free. The same\n760\t          ;; path the named engine's `apply-call` uses for a `fn?` callee:\n761\t          ;; a plain result lands on the stack; an effect descriptor (a\n762\t          ;; `require`, a `stream` module call) is dispatched\n763\t          ;; through the engine and may park with the continuation after\n764\t          ;; the call site.\n765\t          :host-fn\n766\t          (let [result (apply f args)]\n767\t            (if (module/effect? result)\n768\t              (run-effect (engine/check-callee-effect! vm f result)\n769\t                          result\n770\t                          stack')\n771\t              (assoc vm :pc (inc pc) :stack (conj stack' result))))\n772\t\n773\t          ;; A captured continuation: abortive, so the call site's stack,\n774\t          ;; frames and return frames are dropped and the captured\n775\t          ;; registers are restored with the argument on the stack, as a\n776\t          ;; resume would deliver it. The store is not a register.\n777\t          :continuation\n778\t          (stack-restore vm\n779\t                         (values/payload f)\n780\t                         (engine/continuation-argument args))))\n781\t\n782\t      :return\n783\t      (return-transition vm (peek stack))\n784\t\n785\t      :jump\n786\t      (assoc vm :pc (nth inst 1))\n787\t\n788\t      :branch-false\n789\t      (let [val (peek stack), stack' (pop stack)]\n790\t        (if val\n791\t          (assoc vm :pc (inc pc) :stack stack')\n792\t          (assoc vm :pc (nth inst 1) :stack stack')))\n793\t\n794\t      :halt\n795\t      (assoc vm :halted? true, :value (peek stack), :store-of nil)\n796\t\n797\t      ;; A module closure's store instructions route to its module store,\n798\t      ;; with no ambient fallback (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3, r7).\n799\t      :store-get\n800\t      (let [key (nth inst 1)]\n801\t        (assoc vm\n802\t               :pc (inc pc)\n803\t               :stack (conj stack (get (engine/active-store vm store-of)\n804\t                                       key))))\n805\t\n806\t      :store-put\n807\t      (let [key (nth inst 1), value (nth inst 2)]\n808\t        (assoc (engine/put-active vm store-of key value)\n809\t               :pc (inc pc)\n810\t               :stack (conj stack value)))\n811\t\n812\t      ;; :define -- the definition transition: pop the value, write it\n813\t      ;; under the literal name, and leave it as the expression's value.\n814\t      ;; The operator is never resolved (Rule R).\n815\t      :define\n816\t      (let [value (peek stack)]\n817\t        (assoc (engine/put-active vm store-of (nth inst 1) value)\n818\t               :pc (inc pc)\n819\t               :stack (conj (pop stack) value)))\n820\t\n821\t      ;; :gensym -- a fresh id; the engine's counter advances\n822\t      :gensym\n823\t      (let [[id vm'] (engine/gensym vm (nth inst 1))]\n824\t        (assoc vm' :pc (inc pc) :stack (conj stack id)))\n825\t\n826\t      ;; :stream-make -- the composition's :make-stream, through the engine\n827\t      :stream-make\n828\t      (run-effect vm\n829\t                  (module/make-effect :stream/make {:capacity (or (nth inst 1) vm/default-stream-capacity)})\n830\t                  stack)\n831\t\n832\t      ;; :stream-put -- the value on top, its target stream ref beneath\n833\t      ;; (`lower-stack` emits target, push, value, stream-put)\n834\t      :stream-put\n835\t      (let [val (peek stack), stack' (pop stack), target (peek stack')]\n836\t        (run-effect vm\n837\t                    (module/make-effect :stream/put {:stream target, :val val})\n838\t                    (pop stack')))\n839\t\n840\t      ;; :stream-cursor -- the source stream ref on top\n841\t      :stream-cursor\n842\t      (run-effect vm (module/make-effect :stream/cursor {:stream (peek stack)}) (pop stack))\n843\t\n844\t      ;; :stream-next -- the cursor ref on top; may park as a reader\n845\t      :stream-next\n846\t      (run-effect vm (module/make-effect :stream/next {:cursor (peek stack)}) (pop stack))\n847\t\n848\t      ;; :stream-close -- the source stream ref on top; yields nil\n849\t      :stream-close\n850\t      (run-effect vm (module/make-effect :stream/close {:stream (peek stack)}) (pop stack))\n851\t\n852\t      ;; :current-continuation -- the continuation after this instruction,\n853\t      ;; as a value: the register payload under the shared tag the B0\n854\t      ;; normalizer compares by type\n855\t      :current-continuation\n856\t      (assoc vm\n857\t             :pc (inc pc)\n858\t             :stack (conj stack\n859\t                          (values/continuation\n860\t                            (:owner vm)\n861\t                            (merge {:type :reified-continuation}\n862\t                                   (registers vm (inc pc) stack)))))\n863\t\n864\t      ;; :park -- the engine records the payload under a fresh parked id,\n865\t      ;; writes the record into :value, and halts the machine\n866\t      :park\n867\t      (engine/park-continuation (assoc vm :pc (inc pc))\n868\t                                (registers vm (inc pc) stack))\n869\t\n870\t      ;; :resume -- the value on top is delivered to the parked\n871\t      ;; continuation named by the operand, through this machine's restore\n872\t      :resume\n873\t      (let [val (peek stack)]\n874\t        (engine/resume-continuation (assoc vm :stack (pop stack))\n875\t                                    (nth inst 1)\n876\t                                    val\n877\t                                    stack-restore))\n878\t\n879\t      :ffi-call\n880\t      (ffi-call vm (nth inst 1) (nth inst 2))\n881\t\n882\t      (throw (ex-info (str \"Unknown opcode in segment: \" op)\n883\t                      {:rule :unknown-opcode, :op op, :pc pc})))))\n884\t\n885\t\n886\t;; =============================================================================\n887\t;; Scheduling\n888\t;; =============================================================================\n889\t\n890\t(defn- run-scheduler\n891\t  \"The raw runner: already-loaded work only, through the shared scheduler\n892\t   loop with this machine's step function and its restore bound into the\n893\t   engine's run-queue resumption. `ffi/maybe-run` wraps this for bridge\n894\t   dispatch.\"\n895\t  [vm]\n896\t  (engine/run-loop vm\n897\t                   engine/active-continuation?\n898\t                   step1\n899\t                   #(engine/resume-from-run-queue % stack-restore)))\n900\t\n901\t\n902\t;; =============================================================================\n903\t;; IVM / IVMState\n904\t;; =============================================================================\n905\t;; No new protocol methods (section 4, point 7). `environment` is not\n906\t;; implemented at all (point 8): the design defers it until a\n907\t;; frame-to-named lift exists, and returning the raw positional frame\n908\t;; vector from it would be wrong once the method does exist.\n909\t\n910\t(extend-type DebruijnVM\n911\t  vm/IVM\n912\t  (step [this]\n913\t    (cond (engine/active-continuation? this) (step1 this)\n914\t          ;; Between continuations: one scheduler round, as the semantic\n915\t          ;; VM's `step` runs when it has no control left.\n916\t          (or (:blocked? this) (seq (:ready-queue this)))\n917\t          (engine/scheduler-round this stack-restore)\n918\t          :else this))\n919\t  (run [this] (ffi/maybe-run this run-scheduler))\n920\t  (eval [_ ast]\n921\t    (throw (ex-info\n922\t             \"The de Bruijn kernel executes raw instruction vectors, not AST: lower and adapt it first (yin.vm.debruijn-linearize/adapt), then load and run the resulting image\"\n923\t             {:ast ast})))\n924\t  (reset [this]\n925\t    (assoc this\n926\t           :pc 0, :frames [], :stack [], :continuation []\n927\t           :halted? (empty? (:segment this)), :blocked? false, :value nil))\n928\t  (halted? [this] (engine/halted-with-empty-queue? this))\n929\t  (blocked? [this] (engine/vm-blocked? this))\n930\t  (value [this] (engine/vm-value this))\n931\t\n932\t  vm/IVMState\n933\t  (control [this] {:pc (:pc this)})\n934\t  (environment [_this]\n935\t    (throw (ex-info\n936\t             \"environment is not implemented: the design defers it until a frame-to-named lift exists\"\n937\t             {:rule :not-yet-supported})))\n938\t  (store [this] (:store this))\n939\t  (continuation [this] (:continuation this)))\n940\t\n941\t\n942\t;; =============================================================================\n943\t;; Module kernel (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3)\n944\t;; =============================================================================\n945\t;; A positional closure lifts by the offset table: the row holding its body\n946\t;; pc gives the origin identity and the image-relative entry, and its frames\n947\t;; encode value by value, outermost first. It lowers by the receiving\n948\t;; table's row for that identity, which act 3 attached.\n949\t\n950\t(defn- binding-mismatch!\n951\t  [marker]\n952\t  (throw (ex-info \"Closure marker of another binding discipline or format\"\n953\t                  {:reason :binding-mismatch,\n954\t                   :binding (:yin.k/binding marker),\n955\t                   :format (:yin.k/format marker),\n956\t                   :expected-format format-tag})))\n957\t\n958\t\n959\t(extend-type DebruijnVM\n960\t  module/IModuleKernel\n961\t  (link-format [_] {:format format-tag, :contract vm/stack-contract})\n962\t  (spawn-module\n963\t    [vm image {:keys [modules origin ancestry capability-secret]}]\n964\t    (create-vm image\n965\t               {:free-env (:free-env vm),\n966\t                :primitives (:primitives vm),\n967\t                :modules modules,\n968\t                :make-stream (:make-stream vm),\n969\t                :link-request (get (:resources vm)\n970\t                                   module/link-request-resource),\n971\t                :link-response (get (:resources vm)\n972\t                                    module/link-response-resource),\n973\t                :origin origin,\n974\t                :ancestry ancestry,\n975\t                :capability-secret capability-secret,\n976\t                :secret-source (:secret-source vm),\n977\t                :attach-stream (:attach-stream vm),\n978\t                :gc-threshold (:base (:gc vm)),\n979\t                :contract vm/stack-contract}))\n980\t  (image-identity [_ image] (dcode/image-hash image))\n981\t  (image-holds? [_ image segment] (= segment (dcode/image-hash image)))\n982\t  (attach-module [vm image] (attach-image vm image vm/stack-contract))\n983\t  (lift-closure [vm closure encode]\n984\t    (let [[ident rel] (or (image-pc vm (:body-pc closure))\n985\t                          (throw (ex-info \"Closure body in no held image\"\n986\t                                          {:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable,\n987\t                                           :yin.k/kind :unrooted-body})))]\n988\t      (cond-> {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/closure,\n989\t               :yin.k/binding :positional,\n990\t               :yin.k/format format-tag,\n991\t               :yin.k/segment ident,\n992\t               :yin.k/entry rel,\n993\t               :yin.k/arity (:arity closure),\n994\t               :yin.k/frames (mapv #(mapv encode %) (:frames closure))}\n995\t        (:store-of closure) (assoc :yin.k/store-of (:store-of closure)))))\n996\t  (lower-closure [vm marker decode]\n997\t    (when-not (and (= :positional (:yin.k/binding marker))\n998\t                   (= format-tag (:yin.k/format marker)))\n999\t      (binding-mismatch! marker))\n1000\t    (let [pc (absolute-pc vm [(:yin.k/segment marker) (:yin.k/entry marker)])]\n1001\t      (when (nil? pc)\n1002\t        (throw (ex-info \"Closure origin image is not attached\"\n1003\t                        {:reason :origin-not-attached,\n1004\t                         :segment (:yin.k/segment marker)})))\n1005\t      ;; the arity is that of a `:closure` of the attached code whose body\n1006\t      ;; this is, never the wire's\n1007\t      (when-not (some #(= [:closure (:yin.k/arity marker) pc] %)\n1008\t                      (:segment vm))\n1009\t        (engine/marker-mismatch! marker))\n1010\t      (values/closure\n1011\t        (:owner vm)\n1012\t        (cond-> {:type :closure,\n1013\t                 :arity (:yin.k/arity marker),\n1014\t                 :body-pc pc,\n1015\t                 :frames (mapv #(mapv decode %) (:yin.k/frames marker))}\n1016\t          (:yin.k/store-of marker)\n1017\t          (assoc :store-of (:yin.k/store-of marker))))))\n1018\t  (gc-roots [vm]\n1019\t    {:kernel [(:continuation vm)],\n1020\t     :values [(:frames vm) (:stack vm) (:value vm)]})\n1021\t  ;; A register payload (a wait, ready or parked entry, or a reified\n1022\t  ;; continuation) names the code space as `:segment`; that is code, so\n1023\t  ;; every other key is traced. Closures and return frames hold no code.\n1024\t  (gc-children [_ x]\n1025\t    (when (and (map? x) (= format-tag (:format x)))\n1026\t      {:values (into [] (vals (dissoc x :segment)))})))\n1027\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b921a1a6-65e4-4bc4-853c-fc87c8b912fe","uuid":"3c648597-9e71-4684-9f83-d7cfa88b9c70","timestamp":"2026-10-12T17:42:28.083Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6c/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc","content":"(ns yin.vm.debruijn.stack\n  \"B3 + B4 (docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md, 'B3: de Bruijn VM kernel'\n   and 'B4: effects and continuations'): the sibling stack VM that\n   interprets RAW positional instruction vectors --\n   `[:closure arity body-pc]`, `[:load-bound depth position]`,\n   `[:load-free name]`, `[:const value]`, `[:call argc tail?]`, `[:return]`,\n   `[:jump target]`, `[:branch-false target]`, `[:halt]`, `[:store-get key]`,\n   `[:store-put key value]`, `[:gensym prefix]`, `[:stream-make buffer]`,\n   `[:stream-put]`, `[:stream-cursor]`, `[:stream-next]`, `[:stream-close]`,\n   `[:current-continuation]`, `[:park]`, `[:resume parked-id]`,\n   `[:ffi-call op argc]`, `[:define name]` (Rule R's definition\n   transition) -- whose shapes come from\n   `yin.vm.code/vector-operand-table` and section 2 of the design doc.\n\n   B3 implemented the pure kernel: frames, closures, loads, calls, returns,\n   branches, `:const`, and the store operations. B4 puts this machine on\n   the shared scheduler seam `yin.vm.engine.md` states and section 4.1 of\n   the design decides: the engine's bookkeeping keys replace `:status`;\n   `run` is `engine/run-loop`; every blocking instruction parks a pure-data\n   entry carrying this machine's register payload; one restore function,\n   `stack-restore`, puts an entry back into the registers; and the FFI\n   two-step lives in that restore, keyed on the entry keys `:request-sent`\n   and `:call-id`, as the semantic VM places it.\n\n   The register payload of every parked record, wait entry, and reified\n   continuation is\n\n       {:segment :pc :frames :stack :continuation :format :hash :image}\n\n   plus `:store-of` while a linked module's closure runs (yin.vm.linker.md\n   section 7.3): its module store is the active store, a return frame\n   saves the caller's, and a halt clears it. `:format` is\n   `:yin.debruijn.code`, `:hash` is the held code space's H\n   (`yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-hash`), and `:image` is the identity of\n   the offset-table row the payload's pc falls in. `attach-image` extends\n   the code space and the table (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3, r6), so a\n   continuation is never interpreted by a VM or against an image other\n   than one it holds: `stack-restore` refuses any entry whose model\n   differs, or whose `:image` is no row of the table, with a qualified\n   `:continuation-format` outcome. Cross-model transport is not a lift\n   here and is deliberately unsupported (design section 4.1, item 4).\n\n   Every program used in this namespace's own tests is a hand-built\n   instruction vector, the same technique B1's standalone validator tests\n   use; B2 (`yin.vm.debruijn-linearize`) produces real images against this\n   kernel from its own test namespace. This namespace requires\n   `yin.vm.debruijn-code` for one thing only: computing H at load time.\n\n   B3's one accumulator-free obligation carries into every B4 opcode: there\n   is no `val` register, so every value-producing instruction conjes its\n   result straight onto `:stack`, and a `:push` is a pc advance.\"\n  (:refer-clojure :exclude [eval])\n  (:require [dao.stream.apply :as apply2]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn-code :as dcode]\n            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]\n            [yin.vm.ffi :as ffi]\n            [yin.vm.module :as module]\n            [yin.vm.values :as values]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; State\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The record carries the design's explicit VM state (:segment :pc :frames\n;; :free-env :stack :continuation :store), the engine's bookkeeping keys\n;; (:blocked? :halted? :wait-set :ready-queue :parked :id-counter :value\n;; :make-stream -- `yin.vm.engine.md` section 1), and the fixed composition\n;; values :primitives and :modules that `:load-free` resolves through in\n;; the same `env -> store -> primitives -> module registry` order\n;; `yin.vm.engine/resolve-var` implements for the named VM. :hash is the\n;; held code space's H and :images its offset table, which only the\n;; loaders and `attach-image` write; :bridge is the explicit host-side FFI\n;; bridge state `yin.vm.ffi` attaches.\n\n(defrecord DebruijnVM\n  [segment      ; vector of instructions, pc-indexed; the whole program\n   hash         ; H of `segment` (yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-hash)\n   images       ; offset table: [[identity offset length] ...], base first\n   pc           ; program counter into segment\n   frames       ; positional frame stack, outermost first, innermost last\n   free-env     ; initial free-name environment map, fixed for this instance\n   stack        ; operand stack, a vector\n   continuation ; vector of return frames (innermost last); see step-call\n   store        ; heap map, keyed by whatever :store-get/:store-put use\n   blocked?     ; engine: true while parked in the wait set\n   halted?      ; engine: true when the active continuation has completed\n   wait-set     ; engine: vector of pure-data entries waiting on a transport\n   ready-queue  ; engine: vector of woken entries\n   parked       ; engine: parked continuations map\n   id-counter   ; engine: counter for gensym, stream, cursor and park ids\n   value        ; engine: last computed value\n   make-stream  ; host-supplied stream constructor, or nil\n   bridge       ; explicit host-side FFI bridge state, or nil\n   primitives   ; primitive registry, for :load-free\n   modules      ; module registry, for :load-free and effect dispatch\n   store-of     ; the running body's module store address, or nil\n   resources    ; private engine resources: the link pair\n   origin       ; this task's origin tag for link ids\n   ancestry     ; the modules installing on this task's install chain\n   gc])         ; {:since :base :threshold :pinned}: heap reclamation\n\n\n(def format-tag\n  \"The model tag every register payload carries as `:format`.\"\n  :yin.debruijn.code)\n\n\n(defn- install-image\n  \"Install an admitted (or empty) `segment` as the one image, unchecked.\n   The offset table restarts at the base row `[H 0 n]`.\"\n  [vm segment]\n  (let [h (dcode/image-hash segment)]\n    (assoc vm\n           :segment segment\n           :hash h\n           :images [[h 0 (count segment)]]\n           :pc 0\n           :frames []\n           :stack []\n           :continuation []\n           :halted? (empty? segment)\n           :blocked? false\n           :value nil)))\n\n\n(defn- admit!\n  \"Admit a non-empty `segment` under `contract`: the stamp, then\n   `yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-defect`, Rule R included.\"\n  [segment contract]\n  (when (seq segment)\n    (vm/check-contract! vm/stack-contract contract)\n    (when-let [defect (dcode/image-defect segment)]\n      (throw (ex-info (str \"Invalid stack image: \" (:rule defect))\n                      defect)))))\n\n\n(defn load-image\n  \"Load `segment` (a vector of instructions) into `vm` as its one image:\n   H is recomputed, the registers are reset to pc 0 with empty frames,\n   stack, and continuation, and the machine is running unless the segment\n   is empty. The store, the parked map, the wait set, the ready queue,\n   the id counter, and the composition values survive, exactly as they\n   survive a `yin.vm.semantic/load-vector`. The offset table restarts at\n   `[H 0 n]`, so a continuation parked under an earlier image names no row\n   of it and `stack-restore` refuses it.\n\n   `contract` is required and compared with `vm/stack-contract` first\n   (`:contract-missing`, `:contract-mismatch`); the image must then pass\n   `yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-defect`, Rule R included. An empty segment\n   admits no code and needs neither.\"\n  [vm segment contract]\n  (admit! segment contract)\n  (install-image vm segment))\n\n\n(defn relocate\n  \"Shift every `:pc`-kind operand of `inst` by `offset`.\"\n  [offset inst]\n  (reduce (fn [inst [i [_ kind]]]\n            (if (= :pc kind) (update inst (inc i) + offset) inst))\n          inst\n          (map-indexed vector (get dcode/opcode-table (nth inst 0)))))\n\n\n(defn attach-image\n  \"Extend the code space of `vm` with `image`, non-destructively\n   (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3, act 3): `image` is admitted alone as\n   `load-image` admits it, relocated by the held length, and appended to\n   `:segment`; one `[identity offset length]` row, identity H of `image`,\n   is appended to the offset table; `:hash` becomes H of the\n   concatenation. `:pc`, `:stack`, `:frames`, `:continuation`, and every\n   other register are unchanged: appending moves no instruction already\n   held. An image whose identity is already a row is not attached twice,\n   and an empty image, which adds no code, is not attached at all.\"\n  [vm image contract]\n  (admit! image contract)\n  (let [ident (dcode/image-hash image)]\n    (if (or (empty? image) (some #(= ident (nth % 0)) (:images vm)))\n      vm\n      (let [held (:segment vm)\n            offset (count held)\n            combined (into held (map #(relocate offset %)) image)]\n        (assoc vm\n               :segment combined\n               :hash (dcode/image-hash combined)\n               :images (conj (:images vm)\n                             [ident offset (count image)]))))))\n\n\n(defn row-at\n  \"The offset-table row whose range holds `pc`; a pc one past the end of\n   the last row (the pc after a final instruction) falls in that row.\"\n  [images pc]\n  (or (some (fn [[_ off len :as row]]\n              (when (and (<= off pc) (< pc (+ off len))) row))\n            images)\n      (let [[_ off len :as row] (peek images)]\n        (when (and row (= pc (+ off len))) row))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Layouts: the ordered image list a code space is built from (UCF v2, 4.1)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def max-layout-length\n  \"2^52-1: the largest length sum a layout may name.\"\n  4503599627370495)\n\n\n(defn unrelocate\n  \"The inverse of `relocate`: shift every `:pc`-kind operand of `inst`\n   down by `offset`.\"\n  [offset inst]\n  (relocate (- offset) inst))\n\n\n(defn layout\n  \"The ordered identities of `vm`'s offset table: its current layout.\"\n  [vm]\n  (mapv #(nth % 0) (:images vm)))\n\n\n(defn compose\n  \"The code space `images`, the component images in layout order,\n   build: `{:segment :hash :images}` where `:segment` is the relocated\n   concatenation, `:images` the offset table derived by prefix sums, and\n   `:hash` the concatenation's H.  A length sum beyond 2^52-1 refuses\n   with `:layout-overflow`; nothing here validates an image.\"\n  [images]\n  (let [{:keys [segment rows]}\n        (reduce (fn [{:keys [segment rows]} image]\n                  (let [offset (count segment)\n                        total (+ offset (count image))]\n                    (when (> total max-layout-length)\n                      (throw (ex-info \"Layout length overflow\"\n                                      {:rule :layout-overflow})))\n                    {:segment (into segment (map #(relocate offset %)) image)\n                     :rows (conj rows [(dcode/image-hash image) offset\n                                       (count image)])}))\n                {:segment [] :rows []}\n                images)]\n    {:segment segment :hash (dcode/image-hash segment) :images rows}))\n\n\n(defn layout-images\n  \"The component image of each row of the offset table `images` over\n   `segment`, unrelocated, in table order; nil unless the table is\n   contiguous from zero, covers the whole segment, and every slice\n   rehashes to its row's identity.\"\n  [segment images]\n  (loop [rows (seq images), expected 0, out []]\n    (if-let [[ident off len] (first rows)]\n      (let [slice (when (and (= off expected) (<= (+ off len) (count segment)))\n                    (mapv #(unrelocate off %) (subvec segment off (+ off len))))]\n        (when (and slice (= ident (dcode/image-hash slice)))\n          (recur (next rows) (+ off len) (conj out slice))))\n      (when (= expected (count segment)) out))))\n\n\n(defn rebuild\n  \"`vm` over the code space `images` (the component images in layout\n   order) build, as `load-image` would admit them: each non-empty image\n   is admitted alone under the stack contract; the registers are reset\n   and the machine stays whatever the caller makes it.  The store, the\n   parked map, the queues and the composition values survive.\"\n  [vm images]\n  (doseq [image images]\n    (admit! image vm/stack-contract))\n  (let [{:keys [segment hash images]} (compose images)]\n    (assoc vm\n           :segment segment\n           :hash hash\n           :images images\n           :pc 0\n           :frames []\n           :stack []\n           :continuation []\n           :store-of nil)))\n\n\n(def boundary-ops\n  \"The mnemonics whose successor may be a resume pc: `:call`,\n   `:stream-put`, `:stream-next`, `:ffi-call`, `:current-continuation`\n   and `:park`.\"\n  #{:call :stream-put :stream-next :ffi-call :current-continuation :park})\n\n\n(defn continuation-defect\n  \"The first defect of a native stack payload, or nil: the format, the\n   code space (an unrelocated slice per row, each rehashing to its\n   identity and image-valid, the concatenation rehashing to `:hash`),\n   `:image` equal to the row of the resume pc in its own table, a resume\n   pc strictly inside the captured code space whose site (the pc before)\n   is one of `sites`, and every return frame resuming strictly inside\n   after a non-tail `:call`, with stack bases nondecreasing and no\n   deeper than the saved operand stack.  Pure data: nothing runs.\"\n  [payload sites]\n  (let [{:keys [segment images pc stack frames continuation]} payload\n        n (count segment)\n        defect (fn [rule & kvs] (apply hash-map :rule rule kvs))]\n    (cond\n      (not= format-tag (:format payload)) (defect :continuation-format)\n      (not (and (vector? segment) (vector? images)))\n      (defect :continuation-segment)\n\n      :else\n      (let [slices (layout-images segment images)]\n        (cond\n          (nil? slices) (defect :continuation-segment)\n          (some (fn [slice]\n                  (and (seq slice) (dcode/image-defect slice)))\n                slices)\n          (defect :continuation-segment)\n          (not= (dcode/image-hash segment) (:hash payload))\n          (defect :continuation-hash)\n          (not (and (nat-int? pc) (< 0 pc n)))\n          (defect :continuation-pc :pc pc)\n          (not (contains? sites (nth (nth segment (dec pc)) 0)))\n          (defect :continuation-site :pc pc)\n          (not= (:image payload) (nth (row-at images pc) 0 nil))\n          (defect :continuation-image)\n          (not (and (vector? stack) (vector? frames)\n                    (every? vector? frames) (vector? continuation)))\n          (defect :continuation-shape)\n          :else\n          (let [r (reduce\n                    (fn [base frame]\n                      (let [rp (:return-pc frame)\n                            b (:stack-base frame)\n                            site (when (and (nat-int? rp) (< 0 rp n))\n                                   (nth segment (dec rp)))]\n                        (if (and (map? frame)\n                                 (= :call (first site))\n                                 (false? (nth site 2))\n                                 (vector? (:frames frame))\n                                 (every? vector? (:frames frame))\n                                 (nat-int? b)\n                                 (<= base b (count stack)))\n                          b\n                          (reduced (defect :return-frame :return-pc rp)))))\n                    0\n                    continuation)]\n            (when (map? r) r)))))))\n\n\n(defn image-pc\n  \"Lift an absolute `pc` of `vm` to `[identity rel-pc]` by its offset\n   table, or nil when no row holds it.\"\n  [vm pc]\n  (when-let [[ident off] (row-at (:images vm) pc)]\n    [ident (- pc off)]))\n\n\n(defn absolute-pc\n  \"Lower `[identity rel-pc]` to an absolute pc of `vm` by its offset\n   table, or nil when the identity is no row of it or `rel-pc` is no pc\n   `image-pc` lifts into that row.\"\n  [vm [ident rel-pc]]\n  (let [images (:images vm)]\n    (some (fn [[id off :as row]]\n            (when (and (= id ident)\n                       (nat-int? rel-pc)\n                       (= row (row-at images (+ off rel-pc))))\n              (+ off rel-pc)))\n          images)))\n\n\n(defn create-vm\n  \"Build a fresh `DebruijnVM` over `segment` (a vector of instructions).\n\n   Options: `:free-env`, `:store`, `:primitives`, `:modules` (all default to\n   `{}`, matching the named VM's empty defaults except that `:primitives`\n   defaults to `{}` here whereas `yin.vm/empty-state` populates the full\n   primitive table; callers pass `yin.vm/primitives` when they want the\n   standard registry), `:make-stream` (the host's stream constructor; no\n   for every v2 VM), `:call-in`/`:call-out`/`:call-out-cursor`/\n   `:ffi-caller-id`/`:call-capacity` (the FFI pair,\n   built by `yin.vm/empty-state` exactly as the semantic VM's is), and\n   `:bridge` (host FFI handlers, attached by `yin.vm.ffi/attach`), and\n   `:contract`, the segment's stamp, required when `segment` is non-empty\n   (`load-image`). A `:free-env`, `:store`, or `:primitives` binding a\n   reserved name is refused (Rule R). `:link-request`/`:link-response`\n   (the link pair `require` lowers to), `:origin`, and `:ancestry` are\n   `yin.vm/empty-state`'s.\n\n   An empty segment starts halted with an empty program, as the semantic\n   VM's `create-vm` does; `load-image` loads work into it.\n\n   Always fresh: never resume or mutate a VM that has already run, per the\n   design's D4 fixture restriction (the named VM's environment-leak defect\n   is out of scope here and must not be reproduced by accident).\"\n  ([segment] (create-vm segment {}))\n  ([segment opts]\n   (let [base (vm/empty-state\n                (assoc (select-keys opts [:modules :make-stream :call-in\n                                          :call-out :call-out-cursor\n                                          :ffi-caller-id :call-capacity\n                                          :link-request :link-response\n                                          :origin :ancestry\n                                          :capability-secret :secret-source\n                                          :attach-stream :gc-threshold])\n                       :primitives (or (:primitives opts) {})))]\n     (-> (map->DebruijnVM\n           {:segment [],\n            :store-of nil,\n            :resources (:resources base),\n            :origin (:origin base),\n            :ancestry (:ancestry base),\n            :capability-secret (:capability-secret base),\n            :owner (:owner base),\n            :secret-source (:secret-source base),\n            :attach-stream (:attach-stream base),\n            :hash nil,\n            :images [],\n            :pc 0,\n            :frames [],\n            :free-env (vm/check-bindings! :env (or (:free-env opts) {})),\n            :stack [],\n            :continuation [],\n            :store (merge (:store base)\n                          (vm/check-bindings! :store (:store opts))),\n            :blocked? false,\n            :halted? true,\n            :wait-set [],\n            :ready-queue [],\n            :parked {},\n            :heap (:heap base),\n            :gc (:gc base),\n            :id-counter 0,\n            :ffi-caller-id (:ffi-caller-id base),\n            :value nil,\n            :make-stream (:make-stream base),\n            :bridge nil,\n            :primitives (:primitives base),\n            :callable-effects (:callable-effects base),\n            :modules (or (:modules opts) {})})\n         (load-image segment (:contract opts))\n         (ffi/attach (:bridge opts))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Frame addressing\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- frame-value\n  \"Section 4: frame 0 for `:load-bound` is the INNERMOST frame, read from\n   the END of `:frames` -- the opposite of a naive front-of-vector reading,\n   and deliberate per the design. `frames` is outermost-first, so depth `d`\n   is `(count frames) - 1 - d`.\"\n  [frames depth position]\n  (let [n (count frames)\n        idx (- n 1 depth)]\n    (when (or (neg? idx) (>= idx n))\n      (throw (ex-info \"load-bound: depth out of range\"\n                      {:rule :load-bound-depth, :depth depth,\n                       :frame-count n})))\n    (let [frame (nth frames idx)]\n      (when (or (neg? position) (>= position (count frame)))\n        (throw (ex-info \"load-bound: position out of range\"\n                        {:rule :load-bound-position, :depth depth,\n                         :position position, :frame-arity (count frame)})))\n      (nth frame position))))\n\n\n(defn- bind-positional\n  \"The positional equivalent of the named `bind-params`\n   (`yin.vm.engine/bind-params`, which zips params with args and nil-fills):\n   `(vec (take arity (concat args (repeat nil))))`. Missing arguments are\n   nil-filled; extra arguments are dropped. Not `bind-params` itself, which\n   is name-keyed -- this is positional.\"\n  [arity args]\n  (vec (take arity (concat args (repeat nil)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The engine seam: register payload, restore, builders\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- registers\n  \"The register payload of the continuation after the current instruction\n   (design section 4.1, item 3): `pc'` is the advanced pc and `stack'` the\n   operand stack with the instruction's operands popped. Frames and the\n   return-frame continuation are the running ones. `:format` stamps the\n   model and `:image` the offset-table row `pc'` falls in, the identity its\n   positions are relative to and the one `stack-restore` checks; `:hash`\n   is the concatenation's H at the time, informative only. Free\n   environment, store, primitives, and modules are not registers and stay\n   on the state.\"\n  [vm pc' stack']\n  (cond-> {:segment (:segment vm),\n           :pc pc',\n           :frames (:frames vm),\n           :stack stack',\n           :continuation (:continuation vm),\n           :format format-tag,\n           :hash (:hash vm),\n           :image (nth (row-at (:images vm) pc') 0 nil)}\n    (:store-of vm) (assoc :store-of (:store-of vm))))\n\n\n(defn- refuse-continuation!\n  [base entry]\n  (throw (ex-info \"Cannot restore a continuation of another model or image\"\n                  {:reason :foreign-format,\n                   :rule :continuation-format,\n                   :format (:format entry),\n                   :hash (:hash entry),\n                   :image (:image entry),\n                   :expected-format format-tag,\n                   :expected-hash (:hash base),\n                   :images (:images base)})))\n\n\n(defn- return-transition\n  \"Deliver a result through the caller's frame, or halt when K is empty.\"\n  [vm val]\n  (if-let [frame (peek (:continuation vm))]\n    (assoc vm\n           :pc (:return-pc frame)\n           :frames (:frames frame)\n           :stack (conj (subvec (:stack vm) 0 (:stack-base frame)) val)\n           :continuation (pop (:continuation vm))\n           :store-of (:store-of frame))\n    (assoc vm :halted? true :stack [val] :value val :store-of nil)))\n\n\n(defn stack-restore\n  \"This machine's one restore function, `base entry val -> state`\n   (`yin.vm.engine.md` section 2.1; design section 4.1, item 4). `base` is\n   the state after the engine has done its part; `entry` is the wait, ready,\n   or parked record; `val` is the value the parked instruction receives.\n\n   It first refuses with a qualified `:continuation-format` outcome unless\n   the entry's `:format` is `:yin.debruijn.code` and its `:image` is a row\n   of the offset table (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3, r6): a continuation\n   from the named VM, from the register kernel, or from an image this\n   kernel does not hold is never interpreted here. `:hash` is never a\n   restore key: it changes at every `attach-image`, while the table only\n   grows, so an entry parked before an attach still names its row.\n\n   Then the FFI two-step, on the entry keys the `yin.vm.ffi` convention\n   owns, in the semantic VM's placement:\n\n   - A `:request-sent` entry is a writer whose retained request has now been\n     appended. It must not resume: its registers are re-parked as the\n     call-out reader (`ffi/response-wait-entry`) and the machine stays\n     blocked.\n   - A `:call-id` entry is a response reader. The woken value is a response\n     envelope, so `ffi/call-result` unwraps it and checks correlation, and\n     the parked call leaves `:parked` rather than accumulating.\n\n   A `:return-result` entry delivers through the return transition.\n   Otherwise the registers are written from the entry and `val` is conjed\n   onto the restored `:stack` -- the B3 obligation that every\n   value-producing opcode lands on the stack, since there is no\n   accumulator. `:segment` is never written from the entry: the code space\n   is kernel state, and since appending moves nothing, the entry's\n   absolute pcs and stack bases still name what they named.\"\n  [base entry val]\n  (when-not (and (= format-tag (:format entry))\n                 (some #(= (:image entry) (nth % 0)) (:images base)))\n    (refuse-continuation! base entry))\n  (if (:request-sent entry)\n    (-> base\n        (update :wait-set (fnil conj [])\n                (ffi/response-wait-entry entry (:call-id entry)))\n        (assoc :value :yin/blocked\n               :blocked? true\n               :halted? false))\n    (let [call-id (:call-id entry)\n          base (if call-id (update base :parked dissoc call-id) base)\n          val (if call-id (ffi/call-result val call-id) val)\n          restored (assoc base\n                          :pc (:pc entry)\n                          :frames (:frames entry)\n                          :continuation (:continuation entry)\n                          :stack (vec (:stack entry))\n                          :store-of (:store-of entry)\n                          :value val\n                          :halted? false)]\n      (if (= :return-result (:resume-mode entry))\n        (return-transition restored val)\n        (assoc restored :stack (conj (:stack restored) val))))))\n\n\n(defn- park-entry-fns\n  \"Wait-entry builders for an instruction whose effect may block (design\n   section 4.1, item 5): each closes over the post-instruction registers\n   and returns them merged with `:reason` and the resource ids from the\n   handler's result. Nothing else is attached: no handle, no closure, no\n   restore function.\"\n  [vm pc' stack']\n  (let [site (nth (:segment vm) (:pc vm))\n        regs (cond-> (registers vm pc' stack')\n               (and (= :call (first site)) (true? (nth site 2)))\n               (assoc :resume-mode :return-result))]\n    {:stream/put (fn [_state _effect result]\n                   (assoc regs\n                          :reason :put\n                          :stream-id (:stream-id result))),\n     :stream/next (fn [_state _effect result]\n                    (assoc regs\n                           :reason :next\n                           :cursor-ref (:cursor-ref result)\n                           :stream-id (:stream-id result))),\n     :module/require (fn [_state _effect _result] regs)}))\n\n\n(defn- run-effect\n  \"Dispatch one effect through the engine from the instruction at `(:pc vm)`\n   with `stack'` its operand stack after popping the instruction's operands.\n   On a value the machine continues at pc+1 with the value on the stack; on\n   a park the engine has already placed the pure-data entry in the wait set\n   and marked the machine blocked, and the registers mirror that entry.\"\n  [vm effect stack']\n  (let [pc' (inc (:pc vm))\n        {:keys [state value blocked?]}\n        (engine/handle-effect vm\n                              effect\n                              {:park-entry-fns (park-entry-fns vm pc' stack')})]\n    (if blocked?\n      (assoc state :pc pc' :stack stack')\n      (assoc state :pc pc' :stack (conj stack' value)))))\n\n\n(defn- ffi-call\n  \"`[:ffi-call op argc]`: park-and-call over the FFI pair (the two-step of\n   `yin.vm.engine.md` section 4). The continuation after the call is parked,\n   its id is the call's correlation id, and the request is appended to the\n   call-in stream. An `ok` append waits on the call-out cursor for the\n   correlated response; a `full` append waits as a writer retrying the\n   identical request, and `stack-restore` turns that writer into the\n   response reader when it wakes.\"\n  [vm op argc]\n  (let [{:keys [pc stack resources]} vm\n        total (count stack)\n        args (subvec stack (- total argc))\n        stack' (subvec stack 0 (- total argc))\n        ;; The pair is checked before parking: an error raised after\n        ;; park-continuation would strand a continuation in :parked and\n        ;; consume an id counter.\n        {:keys [call-in]} (ffi/require-call-pair! resources op)\n        regs (registers vm (inc pc) stack')\n        call-id (ffi/call-id vm (engine/park-id vm))\n        ;; the request carries `args` out of the VM's view\n        parked (engine/park-continuation (assoc (engine/pin-refs vm args)\n                                                :pc (inc pc)\n                                                :stack stack')\n                                         regs\n                                         call-id)\n        request (apply2/request call-id op args)\n        result (ffi/put-request vm call-in request)\n        blocked (fn [entry]\n                  (-> parked\n                      (update :wait-set (fnil conj []) entry)\n                      (assoc :value :yin/blocked\n                             :blocked? true\n                             :halted? false)))]\n    (case (:dao.stream/outcome result)\n      :dao.stream/ok (blocked (ffi/response-wait-entry regs call-id))\n      :dao.stream/full (blocked (assoc regs\n                                       :request-sent true\n                                       :call-id call-id\n                                       :op op\n                                       :reason :put\n                                       :stream-id vm/call-in-stream-key\n                                       :datom request))\n      (throw (ex-info \"FFI request could not be appended\"\n                      {:op op,\n                       :outcome (or (:dao.stream/outcome result)\n                                    (:dao.stream.apply/outcome result))})))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The step function\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- step1\n  \"Execute exactly one instruction and return the resulting VM. Assumes the\n   VM's continuation is active; callers (`step`, `run`) check that first.\"\n  [vm]\n  (let [{:keys [segment pc frames free-env stack continuation\n                primitives modules store-of]}\n        vm\n        inst (nth segment pc)\n        op (nth inst 0)]\n    (case op\n      :const\n      (assoc vm :pc (inc pc) :stack (conj stack (nth inst 1)))\n\n      :load-bound\n      (let [depth (nth inst 1), position (nth inst 2)]\n        (assoc vm\n               :pc (inc pc)\n               :stack (conj stack (frame-value frames depth position))))\n\n      :load-free\n      (let [name (nth inst 1)\n            value (engine/resolve-var free-env\n                                      (engine/active-store vm store-of)\n                                      primitives modules name)]\n        (assoc vm :pc (inc pc) :stack (conj stack value)))\n\n      ;; This positional dimension has no `:macro?` flag on `:closure` (it\n      ;; is named-datom-only, section 4): the instruction carries only\n      ;; arity and a body pc, so there is nothing here to ignore.\n      :closure\n      (let [arity (nth inst 1), body-pc (nth inst 2)]\n        (assoc vm\n               :pc (inc pc)\n               :stack (conj stack\n                            (values/closure\n                              (:owner vm)\n                              (cond-> {:type :closure, :arity arity,\n                                       :body-pc body-pc, :frames frames}\n                                store-of (assoc :store-of store-of))))))\n\n      ;; The named VM (semantic.cljc) keeps a separate `val` accumulator\n      ;; distinct from its operand stack `St`, so `:push` there commits\n      ;; `val` onto `St` (St <- St ++ [val]). This machine has no such\n      ;; split: every value-producing case already conjes its result\n      ;; straight onto `:stack`. By the time control reaches a `:push`,\n      ;; the value it would push is already there -- so here it is a no-op\n      ;; that only advances `pc`. Every value-producing opcode below\n      ;; (:stream-*, :gensym, :current-continuation, :resume, and the\n      ;; restore after :ffi-call) honours this, or a `:push` immediately\n      ;; after it would silently drop the value -- there is no `val`\n      ;; register to recover it from.\n      :push\n      (assoc vm :pc (inc pc))\n\n      :call\n      (let [argc (nth inst 1), tail? (nth inst 2)\n            total (count stack)\n            f-pos (- total argc 1)\n            f (nth stack f-pos)\n            args (subvec stack (inc f-pos) total)\n            stack' (subvec stack 0 f-pos)]\n        (case (engine/operator-kind vm f)\n          :closure\n          (let [c (values/payload f)\n                locals (bind-positional (:arity c) args)\n                body-frames (conj (:frames c) locals)\n                continuation' (if tail?\n                                continuation\n                                (conj continuation\n                                      (cond-> {:return-pc (inc pc),\n                                               :frames frames,\n                                               :stack-base (count stack')}\n                                        store-of (assoc :store-of store-of))))]\n            (assoc vm\n                   :pc (:body-pc c)\n                   :frames body-frames\n                   :stack stack'\n                   :continuation continuation'\n                   :store-of (:store-of c)))\n\n          ;; A primitive host function, resolved by :load-free. The same\n          ;; path the named engine's `apply-call` uses for a `fn?` callee:\n          ;; a plain result lands on the stack; an effect descriptor (a\n          ;; `require`, a `stream` module call) is dispatched\n          ;; through the engine and may park with the continuation after\n          ;; the call site.\n          :host-fn\n          (let [result (apply f args)]\n            (if (module/effect? result)\n              (run-effect (engine/check-callee-effect! vm f result)\n                          result\n                          stack')\n              (assoc vm :pc (inc pc) :stack (conj stack' result))))\n\n          ;; A captured continuation: abortive, so the call site's stack,\n          ;; frames and return frames are dropped and the captured\n          ;; registers are restored with the argument on the stack, as a\n          ;; resume would deliver it. The store is not a register.\n          :continuation\n          (stack-restore vm\n                         (values/payload f)\n                         (engine/continuation-argument args))))\n\n      :return\n      (return-transition vm (peek stack))\n\n      :jump\n      (assoc vm :pc (nth inst 1))\n\n      :branch-false\n      (let [val (peek stack), stack' (pop stack)]\n        (if val\n          (assoc vm :pc (inc pc) :stack stack')\n          (assoc vm :pc (nth inst 1) :stack stack')))\n\n      :halt\n      (assoc vm :halted? true, :value (peek stack), :store-of nil)\n\n      ;; A module closure's store instructions route to its module store,\n      ;; with no ambient fallback (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3, r7).\n      :store-get\n      (let [key (nth inst 1)]\n        (assoc vm\n               :pc (inc pc)\n               :stack (conj stack (get (engine/active-store vm store-of)\n                                       key))))\n\n      :store-put\n      (let [key (nth inst 1), value (nth inst 2)]\n        (assoc (engine/put-active vm store-of key value)\n               :pc (inc pc)\n               :stack (conj stack value)))\n\n      ;; :define -- the definition transition: pop the value, write it\n      ;; under the literal name, and leave it as the expression's value.\n      ;; The operator is never resolved (Rule R).\n      :define\n      (let [value (peek stack)]\n        (assoc (engine/put-active vm store-of (nth inst 1) value)\n               :pc (inc pc)\n               :stack (conj (pop stack) value)))\n\n      ;; :gensym -- a fresh id; the engine's counter advances\n      :gensym\n      (let [[id vm'] (engine/gensym vm (nth inst 1))]\n        (assoc vm' :pc (inc pc) :stack (conj stack id)))\n\n      ;; :stream-make -- the composition's :make-stream, through the engine\n      :stream-make\n      (run-effect vm\n                  (module/make-effect :stream/make {:capacity (or (nth inst 1) vm/default-stream-capacity)})\n                  stack)\n\n      ;; :stream-put -- the value on top, its target stream ref beneath\n      ;; (`lower-stack` emits target, push, value, stream-put)\n      :stream-put\n      (let [val (peek stack), stack' (pop stack), target (peek stack')]\n        (run-effect vm\n                    (module/make-effect :stream/put {:stream target, :val val})\n                    (pop stack')))\n\n      ;; :stream-cursor -- the source stream ref on top\n      :stream-cursor\n      (run-effect vm (module/make-effect :stream/cursor {:stream (peek stack)}) (pop stack))\n\n      ;; :stream-next -- the cursor ref on top; may park as a reader\n      :stream-next\n      (run-effect vm (module/make-effect :stream/next {:cursor (peek stack)}) (pop stack))\n\n      ;; :stream-close -- the source stream ref on top; yields nil\n      :stream-close\n      (run-effect vm (module/make-effect :stream/close {:stream (peek stack)}) (pop stack))\n\n      ;; :current-continuation -- the continuation after this instruction,\n      ;; as a value: the register payload under the shared tag the B0\n      ;; normalizer compares by type\n      :current-continuation\n      (assoc vm\n             :pc (inc pc)\n             :stack (conj stack\n                          (values/continuation\n                            (:owner vm)\n                            (merge {:type :reified-continuation}\n                                   (registers vm (inc pc) stack)))))\n\n      ;; :park -- the engine records the payload under a fresh parked id,\n      ;; writes the record into :value, and halts the machine\n      :park\n      (engine/park-continuation (assoc vm :pc (inc pc))\n                                (registers vm (inc pc) stack))\n\n      ;; :resume -- the value on top is delivered to the parked\n      ;; continuation named by the operand, through this machine's restore\n      :resume\n      (let [val (peek stack)]\n        (engine/resume-continuation (assoc vm :stack (pop stack))\n                                    (nth inst 1)\n                                    val\n                                    stack-restore))\n\n      :ffi-call\n      (ffi-call vm (nth inst 1) (nth inst 2))\n\n      (throw (ex-info (str \"Unknown opcode in segment: \" op)\n                      {:rule :unknown-opcode, :op op, :pc pc})))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Scheduling\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- run-scheduler\n  \"The raw runner: already-loaded work only, through the shared scheduler\n   loop with this machine's step function and its restore bound into the\n   engine's run-queue resumption. `ffi/maybe-run` wraps this for bridge\n   dispatch.\"\n  [vm]\n  (engine/run-loop vm\n                   engine/active-continuation?\n                   step1\n                   #(engine/resume-from-run-queue % stack-restore)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; IVM / IVMState\n;; =============================================================================\n;; No new protocol methods (section 4, point 7). `environment` is not\n;; implemented at all (point 8): the design defers it until a\n;; frame-to-named lift exists, and returning the raw positional frame\n;; vector from it would be wrong once the method does exist.\n\n(extend-type DebruijnVM\n  vm/IVM\n  (step [this]\n    (cond (engine/active-continuation? this) (step1 this)\n          ;; Between continuations: one scheduler round, as the semantic\n          ;; VM's `step` runs when it has no control left.\n          (or (:blocked? this) (seq (:ready-queue this)))\n          (engine/scheduler-round this stack-restore)\n          :else this))\n  (run [this] (ffi/maybe-run this run-scheduler))\n  (eval [_ ast]\n    (throw (ex-info\n             \"The de Bruijn kernel executes raw instruction vectors, not AST: lower and adapt it first (yin.vm.debruijn-linearize/adapt), then load and run the resulting image\"\n             {:ast ast})))\n  (reset [this]\n    (assoc this\n           :pc 0, :frames [], :stack [], :continuation []\n           :halted? (empty? (:segment this)), :blocked? false, :value nil))\n  (halted? [this] (engine/halted-with-empty-queue? this))\n  (blocked? [this] (engine/vm-blocked? this))\n  (value [this] (engine/vm-value this))\n\n  vm/IVMState\n  (control [this] {:pc (:pc this)})\n  (environment [_this]\n    (throw (ex-info\n             \"environment is not implemented: the design defers it until a frame-to-named lift exists\"\n             {:rule :not-yet-supported})))\n  (store [this] (:store this))\n  (continuation [this] (:continuation this)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Module kernel (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3)\n;; =============================================================================\n;; A positional closure lifts by the offset table: the row holding its body\n;; pc gives the origin identity and the image-relative entry, and its frames\n;; encode value by value, outermost first. It lowers by the receiving\n;; table's row for that identity, which act 3 attached.\n\n(defn- binding-mismatch!\n  [marker]\n  (throw (ex-info \"Closure marker of another binding discipline or format\"\n                  {:reason :binding-mismatch,\n                   :binding (:yin.k/binding marker),\n                   :format (:yin.k/format marker),\n                   :expected-format format-tag})))\n\n\n(extend-type DebruijnVM\n  module/IModuleKernel\n  (link-format [_] {:format format-tag, :contract vm/stack-contract})\n  (spawn-module\n    [vm image {:keys [modules origin ancestry capability-secret]}]\n    (create-vm image\n               {:free-env (:free-env vm),\n                :primitives (:primitives vm),\n                :modules modules,\n                :make-stream (:make-stream vm),\n                :link-request (get (:resources vm)\n                                   module/link-request-resource),\n                :link-response (get (:resources vm)\n                                    module/link-response-resource),\n                :origin origin,\n                :ancestry ancestry,\n                :capability-secret capability-secret,\n                :secret-source (:secret-source vm),\n                :attach-stream (:attach-stream vm),\n                :gc-threshold (:base (:gc vm)),\n                :contract vm/stack-contract}))\n  (image-identity [_ image] (dcode/image-hash image))\n  (image-holds? [_ image segment] (= segment (dcode/image-hash image)))\n  (attach-module [vm image] (attach-image vm image vm/stack-contract))\n  (lift-closure [vm closure encode]\n    (let [[ident rel] (or (image-pc vm (:body-pc closure))\n                          (throw (ex-info \"Closure body in no held image\"\n                                          {:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable,\n                                           :yin.k/kind :unrooted-body})))]\n      (cond-> {:yin.k/tag :yin.k/closure,\n               :yin.k/binding :positional,\n               :yin.k/format format-tag,\n               :yin.k/segment ident,\n               :yin.k/entry rel,\n               :yin.k/arity (:arity closure),\n               :yin.k/frames (mapv #(mapv encode %) (:frames closure))}\n        (:store-of closure) (assoc :yin.k/store-of (:store-of closure)))))\n  (lower-closure [vm marker decode]\n    (when-not (and (= :positional (:yin.k/binding marker))\n                   (= format-tag (:yin.k/format marker)))\n      (binding-mismatch! marker))\n    (let [pc (absolute-pc vm [(:yin.k/segment marker) (:yin.k/entry marker)])]\n      (when (nil? pc)\n        (throw (ex-info \"Closure origin image is not attached\"\n                        {:reason :origin-not-attached,\n                         :segment (:yin.k/segment marker)})))\n      ;; the arity is that of a `:closure` of the attached code whose body\n      ;; this is, never the wire's\n      (when-not (some #(= [:closure (:yin.k/arity marker) pc] %)\n                      (:segment vm))\n        (engine/marker-mismatch! marker))\n      (values/closure\n        (:owner vm)\n        (cond-> {:type :closure,\n                 :arity (:yin.k/arity marker),\n                 :body-pc pc,\n                 :frames (mapv #(mapv decode %) (:yin.k/frames marker))}\n          (:yin.k/store-of marker)\n          (assoc :store-of (:yin.k/store-of marker))))))\n  (gc-roots [vm]\n    {:kernel [(:continuation vm)],\n     :values [(:frames vm) (:stack vm) (:value vm)]})\n  ;; A register payload (a wait, ready or parked entry, or a reified\n  ;; continuation) names the code space as `:segment`; that is code, so\n  ;; every other key is traced. Closures and return frames hold no code.\n  (gc-children [_ x]\n    (when (and (map? x) (= format-tag (:format x)))\n      {:values (into [] (vals (dissoc x :segment)))})))\n","numLines":1027,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1027}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015jWXGfp1D9xGeZZzFaWe4r","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
