kernel diverged, law 4 would fail rather than pass silently.\n- **`canonical-task` copies `:capability-secret` from the kernel.** `kernel-task` keeps the receiving kernel's secret, so refs verify only when the scratch receiver and the kernel share a composition. The harness guarantees this; a production caller must as well.\n- **Space lookups go through `code-index`.** A program that differs from another only in binder names derives the same H, and would be refused as ambiguous rather than mis-resolved. This was not hit.\n"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0127Yd1LWVBUSLiD1FuHP8PV","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6c/collab/1792340000000-engineer-6c-layouts.report.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b921a1a6-65e4-4bc4-853c-fc87c8b912fe","uuid":"18d2bc4b-3dad-46f9-9f28-c6b1f5e7cb91","timestamp":"2026-10-12T18:13:48.425Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6c/collab/1792340000000-engineer-6c-layouts.report.md","content":"# Slice 6c proper: layouts and cross-engine lanes (engineer report, opus)\n\nBranch `srvm-phase6c`, worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p6c`. **Not committed.**\n\n## Step 0: prerequisites\n\nAll present on this branch, so no stop was needed:\n- 5a: `8df1d117`, merged at `71b4f4d7`.\n- 5b: `derive/register-image`, `b2eadc83`, merged at `7517f30e`.\n- 5c: the `scan` namespace, merged at `e5df547a`.\n- 6b: `rows/lift-task` and `rows/lower-task`, merged at `1db08f7d`.\n- 6c-pre: the stack `:resume-mode :return-result` seam and `stack_tail_completion_test.cljc`, merged at `296f75fd`.\n\n## Files\n\n| File | Change | Lines |\n|---|---|---|\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc` | new | 898 |\n| `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout_test.cljc` | new | 425 |\n| `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` | modified | +392 / -13 |\n\nNo kernel, no design doc and no `rows.cljc` changed. `git diff --quiet` is clean on `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn`, `semantic_register.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf` and `docs`.\n\n### `layout.cljc` (namespace `yin.vm.semantic-register.layout`)\n\n**Static half.** `segment v` derives R(A) and H(A) and computes:\n- `:pairs`, the `[pc virtual physical]` operand pairing;\n- `:phys`, per body;\n- the correspondence `:a->h` and `:h->a`;\n- `:layouts`, which is `stack-layout`: the ancestors' pushed earlier children of `:call`, `:ffi-call` and `:stream-put`.\n\n`space` indexes segments by A and by R and H. R and H are checksums only: when two A derive one image, the lookup refuses `:ambiguous-image`.\n\nOther public functions:\n- `phys` and `virt`. `virt` is the restricted inverse over `live-out`; see design defect D3.\n- `live-out`, which is `live-out*`.\n- `register-live`, computed as `(sort (map phys (disj live-out* rd)))`.\n- `chain`.\n- `env-of`, the forward rule of §4.4, and `frames-of`, its inverse.\n- `slices`, implementing `St[b(i-1), b(i))` plus the current activation.\n\n**Dynamic half.**\n- `lift-state cx entry` produces the canonical sr-shaped payload: `{:segment A :pc :env :window :k :deliver}` or `{:segment A :site :k :deliver}`, with engine fields carried.\n  - Register lane: `rd` comes from A, at the top frame and in every K frame.\n  - Stack lane: K slices, and the canonical pc is `h->a(pcₕ−1)+1`.\n  - Values are converted recursively: a positional closure becomes canonical by the forward rule, and a kernel continuation becomes canonical by `lift-state`.\n- `lower-state lx p [fill]` is the inverse.\n  - Register lane: `:live = sort(phys(keys W))`.\n  - Stack lane: bases are cumulative through each slice.\n  - A tail lowers with `:frames []` and `:store-of nil`, or with `fill`.\n- A pc whose predecessor is not a parking instruction, or a `:push` in H, is refused `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint`.\n\n**Task level.**\n- `canonical-task k space template`: a quiescent de Bruijn kernel presented as a semantic-register machine, so that `rows/lift-task` lifts it unchanged. A running kernel is refused not-at-safepoint; `:installs` is refused unsupported.\n- `kernel-task m k`: a machine restored by `rows/lower-task` becomes a fresh kernel. Every A image is derived and attached, `:free-env` is set, and waits, parked records and values are lowered.\n- `lift-task` and `lower-task` compose these with the row carrier.\n- `lower-defect` runs **before any attach** and refuses two things:\n  - install children: `:yin.k/unsupported :installs`;\n  - for the register kernel, a primitive function anywhere in the payload data of a wait or parked state: `:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers`, with a path. This covers windows and lexical bindings, including those reached through collections, closures' lexical frames and captures.\n\n### Harness additions\n\n- `*lane*` (`:semantic-register`, `:stack` or `:register`). `machine` reads it; `load-program` dispatches on the machine's link format. A de Bruijn load attaches the derived image beside what the kernel holds, so parked records keep naming their rows.\n- `code-index`, `kernel-space`, and `lane-closure` (row 18's module closure on any lane).\n- New tests, all in the harness file:\n  - `cross-engine-canonical-equality` (`^:slow` plus `slow/guard`; full matrix) and `cross-engine-canonical-equality-bare` (quick tier);\n  - `cross-engine-lowering` (`^:slow`; full matrix) and `cross-engine-lowering-bare` (quick tier);\n  - `layout-refusals`.\n- The 6b tests are unchanged in behaviour, since `*lane*` defaults to the reference lane.\n\n## Tests and counts (JVM only, all run in the foreground)\n\n- **kondo: BLOCKED.** Both `clojure -M:kondo --lint …` and `clj-kondo --lint …` were refused by the permission gate, so kondo was not run.\n- **Quick tier on the new and touched namespaces** (`-e :slow -n layout-test -n safepoint-harness-test`): 31 tests, 3162 assertions, 0 failures.\n- **Slow tier on the same namespaces** (`-i :slow`): 3 tests, 2411 assertions, 0 failures. These are 6b's `every-row-every-segment…` and the two new full matrices.\n- **Every `yin.vm.ucf.*`, `yin.vm.semantic-register.*` and `yin.vm.debruijn.*` namespace, quick tier** (`-e :slow -r 'yin\\.vm\\.(ucf|semantic-register|debruijn)\\..*'`): 947 tests, 22248 assertions, 0 failures, about 2m20s.\n  - This ran before I added the `:tail-operand` fixture to layout-test's C (see M4). layout-test was rerun clean afterwards, inside the 31-test run above.\n- The slow tier of that wider set was not run.\n\n**Counts asserted:**\n\n| Test | Counts |\n|---|---|\n| law 4, bare | 16 cases |\n| law 4, full | 61 cases (15 lane rows × 4 segments + row 20); every case compares 3 lanes |\n| law 5 | 6 ordered pairs |\n| law 5, bare | 92 pair runs: 16 × 6, less row 4's 4 |\n| law 5, full | 350 pair runs: 61 × 6, less 4 × 4 for row 4 |\n| item 3 | 6 K frames at `:depth-3` |\n| item 6 | bases `[0 0 0 2 4]`, slices `[] [] [] 2 2 2`, stack length 6, 5 lifted K frames, lowered bases `[0 0 0 2 4]`, lowered stack length 6, and the run continues to `[1 [2 [3 \"B\"]]]`; the slice partition is checked at more than 20 safepoints |\n| item 7 | 128 E comparisons: (61 + 3) × 2 lanes |\n| item 9 | 4: rows 6 and 20 × 2 lanes |\n| items 1, 2, 4, 5 | every program of C is checked |\n\n**Acceptance status:**\n- Items 1 to 9 are in `layout_test` under exactly the names the brief gives.\n- Items 10 to 12 are in the harness.\n- Item 13 (bb test:sub on three hosts) was not run: the brief said JVM only.\n\n## Mutation results\n\nEach mutant was applied to a backed-up copy and run against the targeted tests. After each one I restored the file, checked it with `diff` against the backup, and confirmed `git diff --quiet` on the kernels and docs. The backups were then deleted.\n\n| # | Mutant | Caught by |\n|---|---|---|\n| M1 | register `rd` taken from the physical `:dest` (top frame and K frames) | `register-rd-from-a` (both clauses); law 4 and law 5 (the lift refuses `:inconsistent-state`); `layout-refusals` |\n| M2 | `live` including a dead register (`rd` not subtracted) | `register-live-is-live-out`: 56 failures |\n| M3 | stack bases by the before-slice rule (`0 0 0 0 2`) | `stack-slices-partition` (the fixture pins `[0 0 0 2 4]`); the slow `cross-engine-lowering` errors. **The quick `cross-engine-lowering-bare` does not catch it**, because bare rows have only empty K slices. |\n| M4 | an extra window register at a tail boundary (`live-out` treating a tail call as falling through) | **Not caught at first**: in every corpus program the instruction after a tail call reads only the call's own `rd`, so the mutant was equivalent. I added `:tail-operand`, `(f (g x)ᵗᵃⁱˡ y)` from phase-5's last golden, to layout-test's C; `register-live-is-live-out` then catches it. |\n| M5 | E reconstruction dropping a shadowing binding (merge order reversed) | `env-reconstruction` (40 failures); law 4 |\n| M6 | closure round trip losing a binder | `closures-round-trip` (16 failures); law 5 errors |\n| M7 | a cross-engine pair skipped | `cross-engine-lowering-bare` pair counts: 6, `(if (= 4 row) 2 6)` and 92 |\n| M8 | a layout refusal after attach (`lower-defect` moved after `rows/lower-task`) | `layout-refusals`: \"refused before any attachment\" |\n\n## Deviations, with reasons\n\n1. **Design API names.** The design lists `register-layout`, `lift-act`, `lift-tail`, `lower-act` and `lower-tail`. The public API is instead one dispatching function each way, `lift-state` and `lower-state`. The act and tail algorithms are separate inside. The register layout is the segment's `:phys`, `:pairs`, `phys` and `virt`.\n2. **Lowering into a de Bruijn kernel** is `rows/lower-task` into a scratch semantic-register machine of the same composition (the carrier's steps 1 to 12), then `kernel-task`. It is not a separate restore. The layout's own refusals (`lower-defect`) run before step 11. The layout requires `yin.vm.ucf.rows` for this composition.\n3. **Link and install rows (7 to 12, 17) are not on the de Bruijn lanes.** De Bruijn kernels link native images (H and R) while module responses carry A, and §4 defines no layout for install children. So law 4 and law 5 run 15 of the 22 wrapped rows, plus row 20. For the same reason, item 9 runs rows 6 and 20 only; rows 9 and 12 are link rows.\n4. **Row 4 is not lowered into a de Bruijn receiver**, which removes 4 of its 6 pairs. I verified this is necessary: without the exclusion, all 4 pairs stay `:yin/blocked` (see D2).\n5. **The static corpus C** is the B0 corpus, the phase-3 corpus, and `:tail-operand`. derive-test's C cannot be required: derive-test pulls in the yang.python ANTLR lane.\n6. **Items 7 and 8 use variants of the named B0 and B2 programs.** As written, `:duplicate-param`, `:nested-closure`, `:nested-lambdas`, \"closure value\" and \"higher order\" reach no safepoint, so each variant puts a read in the innermost body; \"closure value\" and `:duplicate-param` are also checked as halted values.\n   - For the duplicate binder, positional → canonical → positional gives `[2 2]` for `[1 2]`. The test pins this: per §4.4, the shadowed position is not a claim.\n   - canonical → positional → canonical is the identity.\n7. **`virt` fallback.** For a slot outside `live-out*`'s physical image, `virt` uses the body's unique preimage, so that item 12's \"tampered upward\" case reaches `:window-extra`. If that preimage is ambiguous it refuses `:register-layout`. See D3.\n8. **New status and kind:** `:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers`, for a register receiver of a carrier with a `:prim` in payload data.\n\n## Design defects found (for the Architect; no design text edited)\n\n- **D1, §5.5 `canonical-under`.** The procedure assumes cursor positions are identity-free. A `dao.stream.ringbuffer` cursor embeds its stream's identity (`{:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity … :position n}`). Marker substitution therefore leaves lane-specific identities in every cursor-cell body, and law 4 fails across lanes on every row with a cursor.\n  - The harness renames position identities under the same fixture correspondence (`logical-positions`) before `canonical-under`.\n  - Proposal: have `canonical-under` rename positions too, or state that positions carry identities.\n- **D2, retained FFI on the de Bruijn kernels.** Their `:request-sent` re-park builds `ffi/response-wait-entry`, which names the composition's own call-out pair. The semantic-register kernel's `register-restore` honours the carried `:response-cursor` and `:response-stream`. So a carried row 4 never wakes on a de Bruijn receiver.\n  - Needs a kernel seam in both de Bruijn kernels, like 6c-pre.\n  - Phase 6 does not edit kernels, so it is excluded and reported here.\n- **D3, §4.2.** `virt` is a restricted inverse, defined only on `live-out*`'s physical image. That leaves item 12's \"live tampered upward\" with no reconstruction. The design should state which rule applies; the implemented rule is the unique preimage, else refuse.\n- **D4, register kernel and primitives.** A primitive function is a legal carrier value (`:prim`), and the reference lane runs programs that hold one across a read. The register kernel's restore refuses it (`vm/machine-data?`, `:continuation-registers`), and the register lane cannot even run such a program across a park.\n  - §8.3 item 6 hints at this; §4 and §7.2 do not name it.\n  - The implementation refuses before attach.\n- **D5, carried from 6b, as briefed.** B2: under `:depth-3`, row 21 captures the wrapper's `x`. The value 1 stays pinned and holds on all three lanes.\n\n## Uncertainties\n\n- **Node and Dart were not run**, as the brief said. The new code relies on `satisfies?`, `^:dynamic`/`binding` (harness `*lane*`) and `memoize` on Dart. I believe all three are supported, but this is unverified.\n  - Portability rules were followed: no `for` over long seqs (`ordered-pairs` uses `mapcat`/`keep`), `(assoc {} …)` for multi-key maps, no private var access from tests, no float negation, and the catch forms reuse the harness's `error`.\n- **`logical-names` uses resource keys as the logical stream names.** This holds because all three kernels mint resource ids through the same engine counter for the same program. If a kernel diverged, law 4 would fail rather than pass silently.\n- **`canonical-task` copies `:capability-secret` from the kernel.** `kernel-task` keeps the receiving kernel's secret, so refs verify only when the scratch receiver and the kernel share a composition. The harness guarantees this; a production caller must as well.\n- **Space lookups go through `code-index`.** A program that differs from another only in binder names derives the same H, and would be refused as ambiguous rather than mis-resolved. This was not hit.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0127Yd1LWVBUSLiD1FuHP8PV","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfxoBzks19Q9PvS8At9po","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Slice 6c proper is implemented and green on the JVM, and all eight mutants are caught. Nothing is committed, and kondo was not run: the permission gate blocked both `clojure -M:kondo` and `clj-kondo`.\n\n**Prerequisites:** 5a, 5b `derive`, 5c `scan`, 6b `lift-task`/`lower-task` and the 6c-pre seam are all on the branch, so step 0 passed.\n\n**Files changed:**\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`, new, 898 lines.\n  - The static half: register pairing (`phys` and `virt`), `register-live`, the stack correspondence and `stack-layout`, the stack slices, and the environment both ways.\n  - Lift and lower between each de Bruijn kernel's state and the canonical frame, with return destinations taken from A and stack bases cumulative through each slice.\n  - Task-level lift and lower through the 6b row carrier. Anything a kernel can't hold is refused before any stream is attached.\n- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout_test.cljc`, new, 425 lines: acceptance items 1–9 under the names you gave.\n- `safepoint_harness_test.cljc` (+392/−13):\n  - A `*lane*` switch, so the same worlds can be built on any kernel.\n  - `cross-engine-canonical-equality` and `cross-engine-lowering`, each as a slow full matrix plus a quick bare slice.\n  - `layout-refusals`.\n  - The 6b tests behave as before.\n\nNo kernel, design doc or `rows.cljc` was touched.\n\n**Test results (JVM):**\n- New and touched namespaces, quick tier: 31 tests, 3162 assertions, 0 failures.\n- Same namespaces, slow tier: 3 tests, 2411 assertions, 0 failures.\n- Every `yin.vm.ucf.*`, `yin.vm.semantic-register.*` and `yin.vm.debruijn.*` namespace: 947 tests, 22248 assertions, 0 failures. That run came before I added one fixture to `layout_test` (see M4); I reran `layout_test` cleanly afterwards.\n\n**Counts asserted:**\n- **Law 4 (one root on all three lanes):** 61 cases.\n- **Law 5 (six ordered lowering pairs):** 350 runs.\n- **Multi-frame fixture:** bases `0 0 0 2 4` and stack length 6 pinned from the kernel. Lowering the lifted frame emits the same five bases, and the run continues to `[1 [2 [3 \"B\"]]]`.\n\n**Mutation checks:**\n- M1, M2, M5, M6, M7 and M8 were caught as written.\n- M3 (before-slice bases) is caught by the item-6 fixture and the slow law-5 matrix. The quick bare law-5 slice misses it, because bare rows have empty slices.\n- M4 (extra register at a tail boundary) was not caught at first: in every corpus program it changed nothing. I added a tail-call-in-operand program to the corpus, and the test now catches it.\n\n**Deviations:**\n- **Rows 7–12 and 17 are off the de Bruijn lanes.** They need linking and install children, which the layouts don't reconstruct. So laws 4 and 5 cover 15 of the 22 wrapped rows plus row 20, and item 9 covers rows 6 and 20 only.\n- **Row 4 is never lowered into a de Bruijn receiver.** I checked that the exclusion is needed: without it, all four such pairs stay blocked (D2 below).\n- **The API is one function each way.** `lift-state` and `lower-state` dispatch to separate act and tail paths, instead of the design's `lift-act`/`lift-tail`/`lower-act`/`lower-tail` names.\n\n**Design defects for the Architect:**\n- **D1:** a ring-buffer cursor position embeds its stream's identity, which §5.5's renaming doesn't reach. Law 4 failed across lanes on every row with a cursor until the harness renamed those identities itself.\n- **D2:** both de Bruijn kernels re-park a retained FFI request on their own call-out pair, ignoring the carried response cursor. This needs a kernel seam like 6c-pre.\n- **D3:** §4.2's restricted `virt` gives no answer for item 12's \"live tampered upward\" case. I fall back to the register's unique virtual in the body, and refuse when that is ambiguous.\n- **D4:** the register kernel can't hold a primitive function in a parked frame, although the carrier and the reference lane allow one. I refuse it before attach as `:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers`, a status and kind I named.\n\nThe full report, with every count, deviation and uncertainty, is at `collab/1792340000000-engineer-6c-layouts.report.md`. Node and Dart weren't run, as briefed; the main portability risk is that the harness's `*lane*` uses `binding` and `satisfies?` on Dart."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6530,"cache_read_input_tokens":408075,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6530},"output_tokens":860,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b921a1a6-65e4-4bc4-853c-fc87c8b912fe","uuid":"67c18fd4-51a4-4b38-8341-3710e5e8ea8e","timestamp":"2026-10-12T18:14:04.195Z","request_id":"req_011CfxoBzH6MjF2hViaXLGd3"}
{"duration_api_ms":1537518,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"b921a1a6-65e4-4bc4-853c-fc87c8b912fe","total_cost_usd":12.1890104,"usage":{"input_tokens":200,"cache_creation_input_tokens":402231,"cache_read_input_tokens":28725612,"output_tokens":161262,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":74796},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":402231,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1701,"cache_read_input_tokens":408075,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6530,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6530},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":200,"outputTokens":161262,"cacheReadInputTokens":28725612,"cacheCreationInputTokens":402231,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":12.1890104,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":74796,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_01LWMWmJkCqdPP1FJ5UL3qGH","tool_input":{"command":"git log --oneline --all-match --grep='6c-pre' | head; git log --oneline | grep -iE 'phase ?5|5a|5b|6c' | head; grep -n 'defn lift-task\\|defn lower-task\\|defn canonical-under\\|^(defn' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc | head -100; grep -n '^(defn\\|^(def ' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/derive.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/analysis.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc; grep -n 'return-result\\|resume-mode' src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/stack.cljc | head","description":"Confirm prerequisites exist"}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_019Uok5ZJwv5WhDUtxvNz6YT","tool_input":{"command":"grep -n ':test\\b\\|:test {' -A8 deps.edn | head -30; ls; mise trust 2>&1 | tail -1","description":"Inspect deps.edn test alias"}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_01WFkZoH1sMTLH3WiPhwqVKX","tool_input":{"command":"clojure -M:nREPL --port 47811","description":"Start nREPL server for probing","run_in_background":true}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_01VLqcDDsvVTZcjBBsukhPPe","tool_input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc'\ns=open(p).read()\ns=s.replace(\"            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]\\n\",\"            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]\\n            [yin.vm.module :as module]\\n\",1)\ns+=r'''(defn kernel-kind\n  \"`:stack` or `:register`, by the kernel's link format.\"\n  [k]\n  (let [f (when (satisfies? module/IModuleKernel k) (:format (module/link-format k)))]\n    (cond\n      (= stack/format-tag f) :stack\n      (= register/format-tag f) :register\n      :else (refuse! :yin.k/unsupported :kernel {:yin.k/format f}))))\n\n\n(defn- image-pc\n  \"`[segment rel-pc]` of an absolute kernel pc.\"\n  [kind k space pc]\n  (let [[ident rel] (or (if (= :stack kind) (stack/image-pc k pc) (register/image-pc k pc))\n                        (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :unknown-image {:yin.k/pc pc}))]\n    [(image-segment space kind ident) rel]))\n\n\n(defn- offset\n  \"The offset of segment `seg`'s image in kernel `k`'s code space.\"\n  [kind k seg]\n  (let [ident (if (= :stack kind) (:stack-id seg) (:register-id seg))]\n    (or (if (= :stack kind) (stack/absolute-pc k [ident 0]) (register/absolute-pc k [ident 0]))\n        (refuse! :yin.k/unsatisfied :image-not-attached {:yin.k/segment (:address seg)}))))\n\n\n(def ^:private parking-ops\n  \"A1: the instructions a resume pc may follow, besides a non-tail call.\"\n  #{:stream-next :stream-put :ffi-call :park :current-continuation})\n\n\n(defn- safepoint!\n  \"Refuse `:yin.k/not-at-safepoint` unless the A instruction before `pc`\n   parks: a layout never invents a safepoint (§4.5).\"\n  [seg pc tail?]\n  (let [t (when (and (integer? pc) (< 0 pc (inc (count (:vector seg))))) (inst seg (dec pc)))\n        op (when t (nth t 0))]\n    (when-not (and t (if tail?\n                       (code/tail-call? t)\n                       (or (contains? parking-ops op) (and (= :call op) (not (code/tail-call? t))))))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc pc}))))\n\n\n(defn- h->a\n  \"The A pc of H pc `hpc`, or not-at-safepoint for a `:push`.\"\n  [seg hpc]\n  (or (get (:h->a seg) hpc) (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc hpc})))\n\n\n(defn- a->h\n  [seg pc]\n  (nth (:a->h seg) pc))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Values: positional closures and kernel continuations, both ways\n;; =============================================================================\n;;\n;; A lift context `cx` is {:kind :kernel :space :free-env :owner}; a lower\n;; context `lx` is {:kind :kernel :space :free-env :owner :address}, where\n;; :address names a canonical closure's or payload's :segment as A.\n\n(declare lift-state lower-state)\n\n\n(defn- walk-value\n  \"Rebuild guest collections of `x` around `f`, which converts closures\n   and continuations.\"\n  [f x]\n  (cond\n    (values/host-typed? x) (f x)\n    (map? x) (into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [(walk-value f k) (walk-value f v)])) x)\n    (vector? x) (mapv #(walk-value f %) x)\n    (set? x) (into #{} (map #(walk-value f %)) x)\n    (seq? x) (apply list (map #(walk-value f %) x))\n    :else x))\n\n\n(defn- canonical-closure\n  \"§4.4: a positional closure as the canonical `{:type :closure :params\n   :entry :segment :env}`, `params` from A's `:closure` at the owner of\n   the entry body, `env` by the forward rule over its frames.\"\n  [cx c]\n  (let [[seg rel] (image-pc (:kind cx) (:kernel cx) (:space cx) (:body-pc c))\n        entry (if (= :stack (:kind cx)) (h->a seg rel) rel)\n        owner (:owner (nth (:bodies seg) (body-of seg entry)))\n        _ (when-not (and owner (= entry (nth (inst seg owner) 3)))\n            (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :closure-origin {:yin.k/entry entry}))\n        ps (params seg owner)]\n    (when-not (= (count ps) (:arity c))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :closure-binders {:yin.k/entry entry}))\n    {:type :closure :params ps :entry entry :segment (:address seg)\n     :env (env-of seg owner (:free-env cx)\n                  (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(walk-value (partial canonical-value* cx) %) f)) (:frames c))\n                  (:store-of c))}))\n\n\n(defn- canonical-value*\n  [cx x]\n  (cond\n    (values/closure? x) (values/closure (:owner cx) (canonical-closure cx (values/payload x)))\n    (values/continuation? x)\n    (values/continuation (:owner cx) (assoc (lift-state cx (values/payload x)) :type :reified-continuation))\n    :else x))\n\n\n(defn canonical-value\n  \"A kernel value with every positional closure and kernel continuation\n   reconstructed canonically (cx: a lift context).\"\n  [cx x]\n  (walk-value (partial canonical-value* cx) x))\n\n\n(defn- address-of\n  [lx seg-id]\n  (let [a ((:address lx) seg-id)]\n    (when (nil? a) (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :unaddressed-segment {:yin.k/hint seg-id}))\n    a))\n\n\n(defn- positional-closure\n  \"The inverse of canonical-closure: the entry's absolute pc in the\n   receiving kernel and the frames read back from `env` by the chain.\"\n  [lx c]\n  (let [seg (segment-of (:space lx) (address-of lx (:segment c)))\n        entry (:entry c)\n        owner (:owner (nth (:bodies seg) (body-of seg entry)))\n        off (offset (:kind lx) (:kernel lx) seg)\n        store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env c))]\n    (when-not (and owner (= (vec (:params c)) (vec (params seg owner))))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/undecodable :closure-binders {:yin.k/entry entry}))\n    (cond-> {:type :closure :arity (count (:params c))\n             :body-pc (+ off (if (= :stack (:kind lx)) (a->h seg entry) entry))\n             :frames (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(positional-value lx %) f)) (frames-of seg owner (:env c)))}\n      (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))\n\n\n(defn- positional-value*\n  [lx x]\n  (cond\n    (values/closure? x) (values/closure (:owner lx) (positional-closure lx (values/payload x)))\n    (values/continuation? x)\n    (values/continuation (:owner lx) (merge {:type :reified-continuation} (lower-state lx (values/payload x))))\n    :else x))\n\n\n(defn positional-value\n  \"A canonical value with every closure and continuation lowered into the\n   receiving kernel (lx: a lower context).\"\n  [lx x]\n  (walk-value (partial positional-value* lx) x))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Lift: a kernel payload as the canonical act or tail\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def ^:private kernel-keys\n  \"The register payload keys of either kernel; every other key of an\n   entry is the engine's and travels as the pending.\"\n  #{:segment :site-pc :pc :frames :regs :live :continuation :dest :resume-mode\n    :format :hash :image :images :store-of :stack})\n\n\n(defn- engine-fields\n  [conv entry]\n  (let [fields (apply dissoc entry kernel-keys)]\n    (cond-> fields (contains? fields :datom) (update :datom conv))))\n\n\n(defn- canonical-env\n  [cx seg pc frames store-of]\n  (env-of seg pc (:free-env cx)\n          (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(canonical-value cx %) f)) frames)\n          store-of))\n\n\n(defn- register-kframe\n  \"§4.2 step 6, one frame independently: its image row, A, `rd` from A.\"\n  [cx f]\n  (let [[seg pc] (image-pc :register (:kernel cx) (:space cx) (:return-pc f))\n        _ (safepoint! seg pc false)\n        site (dec pc)]\n    {:type :return :segment (:address seg) :pc pc\n     :env (canonical-env cx seg pc (:frames f) (:store-of f))\n     :window (into {} (map (fn [[s x]] [(virt seg site s) (canonical-value cx x)])) (:regs f))\n     :rd (code/rd (inst seg site))}))\n\n\n(defn- lift-register\n  [cx entry]\n  (let [[seg rel] (image-pc :register (:kernel cx) (:space cx) (:pc entry))\n        tail? (= :return-result (:resume-mode entry))\n        k (mapv #(register-kframe cx %) (:continuation entry))]\n    (safepoint! seg rel tail?)\n    (if tail?\n      {:segment (:address seg) :site (dec rel) :k k :deliver {:deliver :return}}\n      (let [site (dec rel)]\n        {:segment (:address seg) :pc rel\n         :env (canonical-env cx seg rel (:frames entry) (:store-of entry))\n         :window (into {} (map (fn [[s x]] [(virt seg site s) (canonical-value cx x)])) (:regs entry))\n         :k k\n         :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd (code/rd (inst seg site))}}))))\n\n\n(defn- slice-window\n  \"`(zipmap (stack-layout (dec pc)) slice)`, the two of equal length.\"\n  [cx seg pc slice]\n  (let [layout (stack-layout seg (dec pc))]\n    (when-not (= (count layout) (count slice))\n      (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :stack-layout {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/layout layout :yin.k/slice (count slice)}))\n    (zipmap layout (map #(canonical-value cx %) slice))))\n\n\n(defn slices\n  \"§4.3 K slices of an operand stack `st` under return frames with bases\n   `bases` (outermost first): `St[b(i-1), b(i))` per frame, `b(-1) = 0`,\n   then the current activation's `St[b(m-1), n)`.\"\n  [st bases]\n  (let [bounds (into [0] bases)]\n    (conj (mapv (fn [lo hi] (subvec st lo hi)) bounds (rest bounds))\n          (subvec st (peek bounds) (count st)))))\n\n\n(defn- stack-pc\n  \"The canonical resume pc of an absolute stack resume pc.\"\n  [cx abs]\n  (let [[seg rel] (image-pc :stack (:kernel cx) (:space cx) abs)]\n    [seg (inc (h->a seg (dec rel)))]))\n\n\n(defn- lift-stack\n  [cx entry]\n  (let [st (vec (:stack entry))\n        frames (:continuation entry)\n        bases (mapv :stack-base frames)\n        _ (when-not (and (every? integer? bases) (apply <= 0 (conj bases (count st))))\n            (refuse! :yin.k/non-portable :stack-base {:yin.k/bases bases}))\n        parts (slices st bases)\n        k (mapv (fn [f slice]\n                  (let [[seg pc] (stack-pc cx (:return-pc f))]\n                    (safepoint! seg pc false)\n                    {:type :return :segment (:address seg) :pc pc\n                     :env (canonical-env cx seg pc (:frames f) (:store-of f))\n                     :window (slice-window cx seg pc slice)\n                     :rd (code/rd (inst seg (dec pc)))}))\n                frames parts)\n        [seg rel] (image-pc :stack (:kernel cx) (:space cx) (:pc entry))\n        site (h->a seg (dec rel))\n        tail? (code/tail-call? (inst seg site))]\n    (safepoint! seg (inc site) tail?)\n    (if tail?\n      {:segment (:address seg) :site site :k k :deliver {:deliver :return}}\n      (let [pc (inc site)]\n        {:segment (:address seg) :pc pc\n         :env (canonical-env cx seg pc (:frames entry) (:store-of entry))\n         :window (slice-window cx seg pc (peek parts))\n         :k k\n         :deliver {:deliver :rd :rd (code/rd (inst seg site))}}))))\n\n\n(defn lift-state\n  \"A kernel's wait entry, parked record or reified payload as the\n   canonical act or tail payload, engine fields carried (§4.2, §4.3).\"\n  [cx entry]\n  (merge (engine-fields #(canonical-value cx %) entry)\n         (if (= :stack (:kind cx)) (lift-stack cx entry) (lift-register cx entry))))\n\n\n(defn lift-context\n  \"The lift context of kernel `k` over `space`: positional closures and\n   continuations are reminted under `owner`.\"\n  [k space owner]\n  (assoc {} :kind (kernel-kind k) :kernel k :space space :free-env (or (:free-env k) {}) :owner owner))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Lower: a canonical act or tail into a kernel\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- lower-frames\n  [lx seg pc env]\n  (mapv (fn [f] (mapv #(positional-value lx %) f)) (frames-of seg pc env)))\n\n\n(defn- payload-segment\n  [lx p]\n  (segment-of (:space lx) (address-of lx (:segment p))))\n\n\n(defn- lower-regs\n  [lx seg pc w]\n  (vec (sort-by first (map (fn [[x v]] [(phys seg pc x) (positional-value lx v)]) w))))\n\n\n(defn- register-k\n  [lx k]\n  (mapv (fn [f]\n          (let [seg (payload-segment lx f)\n                off (offset :register (:kernel lx) seg)\n                pc (:pc f)\n                regs (lower-regs lx seg pc (:window f))\n                store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env f))]\n            (cond-> {:site-pc (+ off pc -1) :return-pc (+ off pc)\n                     :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env f))\n                     :regs regs :live (mapv first regs)\n                     :dest (phys seg (dec pc) (:rd f))}\n              (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))\n        k))\n\n\n(defn- register-common\n  [k ident]\n  (cond-> {:segment (:segment k) :format register/format-tag :hash (:hash k) :image ident}\n    (< 1 (count (:images k))) (assoc :images (:images k))))\n\n\n(defn- lower-register\n  [lx p fill]\n  (let [k (:kernel lx)\n        seg (payload-segment lx p)\n        off (offset :register k seg)\n        common (register-common k (:register-id seg))\n        kk (register-k lx (:k p))]\n    (if (contains? p :site)\n      (merge common\n             {:site-pc (+ off (:site p)) :pc (+ off (:site p) 1)\n              :frames (:frames fill []) :regs [] :live [] :continuation kk :dest nil\n              :resume-mode :return-result}\n             (when (some? (:store-of fill)) {:store-of (:store-of fill)}))\n      (let [pc (:pc p)\n            regs (lower-regs lx seg pc (:window p))\n            store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env p))]\n        (cond-> (merge common\n                       {:site-pc (+ off pc -1) :pc (+ off pc)\n                        :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env p))\n                        :regs regs :live (mapv first regs) :continuation kk\n                        :dest (phys seg (dec pc) (get-in p [:deliver :rd]))\n                        :resume-mode :write-result})\n          (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))))\n\n\n(defn- layout-slice\n  \"`(mapv W (stack-layout (dec pc)))`, every member present in W.\"\n  [lx seg pc w]\n  (mapv (fn [x]\n          (when-not (contains? w x)\n            (refuse! :yin.k/undecodable :window-missing {:yin.k/pc pc :yin.k/register x}))\n          (positional-value lx (get w x)))\n        (stack-layout seg (dec pc))))\n\n\n(defn- stack-k\n  \"The return frames and their slices, bases cumulative through each\n   slice (§4.3): `b(i) = b(i-1) + |slice(i)|`.\"\n  [lx k]\n  (reduce (fn [[frames st] f]\n            (let [seg (payload-segment lx f)\n                  pc (:pc f)\n                  slice (layout-slice lx seg pc (:window f))\n                  st (into st slice)\n                  store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env f))]\n              [(conj frames (cond-> {:return-pc (+ (offset :stack (:kernel lx) seg) (a->h seg (dec pc)) 1)\n                                     :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env f))\n                                     :stack-base (count st)}\n                              (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of)))\n               st]))\n          [[] []]\n          k))\n\n\n(defn- lower-stack\n  [lx p fill]\n  (let [k (:kernel lx)\n        seg (payload-segment lx p)\n        off (offset :stack k seg)\n        [frames st] (stack-k lx (:k p))\n        common {:segment (:segment k) :format stack/format-tag :hash (:hash k) :image (:stack-id seg)}]\n    (if (contains? p :site)\n      (merge common\n             {:pc (+ off (a->h seg (:site p)) 1) :resume-mode :return-result\n              :stack st :frames (:frames fill []) :continuation frames}\n             (when (some? (:store-of fill)) {:store-of (:store-of fill)}))\n      (let [pc (:pc p)\n            store-of (engine/env-store-of (:env p))]\n        (cond-> (merge common\n                       {:pc (+ off (a->h seg (dec pc)) 1)\n                        :frames (lower-frames lx seg pc (:env p))\n                        :stack (into st (layout-slice lx seg pc (:window p)))\n                        :continuation frames})\n          (some? store-of) (assoc :store-of store-of))))))\n\n\n(def ^:private canonical-keys\n  #{:segment :pc :site :env :window :k :deliver :type :id})\n\n\n(defn lower-state\n  \"A canonical act or tail payload as the receiving kernel's entry, engine\n   fields carried (§4.2, §4.3). `fill` gives a tail entry's unobservable\n   `:frames`/`:store-of` (default `[]`/nil; §5.6 item 8).\"\n  ([lx p] (lower-state lx p {}))\n  ([lx p fill]\n   (merge (let [fields (apply dissoc p canonical-keys)]\n            (cond-> fields (contains? fields :datom) (update :datom #(positional-value lx %))))\n          (if (= :stack (:kind lx)) (lower-stack lx p fill) (lower-register lx p fill)))))\n\n\n(defn lower-context\n  \"The lower context into kernel `k` over `space`; `address` maps a\n   payload's or closure's `:segment` to A (default: it is A).\"\n  ([k space] (lower-context k space identity))\n  ([k space address]\n   (assoc {} :kind (kernel-kind k) :kernel k :space space :free-env (or (:free-env k) {})\n          :owner (:owner k) :address address)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Tasks\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- refuse-busy!\n  [k]\n  (when (engine/active-continuation? k)\n    (refuse! :yin.k/not-at-safepoint nil {:yin.k/pc (:pc k)}))\n  (when (seq (:installs k))\n    (refuse! :yin.k/unsupported :installs {:yin.k/names (vec (keys (:installs k)))})))\n\n\n(defn canonical-task\n  \"Quiescent kernel `k` presented as the semantic-register machine\n   `template` (a fresh machine of the same composition): code by A from\n   `space`, every wait, parked record and value reconstructed through the\n   layout, resources, stores and counters carried. `rows/lift-task` then\n   lifts it as it lifts its own kernel.\"\n  [k space template]\n  (refuse-busy! k)\n  (let [cx (lift-context k space (:owner template))\n        conv #(canonical-value cx %)\n        parked (into {} (map (fn [[id rec]]\n                               [id (assoc (lift-state cx rec) :type :parked-continuation :id id)]))\n                     (:parked k))\n        value (:value k)\n        parked-value? (and (map? value) (= :parked-continuation (:type value)) (contains? parked (:id value)))\n        code (into {} (map (fn [[a s]] [a (assoc {} :vector (:vector s) :address a :analysis (:analysis s))]))\n                   (:segments space))]\n    (assoc template\n           :code code\n           :code-aliases (into {} (map (fn [a] [a a])) (keys code))\n           :program nil :control nil :window {} :k nil\n           :resources (:resources k)\n           :capability-secret (:capability-secret k)\n           :free-env (:free-env k)\n           :env (or (:free-env k) {})\n           :store (conv (:store k))\n           :module-stores (conv (or (:module-stores k) {}))\n           :parked parked\n           :wait-set (mapv #(lift-state cx %) (:wait-set k))\n           :ready-queue (:ready-queue k)\n           :value (if parked-value? (get parked (:id value)) (conv value))\n           :id-counter (:id-counter k)\n           :installs {}\n           :halted? (:halted? k)\n           :blocked? (:blocked? k))))\n\n\n(defn- attach-all\n  \"`k` holding the image of every segment of `space`, in A order.\"\n  [k space]\n  (let [kind (kernel-kind k)]\n    (reduce (fn [k a]\n              (let [seg (segment-of space a)]\n                (if (= :stack kind)\n                  (stack/attach-image k (:stack seg) vm-stack-contract)\n                  (register/attach-image k (:register seg) vm-register-contract))))\n            k\n            (sort-by str (keys (:segments space))))))\n'''\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF","description":"Append dynamic half to layout"}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_011gmzK1YZNt83KJi4uiSccB","tool_input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc'\ns=open(p).read()\nold_ns_doc='''   observed wait's (law 3); every lifted frame passes the validator\n   (law 6). Row 20 runs once, outside the wrappers.\"'''\nnew_ns_doc='''   observed wait's (law 3); every lifted frame passes the validator\n   (law 6). Row 20 runs once, outside the wrappers.\n\n   Phase 6c adds the two de Bruijn lanes (§5.3): a world built under\n   *lane* :stack or :register runs A's derived image on that kernel and\n   lifts and lowers through yin.vm.semantic-register.layout. Law 4 (one\n   root id on all three lanes under canonical-under), law 5 (six ordered\n   lowering pairs, each continuing to the reference result) and the\n   layout refusals close the file.\"'''\nassert old_ns_doc in s\ns=s.replace(old_ns_doc,new_ns_doc)\ns=s.replace('''            [yin.vm.semantic-register.code :as code]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.frame :as frame]''','''            [yin.vm.debruijn.register :as rvm]\n            [yin.vm.debruijn.stack :as dvm]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.code :as code]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.frame :as frame]''')\ns=s.replace('''            [yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test :as ft]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.linearize :as linearize]''','''            [yin.vm.semantic-register.frame-test :as ft]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.layout :as layout]\n            [yin.vm.semantic-register.linearize :as linearize]''')\nold='''(defn project\n  [ast]\n  (let [v (:vector (linearize/project ast))]\n    (swap! source-index update (jing/segment-key v) (fnil conj #{}) (jing/segment-key [:yin/tree ast]))\n    v))\n\n\n(def stream-modules (module/register-stream-module (module/default-registry)))\n\n\n(defn machine\n  ([] (machine {}))\n  ([opts]\n   (sr/create-vm (merge {:make-stream tu/make-stream :capability-secret tu/secret\n                         :secret-source (fn [origin] (str tu/secret \"/\" (name origin)))\n                         :modules stream-modules}\n                        opts))))\n\n\n(defn load-program\n  [m ast]\n  (sr/load-vector m (project ast) code/contract))\n'''\nnew='''(def code-index\n  \"{A v}: every vector this harness projects, for the layouts' spaces.\"\n  (atom {}))\n\n\n(defn project\n  [ast]\n  (let [v (:vector (linearize/project ast))\n        a (jing/segment-key v)]\n    (swap! source-index update a (fnil conj #{}) (jing/segment-key [:yin/tree ast]))\n    (swap! code-index assoc a v)\n    v))\n\n\n(def stream-modules (module/register-stream-module (module/default-registry)))\n\n\n(def ^:dynamic *lane*\n  \"The kernel a world is built on: :semantic-register (the reference\n   lane), or :stack or :register, which run A's derived image and lift\n   and lower through the layouts (§5.3).\"\n  :semantic-register)\n\n\n(def lanes [:semantic-register :stack :register])\n\n\n(defn- composition\n  [opts]\n  (merge {:make-stream tu/make-stream :capability-secret tu/secret\n          :secret-source (fn [origin] (str tu/secret \"/\" (name origin)))\n          :modules stream-modules}\n         opts))\n\n\n(defn- kernel-opts\n  \"A de Bruijn kernel's options for the same composition: the full\n   primitive table, and the initial environment as :free-env.\"\n  [opts]\n  (cond-> (assoc (dissoc (composition opts) :env) :primitives vm/primitives)\n    (contains? opts :env) (assoc :free-env (:env opts))))\n\n\n(defn machine\n  ([] (machine {}))\n  ([opts]\n   (case *lane*\n     :semantic-register (sr/create-vm (composition opts))\n     :stack (dvm/create-vm [] (kernel-opts opts))\n     :register (rvm/create-vm {:bodies [] :instructions []} (kernel-opts opts)))))\n\n\n(defn lane-of\n  [m]\n  (let [f (:format (module/link-format m))]\n    (cond (= dvm/format-tag f) :stack\n          (= rvm/format-tag f) :register\n          :else :semantic-register)))\n\n\n(def segment-of\n  \"layout/segment, once per A.\"\n  (memoize layout/segment))\n\n\n(defn kernel-space\n  \"The layout space of the A vectors whose images de Bruijn kernel k holds.\"\n  [k]\n  (let [id (if (= :stack (lane-of k)) :stack-id :register-id)\n        held (into #{} (map first) (:images k))]\n    (layout/space (filter #(contains? held (id %)) (map segment-of (vals @code-index))))))\n\n\n(defn- kernel-load\n  \"Attach v's derived image and start it at its row, beside whatever the\n   kernel already holds: parked records keep naming their own rows.\"\n  [k v]\n  (let [kind (lane-of k)\n        sg (segment-of v)\n        k (if (= :stack kind)\n            (dvm/attach-image k (:stack sg) vm/stack-contract)\n            (rvm/attach-image k (:register sg) vm/register-contract))\n        k (assoc k :pc (layout/offset kind k sg) :frames [] :continuation [] :store-of nil\n                 :halted? false :blocked? false :value nil)]\n    (if (= :stack kind)\n      (assoc k :stack [])\n      (assoc k :registers (vec (repeat (:registers (first (:bodies (:register sg)))) nil))))))\n\n\n(defn load-program\n  [m ast]\n  (if (= :semantic-register (lane-of m))\n    (sr/load-vector m (project ast) code/contract)\n    (kernel-load m (project ast))))\n\n\n(defn lane-closure\n  \"Canonical closure payload c (its :segment an A) as machine m's value.\"\n  [m c]\n  (if (= :semantic-register (lane-of m))\n    (values/closure (:owner m) (update c :segment #(get (:code-aliases m) %)))\n    (layout/positional-value (layout/lower-context m (kernel-space m)) (values/closure (:owner m) c))))\n'''\nassert old in s\ns=s.replace(old,new)\nold='''  (let [ring (s/ring 4)\n        m (vm/run (load-program (machine) (with-cat (wrap (s/app (s/lam ['p] {:type :vm/park}) (s/lit 1))))))\n        cursor (fn [m]\n                 (let [[ref m] (engine/attach-resource m ring) [id m] (engine/gensym m \"cursor\")]\n                   (engine/handle-cursor m {:stream ref} id)))\n        [c1 m] (cursor m)\n        [c2 m] (cursor m)\n        [seg image] (first (:code m))\n        closure (first (filter #(= :closure (first %)) (:vector image)))\n        clo (values/closure (:owner m) {:type :closure :params (nth closure 2) :entry (nth closure 3)\n                                        :segment seg :env {engine/store-of-key 'host.mod}})]'''\nnew='''  (let [ring (s/ring 4)\n        ast (with-cat (wrap (s/app (s/lam ['p] {:type :vm/park}) (s/lit 1))))\n        v (project ast)\n        m (vm/run (load-program (machine) ast))\n        cursor (fn [m]\n                 (let [[ref m] (engine/attach-resource m ring) [id m] (engine/gensym m \"cursor\")]\n                   (engine/handle-cursor m {:stream ref} id)))\n        [c1 m] (cursor m)\n        [c2 m] (cursor m)\n        closure (first (filter #(= :closure (first %)) v))\n        clo (lane-closure m {:type :closure :params (nth closure 2) :entry (nth closure 3)\n                             :segment (jing/segment-key v) :env {engine/store-of-key 'host.mod}})]'''\nassert old in s\ns=s.replace(old,new)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF","description":"Add lane infrastructure to harness"}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_01MPmzSxZGAouVTybkNLEeNG","tool_input":{"command":"cat >> test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc <<'EOF'\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Phase 6c: the cross-engine lanes (§5.3, §5.4 laws 4 and 5, §8.3 10-12)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def link-rows\n  \"Rows the de Bruijn lanes do not carry: a require installs a child and\n   attaches a module image in the kernel's own format, and the layouts\n   of §4 reconstruct no install child (canonical-task refuses\n   :installs).\"\n  #{7 8 9 10 11 12 17})\n\n\n(def lane-rows\n  \"The wrapped rows every lane executes: 15 of §5.2's 22.\"\n  (filterv #(not (contains? link-rows (first %))) rows))\n\n\n(defn lane-cases\n  \"15 lane rows x 4 segments, plus row 20 once: [row segment expected build].\"\n  []\n  (filterv #(not (contains? link-rows (first %))) (matrix-cases)))\n\n\n(defn on-lane\n  [lane build]\n  (binding [*lane* lane] (build)))\n\n\n(defn lane-lift\n  \"The lift of §7.1 on m's own lane.\"\n  [m serve!]\n  (if (= :semantic-register (lane-of m))\n    (rows/lift-task m serve! (lift-opts))\n    (layout/lift-task m (kernel-space m) (on-lane :semantic-register machine) serve! (lift-opts))))\n\n\n(defn lane-lower\n  \"The lower of §7.2 into a fresh receiver on lane, of composition opts.\"\n  [lane opts wire attach!]\n  (let [scratch (on-lane :semantic-register #(machine opts))]\n    (if (= :semantic-register lane)\n      (rows/lower-task scratch wire attach!)\n      (layout/lower-task scratch (on-lane lane #(machine opts)) wire attach!))))\n\n\n(defn logical-names\n  \"§5.5's explicit correspondence: each served identity to the resource\n   key the lifted machine holds that handle under. The fixture builds the\n   same program on every lane, so a key names one logical stream.\"\n  [m peer]\n  (into {} (map (fn [[id e]]\n                  [id (or (some (fn [[k h]] (when (identical? h (:handle e)) k)) (:resources m))\n                          (throw (ex-info \"served handle the machine does not hold\" {:id id})))]))\n        @(:table peer)))\n\n\n(defn lane-root\n  \"Build on lane, lift, and answer [root-under-logical-names reference].\"\n  [lane build]\n  (let [world (on-lane lane build)\n        {:keys [serve! peer]} (served (or (:surfaces world) #{:reader}))\n        wire (lane-lift (:machine world) serve!)]\n    (is (nil? (rows/validate-frames (rows/decode wire))) \"law 6 on every lane\")\n    [(:yin.k/root (rows/canonical-under wire (logical-names (:machine world) peer)))\n     (reference (on-lane lane build))]))\n\n\n(defn canonical-equality\n  \"Law 4 for one case: the three lanes lift to one root, and each lane's\n   own run answers the reference.\"\n  [build expected]\n  (let [roots (mapv #(lane-root % build) lanes)]\n    (is (apply = (map first roots)) \"law 4: one root id on every lane\")\n    (doseq [[lane [_ ref]] (map vector lanes roots)]\n      (is (:halted ref) (str lane \" completes\"))\n      (when (some? expected) (is (= expected (:value ref)) (str lane \" answers the reference\"))))\n    roots))\n\n\n(deftest cross-engine-canonical-equality-bare\n  ;; the quick-tier slice of acceptance 10: every lane row once, bare\n  (let [n (atom 0)]\n    (doseq [[row r build] lane-rows]\n      (testing (str \"row \" row)\n        (canonical-equality #(build identity) r)\n        (swap! n inc)))\n    (testing \"row 20\"\n      (canonical-equality tail-effect-halt \"B\")\n      (swap! n inc))\n    (is (= 16 @n))))\n\n\n(deftest ^:slow cross-engine-canonical-equality\n  (slow/guard\n    \"cross-engine-canonical-equality\"\n    (fn []\n      (let [cases (lane-cases) n (atom 0)]\n        (is (= 61 (count cases)) \"15 lane rows x 4 segments + row 20\")\n        (doseq [[row label expected build] cases]\n          (testing (str \"row \" row \" \" (name label))\n            (canonical-equality build expected)\n            (swap! n inc)))\n        (is (= 61 @n))))))\n\n\n(def ordered-pairs\n  \"Law 5's six ordered pairs of distinct lanes.\"\n  (vec (for [from lanes to lanes :when (not= from to)] [from to])))\n\n\n(def unlowerable\n  \"[row target] pairs a de Bruijn receiver cannot continue: row 4's\n   retained FFI re-parks through the kernel's own call-out pair\n   (ffi/response-wait-entry), not the carried response cursor cell the\n   semantic-register kernel restores (a kernel seam, reported).\"\n  #{[4 :stack] [4 :register]})\n\n\n(defn crossed\n  \"Lift on from, lower into a fresh receiver on to, satisfy through the\n   source's streams: the run's observables and attaches.\"\n  [from to build]\n  (let [world (on-lane from build)\n        {:keys [toy peer serve!]} (served (or (:surfaces world) #{:reader}))\n        wire (lane-lift (:machine world) serve!)\n        receiver ((or (:receiver world) (constantly nil)))\n        {:keys [attach!] n :count} (recording-attacher toy)\n        recv (lane-lower to (merge {:attach-stream attach!} (:opts receiver)) wire attach!)\n        _ (is (= to (lane-of recv)))\n        attaches @n\n        done (satisfy world recv #(drive-peers % [peer]))\n        _ (s/serve-mirror! peer)]\n    (when-let [f (:finish world)] (f))\n    (when-let [f (:finish receiver)] (f))\n    {:attaches attaches :wire wire\n     :halted (vm/halted? done) :value (normalize (vm/value done)) :trace @(:trace world)}))\n\n\n(defn cross-lowering\n  \"Law 5 for one case: every admitted ordered pair continues to the\n   reference; answers the pairs run.\"\n  [row build]\n  (let [ref (reference (on-lane :semantic-register build))]\n    (is (:halted ref))\n    (reduce (fn [ran [from to]]\n              (if (contains? unlowerable [row to])\n                ran\n                (testing (str (name from) \" -> \" (name to))\n                  (let [got (crossed from to build)]\n                    (is (= (count (stream-identities (:wire got))) (:attaches got)))\n                    (is (= (select-keys ref [:halted :value :trace]) (select-keys got [:halted :value :trace]))\n                        \"law 5: the lowered run continues to the reference\")\n                    (conj ran [from to])))))\n            []\n            ordered-pairs)))\n\n\n(deftest cross-engine-lowering-bare\n  ;; the quick-tier slice of acceptance 11: every lane row once, bare\n  (is (= 6 (count ordered-pairs)))\n  (let [ran (atom 0)]\n    (doseq [[row _ build] lane-rows]\n      (testing (str \"row \" row)\n        (let [pairs (cross-lowering row #(build identity))]\n          (is (= (if (= 4 row) 2 6) (count pairs)) \"every admitted ordered pair ran\")\n          (swap! ran + (count pairs)))))\n    (testing \"row 20\"\n      (is (= 6 (count (cross-lowering 20 tail-effect-halt))))\n      (swap! ran + 6))\n    (is (= 92 @ran) \"16 cases x 6 pairs, less row 4's 4 into a de Bruijn receiver\")))\n\n\n(deftest ^:slow cross-engine-lowering\n  (slow/guard\n    \"cross-engine-lowering\"\n    (fn []\n      (let [ran (atom 0) cases (lane-cases)]\n        (is (= 61 (count cases)))\n        (doseq [[row label _ build] cases]\n          (testing (str \"row \" row \" \" (name label))\n            (swap! ran + (count (cross-lowering row build)))))\n        (is (= 350 @ran) \"61 cases x 6 pairs, less row 4's 4 per segment\")))))\n\n\n(deftest layout-refusals\n  (testing \"a kernel stepped to a non-safepoint pc\"\n    (doseq [lane [:stack :register]]\n      (testing (name lane)\n        (let [m (on-lane lane #(vm/step (load-program (machine) (s/app (s/lam ['p] (s/lit 42)) (s/lit 1)))))\n              n (atom 0)\n              {:keys [serve!]} (served #{:reader})\n              d (error #(lane-lift m (fn [h] (swap! n inc) (serve! h))))]\n          (is (not (vm/halted? m)))\n          (is (= :yin.k/not-at-safepoint (:yin.k/status d)) (pr-str d))\n          (is (zero? @n) \"no stream was served\")\n          (testing \", its registers read as a payload\"\n            (let [entry (if (= :stack lane)\n                          (select-keys m [:pc :frames :stack :continuation])\n                          (assoc (select-keys m [:pc :frames :continuation]) :regs [] :resume-mode :write-result))\n                  cx (layout/lift-context m (kernel-space m) (:owner m))]\n              (is (= :yin.k/not-at-safepoint (:yin.k/status (error #(layout/lift-state cx entry))))))))))))\n  (testing \"a register entry whose live was tampered\"\n    (let [world (on-lane :register #(bare 1))\n          m (:machine world)\n          entry (first (:wait-set m))\n          sp (kernel-space m)\n          cx (layout/lift-context m sp (:owner m))\n          [seg pc] [(layout/segment-of sp (first (keys (:segments sp)))) nil]\n          code-map {(:address seg) (:vector seg)}\n          judge (fn [e]\n                  (let [p (layout/lift-state cx e)]\n                    (:yin.k/kind (frame/defect (ft/canonical {:code {(:address seg) {:address (:address seg)}}}\n                                                             (assoc p :segment (:address seg)) true)\n                                               code-map :wait {:free-env {} :ffi-call-ids #{}}))))\n          live (:live entry)\n          site (dec (- (:pc entry) (layout/offset :register m seg)))\n          spare (first (remove (set live) (keys (into {} (map (fn [[x s]] [s x])) (nth (:phys seg) (code/body-index (:bodies seg) site))))))]\n      (is (nil? pc))\n      (is (nil? (judge entry)) \"the untampered entry reconstructs a valid frame\")\n      (is (seq live))\n      (is (= :window-missing (judge (assoc entry :live (vec (rest live)) :regs (vec (rest (:regs entry))))))\n          \"a live register dropped\")\n      (is (some? spare))\n      (is (= :window-extra (judge (assoc entry :live (vec (sort (conj live spare)))\n                                         :regs (vec (sort-by first (conj (:regs entry) [spare :x]))))))\n          \"a dead register added\")))\n  (testing \"a primitive in a window cannot be held by the register kernel: refused before any attach\"\n    (let [build #(reader % (s/app (s/v 'conj) (s/lit []) read-op))\n          world (build identity)\n          {:keys [serve! toy]} (served #{:reader})\n          wire (rows/lift-task (:machine world) serve! (lift-opts))\n          {:keys [attach!] n :count} (recording-attacher toy)\n          d (error #(lane-lower :register {:attach-stream attach!} wire attach!))]\n      (is (= [:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers] [(:yin.k/status d) (:yin.k/kind d)]) (pr-str d))\n      (is (zero? @n) \"refused before any attachment\")\n      (testing \", the stack kernel holds it\"\n        (let [ref (reference (build identity))\n              got (crossed :semantic-register :stack #(build identity))]\n          (is (= [\"B\"] (:value ref)))\n          (is (= (:value ref) (:value got))))))))\nEOF","description":"Append cross-engine lanes to harness"}},{"tool_name":"Write","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rdr8UPsENnvYMBdu9c1mEG","tool_input":{"file_path":"/tmp/p6c_lanes.cljc","content":"\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Phase 6c: the cross-engine lanes (§5.3, §5.4 laws 4 and 5, §8.3 10-12)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def link-rows\n  \"Rows the de Bruijn lanes do not carry: a require installs a child and\n   attaches a module image in the kernel's own format, and the layouts\n   of §4 reconstruct no install child (canonical-task refuses\n   :installs).\"\n  #{7 8 9 10 11 12 17})\n\n\n(def lane-rows\n  \"The wrapped rows every lane executes: 15 of §5.2's 22.\"\n  (filterv #(not (contains? link-rows (first %))) rows))\n\n\n(defn lane-cases\n  \"15 lane rows x 4 segments, plus row 20 once: [row segment expected build].\"\n  []\n  (filterv #(not (contains? link-rows (first %))) (matrix-cases)))\n\n\n(defn on-lane\n  [lane build]\n  (binding [*lane* lane] (build)))\n\n\n(defn lane-lift\n  \"The lift of §7.1 on m's own lane.\"\n  [m serve!]\n  (if (= :semantic-register (lane-of m))\n    (rows/lift-task m serve! (lift-opts))\n    (layout/lift-task m (kernel-space m) (on-lane :semantic-register machine) serve! (lift-opts))))\n\n\n(defn lane-lower\n  \"The lower of §7.2 into a fresh receiver on lane, of composition opts.\"\n  [lane opts wire attach!]\n  (let [scratch (on-lane :semantic-register #(machine opts))]\n    (if (= :semantic-register lane)\n      (rows/lower-task scratch wire attach!)\n      (layout/lower-task scratch (on-lane lane #(machine opts)) wire attach!))))\n\n\n(defn logical-names\n  \"§5.5's explicit correspondence: each served identity to the resource\n   key the lifted machine holds that handle under. The fixture builds the\n   same program on every lane, so one key names one logical stream.\"\n  [m peer]\n  (into {} (map (fn [[id e]]\n                  [id (or (some (fn [[k h]] (when (identical? h (:handle e)) k)) (:resources m))\n                          (throw (ex-info \"A served handle the machine does not hold\" {:id id})))]))\n        @(:table peer)))\n\n\n(defn lane-root\n  \"Build on lane, lift: [root under the logical names, the lane's own\n   reference run].\"\n  [lane build]\n  (let [world (on-lane lane build)\n        {:keys [serve! peer]} (served (or (:surfaces world) #{:reader}))\n        wire (lane-lift (:machine world) serve!)]\n    (is (nil? (rows/validate-frames (rows/decode wire))) \"law 6 on every lane\")\n    [(:yin.k/root (rows/canonical-under wire (logical-names (:machine world) peer)))\n     (reference (on-lane lane build))]))\n\n\n(defn canonical-equality\n  \"Law 4 for one case: the three lanes lift to one root, and each lane's\n   own run answers the reference.\"\n  [build expected]\n  (let [roots (mapv #(lane-root % build) lanes)]\n    (is (apply = (map first roots)) \"law 4: one root id on every lane\")\n    (doseq [[lane [_ ref]] (map vector lanes roots)]\n      (is (:halted ref) (str lane \" completes\"))\n      (when (some? expected) (is (= expected (:value ref)) (str lane \" answers the reference\"))))\n    roots))\n\n\n(deftest cross-engine-canonical-equality-bare\n  ;; the quick-tier slice of acceptance 10: every lane row once, bare\n  (let [n (atom 0)]\n    (doseq [[row r build] lane-rows]\n      (testing (str \"row \" row)\n        (canonical-equality #(build identity) r)\n        (swap! n inc)))\n    (testing \"row 20\"\n      (canonical-equality tail-effect-halt \"B\")\n      (swap! n inc))\n    (is (= 16 @n))))\n\n\n(deftest ^:slow cross-engine-canonical-equality\n  (slow/guard\n    \"cross-engine-canonical-equality\"\n    (fn []\n      (let [cases (lane-cases) n (atom 0)]\n        (is (= 61 (count cases)) \"15 lane rows x 4 segments + row 20\")\n        (doseq [[row label expected build] cases]\n          (testing (str \"row \" row \" \" (name label))\n            (canonical-equality build expected)\n            (swap! n inc)))\n        (is (= 61 @n))))))\n\n\n(def ordered-pairs\n  \"Law 5's six ordered pairs of distinct lanes.\"\n  (into [] (mapcat (fn [from] (keep (fn [to] (when (not= from to) [from to])) lanes))) lanes))\n\n\n(def unlowerable\n  \"[row receiver] pairs a de Bruijn receiver cannot continue: row 4's\n   retained FFI re-parks through the kernel's own call-out pair\n   (ffi/response-wait-entry), not the carried response cursor cell the\n   semantic-register kernel restores. A kernel seam, reported.\"\n  #{[4 :stack] [4 :register]})\n\n\n(defn crossed\n  \"Lift on from, lower into a fresh receiver on to, satisfy through the\n   source's streams: the run's observables and attaches.\"\n  [from to build]\n  (let [world (on-lane from build)\n        {:keys [toy peer serve!]} (served (or (:surfaces world) #{:reader}))\n        wire (lane-lift (:machine world) serve!)\n        receiver ((or (:receiver world) (constantly nil)))\n        {:keys [attach!] n :count} (recording-attacher toy)\n        recv (lane-lower to (merge {:attach-stream attach!} (:opts receiver)) wire attach!)\n        _ (is (= to (lane-of recv)))\n        attaches @n\n        done (satisfy world recv #(drive-peers % [peer]))\n        _ (s/serve-mirror! peer)]\n    (when-let [f (:finish world)] (f))\n    (when-let [f (:finish receiver)] (f))\n    {:attaches attaches :wire wire\n     :halted (vm/halted? done) :value (normalize (vm/value done)) :trace @(:trace world)}))\n\n\n(defn cross-lowering\n  \"Law 5 for one case: every admitted ordered pair continues to the\n   reference. Answers the pairs that ran.\"\n  [row build]\n  (let [ref (reference (on-lane :semantic-register build))]\n    (is (:halted ref))\n    (reduce (fn [ran [from to]]\n              (if (contains? unlowerable [row to])\n                ran\n                (testing (str (name from) \" -> \" (name to))\n                  (let [got (crossed from to build)]\n                    (is (= (count (stream-identities (:wire got))) (:attaches got)))\n                    (is (= (select-keys ref [:halted :value :trace]) (select-keys got [:halted :value :trace]))\n                        \"law 5: the lowered run continues to the reference\")\n                    (conj ran [from to])))))\n            []\n            ordered-pairs)))\n\n\n(deftest cross-engine-lowering-bare\n  ;; the quick-tier slice of acceptance 11: every lane row once, bare\n  (is (= 6 (count ordered-pairs)))\n  (let [ran (atom 0)]\n    (doseq [[row _ build] lane-rows]\n      (testing (str \"row \" row)\n        (let [pairs (cross-lowering row #(build identity))]\n          (is (= (if (= 4 row) 2 6) (count pairs)) \"every admitted ordered pair ran\")\n          (swap! ran + (count pairs)))))\n    (testing \"row 20\"\n      (is (= 6 (count (cross-lowering 20 tail-effect-halt))))\n      (swap! ran + 6))\n    (is (= 92 @ran) \"16 cases x 6 pairs, less row 4's 4 into a de Bruijn receiver\")))\n\n\n(deftest ^:slow cross-engine-lowering\n  (slow/guard\n    \"cross-engine-lowering\"\n    (fn []\n      (let [ran (atom 0) cases (lane-cases)]\n        (is (= 61 (count cases)))\n        (doseq [[row label _ build] cases]\n          (testing (str \"row \" row \" \" (name label))\n            (swap! ran + (count (cross-lowering row build)))))\n        (is (= 350 @ran) \"61 cases x 6 pairs, less row 4's 4 per segment\")))))\n\n\n(defn register-judge\n  \"The frame validator's verdict on a register entry of m, reconstructed\n   through the layout.\"\n  [m entry]\n  (let [sp (kernel-space m)\n        p (layout/lift-state (layout/lift-context m sp (:owner m)) entry)\n        code-map (into {} (map (fn [[a sg]] [a (:vector sg)])) (:segments sp))\n        as-sr {:code (into {} (map (fn [a] [a {:address a}])) (keys code-map))}]\n    (:yin.k/kind (frame/defect (ft/canonical as-sr p true) code-map :wait {:free-env {} :ffi-call-ids #{}}))))\n\n\n(defn spare-register\n  \"A physical register of the entry's body, outside its live set.\"\n  [m entry]\n  (let [sp (kernel-space m)\n        sg (val (first (:segments sp)))\n        site (- (:site-pc entry) (layout/offset :register m sg))\n        body (nth (:phys sg) (code/body-index (:bodies sg) site))]\n    (first (remove (set (:live entry)) (sort (distinct (vals body)))))))\n\n\n(deftest layout-refusals\n  (testing \"a kernel stepped to a non-safepoint pc\"\n    (doseq [lane [:stack :register]]\n      (testing (name lane)\n        (let [m (on-lane lane #(vm/step (load-program (machine) (s/app (s/lam ['p] (s/lit 42)) (s/lit 1)))))\n              n (atom 0)\n              {:keys [serve!]} (served #{:reader})\n              d (error #(lane-lift m (fn [h] (swap! n inc) (serve! h))))]\n          (is (not (vm/halted? m)))\n          (is (= :yin.k/not-at-safepoint (:yin.k/status d)) (pr-str d))\n          (is (zero? @n) \"no stream was served\")\n          (testing \", its registers read as a payload\"\n            (let [entry (if (= :stack lane)\n                          (select-keys m [:pc :frames :stack :continuation])\n                          (assoc (select-keys m [:pc :frames :continuation]) :regs [] :resume-mode :write-result))\n                  cx (layout/lift-context m (kernel-space m) (:owner m))]\n              (is (= :yin.k/not-at-safepoint (:yin.k/status (error #(layout/lift-state cx entry))))))))))))\n  (testing \"a register entry whose live was tampered\"\n    (let [m (:machine (on-lane :register #(bare 1)))\n          entry (first (:wait-set m))\n          live (:live entry)\n          spare (spare-register m entry)]\n      (is (nil? (register-judge m entry)) \"the untampered entry reconstructs a valid frame\")\n      (is (seq live))\n      (is (= :window-missing (register-judge m (assoc entry :live (vec (rest live)) :regs (vec (rest (:regs entry))))))\n          \"a live register dropped\")\n      (is (some? spare))\n      (is (= :window-extra (register-judge m (assoc entry :live (vec (sort (conj live spare)))\n                                                    :regs (vec (sort-by first (conj (:regs entry) [spare :x]))))))\n          \"a dead register added\")))\n  (testing \"a primitive in a window: the register kernel cannot hold it, refused before any attach\"\n    (let [build #(reader % (s/app (s/v 'conj) (s/lit []) read-op))\n          world (build identity)\n          {:keys [serve! toy]} (served #{:reader})\n          wire (rows/lift-task (:machine world) serve! (lift-opts))\n          {:keys [attach!] n :count} (recording-attacher toy)\n          d (error #(lane-lower :register {:attach-stream attach!} wire attach!))]\n      (is (= [:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers] [(:yin.k/status d) (:yin.k/kind d)]) (pr-str d))\n      (is (zero? @n) \"refused before any attachment\")\n      (testing \", the stack kernel holds it\"\n        (let [ref (reference (build identity))\n              got (crossed :semantic-register :stack #(build identity))]\n          (is (= [\"B\"] (:value ref)))\n          (is (= (:value ref) (:value got))))))))\n"}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_01A1Eat4ehLgzgQ651pcEB8i","tool_input":{"command":"cp test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc /tmp/h.bak 2>/dev/null || cp test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc collab/h.bak; sed -i '' 's/#{\\[4 :stack\\] \\[4 :register\\]})/#{})/' test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc && grep -n 'unlowerable$' -A5 test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc | tail -2; clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test -v yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test/cross-engine-lowering-bare 2>&1 | grep -v '^\\s*$' | grep -v '^    [a-z]' | grep -B2 -A6 'FAIL\\|ERROR' | head -60","timeout":600000,"description":"Probe row 4 without exclusion"}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_01MGxeUkZ99F8GmqP2F2MFxx","tool_input":{"command":"rm test/yin/vm/semantic_register/zz_probe_test.clj && clojure -M:kondo --lint src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc test/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout_test.cljc test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc 2>&1 | tail -30","timeout":300000,"description":"Remove probe and run kondo on changed files"}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_01HRbdwC56rWSYSfuSZ7ioB9","tool_input":{"command":"clojure -M:kondo --lint src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc test/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout_test.cljc test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc","timeout":300000,"description":"Run kondo on changed files"}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_012yR7jvhAEAtXP9ihH7udo4","tool_input":{"command":"clj-kondo --lint src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc test/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout_test.cljc test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc","timeout":300000,"description":"Run clj-kondo binary on changed files"}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sv3cLKUrReWHhQ2u8JbjnM","tool_input":{"command":"grep -n '^(defn\\|^(def ' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc | awk '{print $2}' | sort | uniq -d; grep -c '' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc; grep -n '^(defn\\|^(def ' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc | wc -l","description":"Check layout for duplicate definitions"}}],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":124,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Slice 6c proper is implemented and green on the JVM, and all eight mutants are caught. Nothing is committed, and kondo was not run: the permission gate blocked both `clojure -M:kondo` and `clj-kondo`.\n\n**Prerequisites:** 5a, 5b `derive`, 5c `scan`, 6b `lift-task`/`lower-task` and the 6c-pre seam are all on the branch, so step 0 passed.\n\n**Files changed:**\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc`, new, 898 lines.\n  - The static half: register pairing (`phys` and `virt`), `register-live`, the stack correspondence and `stack-layout`, the stack slices, and the environment both ways.\n  - Lift and lower between each de Bruijn kernel's state and the canonical frame, with return destinations taken from A and stack bases cumulative through each slice.\n  - Task-level lift and lower through the 6b row carrier. Anything a kernel can't hold is refused before any stream is attached.\n- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout_test.cljc`, new, 425 lines: acceptance items 1–9 under the names you gave.\n- `safepoint_harness_test.cljc` (+392/−13):\n  - A `*lane*` switch, so the same worlds can be built on any kernel.\n  - `cross-engine-canonical-equality` and `cross-engine-lowering`, each as a slow full matrix plus a quick bare slice.\n  - `layout-refusals`.\n  - The 6b tests behave as before.\n\nNo kernel, design doc or `rows.cljc` was touched.\n\n**Test results (JVM):**\n- New and touched namespaces, quick tier: 31 tests, 3162 assertions, 0 failures.\n- Same namespaces, slow tier: 3 tests, 2411 assertions, 0 failures.\n- Every `yin.vm.ucf.*`, `yin.vm.semantic-register.*` and `yin.vm.debruijn.*` namespace: 947 tests, 22248 assertions, 0 failures. That run came before I added one fixture to `layout_test` (see M4); I reran `layout_test` cleanly afterwards.\n\n**Counts asserted:**\n- **Law 4 (one root on all three lanes):** 61 cases.\n- **Law 5 (six ordered lowering pairs):** 350 runs.\n- **Multi-frame fixture:** bases `0 0 0 2 4` and stack length 6 pinned from the kernel. Lowering the lifted frame emits the same five bases, and the run continues to `[1 [2 [3 \"B\"]]]`.\n\n**Mutation checks:**\n- M1, M2, M5, M6, M7 and M8 were caught as written.\n- M3 (before-slice bases) is caught by the item-6 fixture and the slow law-5 matrix. The quick bare law-5 slice misses it, because bare rows have empty slices.\n- M4 (extra register at a tail boundary) was not caught at first: in every corpus program it changed nothing. I added a tail-call-in-operand program to the corpus, and the test now catches it.\n\n**Deviations:**\n- **Rows 7–12 and 17 are off the de Bruijn lanes.** They need linking and install children, which the layouts don't reconstruct. So laws 4 and 5 cover 15 of the 22 wrapped rows plus row 20, and item 9 covers rows 6 and 20 only.\n- **Row 4 is never lowered into a de Bruijn receiver.** I checked that the exclusion is needed: without it, all four such pairs stay blocked (D2 below).\n- **The API is one function each way.** `lift-state` and `lower-state` dispatch to separate act and tail paths, instead of the design's `lift-act`/`lift-tail`/`lower-act`/`lower-tail` names.\n\n**Design defects for the Architect:**\n- **D1:** a ring-buffer cursor position embeds its stream's identity, which §5.5's renaming doesn't reach. Law 4 failed across lanes on every row with a cursor until the harness renamed those identities itself.\n- **D2:** both de Bruijn kernels re-park a retained FFI request on their own call-out pair, ignoring the carried response cursor. This needs a kernel seam like 6c-pre.\n- **D3:** §4.2's restricted `virt` gives no answer for item 12's \"live tampered upward\" case. I fall back to the register's unique virtual in the body, and refuse when that is ambiguous.\n- **D4:** the register kernel can't hold a primitive function in a parked frame, although the carrier and the reference lane allow one. I refuse it before attach as `:yin.k/unsupported :continuation-registers`, a status and kind I named.\n\nThe full report, with every count, deviation and uncertainty, is at `collab/1792340000000-engineer-6c-layouts.report.md`. Node and Dart weren't run, as briefed; the main portability risk is that the harness's `*lane*` uses `binding` and `satisfies?` on Dart.","ttft_ms":2236,"type":"result","duration_ms":1945337,"uuid":"0abfc1bc-87af-4904-ba53-012328ef225d","ttft_stream_ms":1858,"time_to_request_ms":46,"first_content_frame_ms":1858,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
